A Republican strategy to oppose Obama's re-election
Republican leaders justify their policies with the need to fight voter fraud.
However, the Brennan Center considers the latter to be a myth by reaffirming that U.S. voter fraud is rarer than the number of people killed by lightning.
By the way, in 10 years in the U.S., Republican lawyers have recorded only 300 cases of voter fraud.
One thing is certain: these new provisions will have a negative impact on voter turnout.
In this sense, these measures partly undermine the democratic system of the United States.
In contrast to Canada, the U.S. states are responsible for conducting elections in the individual states.
In this sense, the majority of American governments have promulgated new laws since 2009 that complicate the procedure for registration or ballout.
This phenomenon has gained importance after the November 2010 elections, in which 675 new republican representatives could be recorded in 26 states.
As a result, 180 bills were instituted in 2011 alone, restricting the exercise of the right to vote in 41 states.
The new electoral laws require voters to present a photo ID and proof of U.S. citizenship.
In addition, these laws also shorten periods for early voting, invalidating the right to register as voters on electoral day, and citizens are denied the right to vote, for which a courting file exists.
Ahead of the 2006 elections, no U.S. state has demanded voters to present a photo ID.
Indiana was the first state to make such a claim.
The U.S. Supreme Court upheld the constitutionality of Indiana's law in 2008.
Republican authorities rushed to extend this practice to other states.
Over the past two years, in 34 states, they have promoted bills forcing voters to display a photo ID.
It is important to note that U.S. citizens, unlike Quebec, don't have a universal identification card like the health insurance card.
In fact, 11% of U.S. citizens, i.e. 21 million persons eligible to vote, do not have a photo ID issued by a government agency of their state.
In addition, five million new voters in 2012 do not have such an identity card.
However, more than a hundred dollars are often payable to receive the required ID.
The new restrictions disproportionately affect young people, minorities and low-income people.
In fact, 25% of Afro-Americans, 15% of those with an income of less than $35,000, 18% of citizens over the age of 65 and 20% of voters between the ages of 18 and 29 do not have the required photo ID.
This extends even further.
The students who are regarded as voters, who increasingly cast their votes for democratic candidates, are not allowed in several states to use their photo ID issued by their institution.
On the other hand, the samele states allow members of fishing or hunting clubs, who vote more strongly for the republican side to use the cards issued by these clubs for elections.
Prior to 2004, no state has demanded evidence of citizenship for voting.
Arizona was the first state to introduce such a demand.
Since 2011, a dozen states have passed laws requiring voters to prove their U.S. citizenship.
These measures are clearly aimed at limiting the Hispanic votes.
Because it is obvious that two out of three Hispanic voters favor the democratic party.
In addition, in 2011, Republican lawmakers promoted laws in eight states that abolished the registration of voters on Election Day.
In addition, you have restricted the right of individuals and groups to provide assistance to those voters who wish to register.
These restrictions aren’t without consequences.
For example, the voter registration campaigns in the 2004 general elections have helped to register about 10 million citizens.
Because of the measures adopted since 2009, the share of registration of new electors in 2010 has decreased by 17% compared to 2006.
In addition, Republican lawmakers in five other states have passed legislation aimed at shortening the period for early voting.
For example, in the 2008 Florida general election, 33% of voters who voted in advance were Afro-Americans, even though they make up only 13% of the state's voters.
The same goes for the Hispanic people.
The latter accounted for only 11% of voters, but 24% of citizens who voted in advance.
In contrast, the whites, which represented 76% of voters, accounted for only 46% of voters who voted in advance.
Of course, democratic legislators and their supporters have vigorously opposed the passage of laws that restrict the registration of voters.
Several bills have been blocked by vetos of Democratic governors.
U.S. Advocate General intervened to suspend the most controversial laws.
They were able to partially limit the damage.
For example, only 16 of the 34 states have passed laws that require the presentation of a photo ID.
However, the newly introduced rules in 2012 will undoubtedly complicate the exercise of the right to vote.
Democratic critics denounce the partisan political nature of the passed laws and see them as a clear goal of influencing the results in the states decisive for 2012.
A 2011 report by the Brennan Center shows that those states that have passed these laws make up 171 of the 270 votes required in the Electoral College to win the presidency.
It is too early to confirm with certainty that these changes in legislation on the electoral system will have a significant impact on the outcome of the 2012 presidential election.
But one thing is certain: these new provisions will have a negative impact on voter turnout.
In this sense, these measures partly undermine the democratic system of the United States.
Prostate cancer detection: should the test be taken or not?
The PSA test, in fact, sometimes has faulty results, with false negative or even false positive results that lead to unnecessary medical interventions.
This leads to stronger hesitation of the already undecided men for conducting early detection tests.
Should the test be taken or not?
We're getting the opinion of two specialists.
There were numerous disturbing factors between the control groups in the U.S., so it is difficult to interpret this data and make clear recommendations.
Another study, this time from Europe, came to the conclusion that there is a difference in the mortality of patients with and without early detection.
This study has also shown that with a follow-up observation after 12 years, the probability of metastasis formation is 30 to 40% higher if no early detection has been carried out.
That’s why I recommend the test from the age of 50 years or 40 years if you have a direct relative who has already had prostate cancer.
There is also a higher risk among men of Afro-American origin.
The most important thing is to make the right decision once a cancer has been detected.
There are types of cancer that are aggressive and others that are indolent.
The patient must really understand the extent of the risk of his cancer by showing him the possible options in which prostate cancer can remain untreated if he does not impair the quality of life in the long term, and in these cases one is more likely to opt for active monitoring of the disease.
Today there is no treatment in many men in whom cancer has been detected, because their cancer is not aggressive and poses no threat to their lives.
They are proposed with active monitoring and offered treatment if the disease progresses.
One determines with increasing accuracy the criteria for a decision, who should be treated and who should not.
That’s why I still recommend carrying out the test.
However, the most important thing is to talk to his doctor to determine whether he should be performed or not.
In cooperation with the international society of urology, Movember has developed an instrument with which it is possible to evaluate the advantages and disadvantages of the PSA test.
This document (currently only in English, but a translation will be available soon) can be downloaded at: http://ca.movember.com/fr/mens-health/prostate-cancer-screening
prevention of the disease
Unfortunately, there is no miraculous cure for the prevention of cancer.
Despite advances in research, adopting a healthy lifestyle remains the best way to reduce the risk of developing it.
It is estimated that 30% of cancers could be prevented if everyone eats well and moves sufficiently.
"If there were no more smokers, this value would rise to at least 50%," emphasizes André Beaulieu, spokesman for the Canadian Cancer Society.
However, it is assumed that about 10% of cancers are caused by heredity.
Several also remain completely unexplained.
For the Canadian cancer society, the fight against tobacco addiction remains a priority, despite the decline in the number of smokers.
Smoking is responsible for 85% of lung cancer cases.
It's also a risk factor for several other cancers.
It severely damages people's health.
"There are still 1.5 million smokers in Quebec today," regrets spokesman André Beaulieu.
Encouraging data: 10 years after abandoning the cigarette, the risk of dying from cancer decreases by half.
The weight
According to Canada's cancer society, overweight and obesity also promote the outbreak of the disease.
They appear to increase the risk of breast, wretth, rectum, esophagus, pancreatic and uterine cancers.
"Research shows that regular physical activity throughout life protects against grim intestine cancer," he adds.
The diet
The organisation also recommends limiting the consumption of red meat.
In too large an amount, it increases the risk of grimming or rectum cancer.
Sausages should also be avoided.
Preservation of meat by smoking, drying or salts can lead to the formation of carcinogens.
"The latter can damage the body's cells and lead to the development of cancer," he explains.
Vitamins
In recent years, several scientists have studied the association between vitamin additions and cancer.
However, their investigations are currently inconclusive.
According to the Canadian Cancer Society, studies on vitamin E are contradictory.
While one study found the reduction in the risk of prostate cancer, another was more likely to show an increase.
The effect of vitamin D on cancer has not yet been clearly demonstrated.
In addition, Mr Beaulieu stresses the importance of talking to his doctor about his concerns and his familial burden.
"Carrying out an early detection test does not lead to cancer."
The Higgs boson analyzes
Announcing the likely discovery of the Higgs boson caused some stir last summer, and for good reason.
It is indeed believed that this boson is involved in nothing less than the mechanism responsible for the mass of all objects in the universe.
And finally, it is the last particle whose existence was predicted by the standard model - our best or "least wrong" explanation of nature and the behavior of matter - but has not yet been empirically observed.
But for the physicists, it's not yet entirely certain that it's really Higgs.
We don't know the shadow of any doubt that we've had a real new particle, and that it strongly resembles the Higgs boson predicted by the standard model.
In addition, this appears to be confirmed by new data presented at a major physics congress in Kyoto this week, but data is still lacking to be perfectly secure about it.
Nevertheless, let’s assume that it’s actually Higgs, because there’s seemingly only a slight risk of being mistaken, and let’s look at what it’s about.
There is an inevitable law in this world that causes two objects not to be present in the same place at the same time.
It's not possible to violate this rule - and don't try too hard as you'll hurt yourself.
Because even if particle physics is a really strange world, it turns out that such a law also applies to them: the Paulian exclusion principle, which states that two particles cannot occupy the same space at the same time if they are in the same "quantum state", whereby this "state" by and large consists of some of their properties.
Based on this, the physicists divide the particles into two categories.
In one corner there are the good citizens who are referred to as fermiones and kindly obey the Pauli principle.
And in the other the bosons move, a nasty gang of anarchists to whom nothing is sacred - definitely not this principle, which means that they can indeed be in the same place at the same time.
According to The Particle Adventure from the Berkeley labs (which is absolutely astonishment), these bosons are divided into two groups: the mesons that we will not treat here and the "power particles" through which the great forces of nature spread and with which the Higgs boson would somehow be related.
However, it has to be emphasized here that these bosons are not all such exotic creatures as you might think.
If you're able to read this chronicle, it's only thanks to a boson of extraordinary banality: the photon, or the "light particle," that's the "bot" of electromagnetic force.
If a particle accelerates with an electrical charge or changes the direction, the electromagnetic field is "disturbed" exactly at this location, roughly like a pebble that was throwned into a pond.
This “disorder” creates an electromagnetic wave (light, infrared or ultraviolet radiation, etc.), and this wave is nothing more than a photon and thus one of the boson “carriers of force”.
Stabler field
The same applies to the Higgs boson, with the difference that another field, the Higgs field, must be "disturbed" for this boson to appear.
However, the Higgs field is much more stable than the electromagnetic field; the pathogen has to reach very high energies, a little comparable to a frozen pond, the surface of which could only be smashed by a very large rock.
That's why a giant particle accelerator such as CERN is required - the Large Hadron Collider is a ring with a circumference of 27 km! - to reach such energies.
Analogy with the electromagnetic field is once again helpful in explaining the relationship between Higgs and mass.
In fact, it's not all particles and not all the materials that interact with the electromagnetic field.
Some, such as magnets, do this, but others don't - for example, a piece of paper would never stick to the fridge by themselves.
And equally, not all particles interact with the Higgs field: those who do possess a mass, while the others (like, by the way, the photon) have no mass.
For what can all this research be "good," asks Ms Plamondon?
For science, they are used to verify the standard model (SM), and they also allow physicists to closely examine all deviations between the observations and the predictions of the SM.
Incidentally, there are numerous physicists who eagerly hope that such deviations will be found, as the slightest difference could open a door to a "new physics" and stuff some holes of the model.
It must be said that the latter still has great flaws, as it offers no explanation for gravity (ups!) and dark matter, which forms about 80% of the matter of the universe (again ups!).
However, such deviations have not yet been found at CERN.
Impacts
The implications of this research for the general population are more difficult to predict, but it would be misconception that there would be none.
By the way: in the early 1960s, the pioneers of the laser did not suspect in the Bell laboratories that their works would trigger such a revolution.
They saw scientific applications, but nothing else.
In fact, the late physicist Willard Boyle - who worked in the Bell Labs where the laser was invented in the 1960s, and who himself developed the first period laser (the first ones were pulsed) in 1962 - has already told us that in the beginning the laser was considered a "laboratory."
Introduce Yourself...
In addition, applications can also result from all the instruments that surround research.
For example, Willard Boyle developed a small light sensor in 1969 during his work in optics.
Although it was not the original purpose, this sensor now serves as an "eye" for all digital cameras worldwide and has earned it the Nobel Prize for physics in 2009.
Of course, this doesn’t mean that LHC’s activities will inevitably change our lives, but it means that you really can’ve never known...
Palliative care - the best way to die... | Le Devoir
Quebec recently discussed the sensitive issue of the end of life with its Committee on "Dying in Dignity."
The debate must be resumed shortly, although a bill will be prepared.
However, there is still a lot of work to be done in this important subject area.
Le Devoir tried to take a closer look.
A few weeks ago, Mr. L. lived alone in his apartment in Montérégie.
The prostate cancer that consumed him had given him a period of two years.
"They gave me five years to live, I managed seven years," he said half-heartedly, lying in his bed at the home for palliative care Victor-Gadbois in Beloeil where he had arrived the day before.
"However, it's always a shock, you can't be prepared for that," he says.
The illness does its job: an immeasurable weakness prevents him from going to the toilet alone and even eating himself.
Sitting at the table in front of a delicious lunch, he resignedly agrees to be helped with the food.
Courageous he even manages to smile and talk to the strangers who are busy around him, bring him his medication, and offer him a bath.
Courage of Ordinary Death.
"My greatest wish is to heal my diarrhea as this is humiliating," he shares.
Several hours later, the team has found a remedy for this evil.
"In the course of our lives, we learn that a man pees while standing," says Pierre Brodeur, a psychologist at home Victor-Gadbois.
Returning to the childhood period is an unacceptable humiliation for certain persons.
"That depends on the ability of the person," he notes a regression, he notes.
Because according to several people who work in palliative care, great events take place in the midst of these regressions.
Patients admitted to Victor-Gadboi's palliative care home are all suffering from cancer.
They have a maximum life expectancy of three months.
At this stage, they are no longer showered with so-called "healing" care by the doctors and nurses who take care of them.
For Ms. A., 89 years old, is the worst fear of dying "consciously and through suffocation."
But due to the illness I really got to know my children.
"I've got good kids," she adds.
"I don't have any more desires in life," she says, before accepting that you put a mask on her that helps her breathe.
In the next few days, she is still expecting a final visit from her son, who comes from Italy.
In the Victor-Gadbois home, a group of volunteers offer personal care and help with food intake.
Palliative care is used when there are no other options.
In order to make dying more pleasant.
In Quebec, there is a bed for palliative care for 11,700 inhabitants.
That's very little, considering that one day we're all going to die.
Here, life will continue in the best possible conditions, explains Dr. Christiane Martel, one of the doctors of the home.
Both at the level of physical, emotional or spiritual comfort.
Depending on their wishes, the dying person is granted oral care with brandy or Pepsi.
Diabetics no longer need to control their blood sugar levels.
And death is part of everyday life.
Last night we served a beer that died over the course of the night.
This morning, his son will drink the beer at the deceased's feet.
"We help relatives and patients alike," nursing director Nathalie Savard reports.
In home Victor-Gadbois is not a day like the other.
In addition to an old man of 93 who enjoys the last family gathering in the courtyard and is well supported by his pillows while bumping into his honor, a young man of 36 who is surrounded by his parents, his wife and his two young children tragically dies after attempting everything to survive.
"For six months, three to five beds have always been occupied by cancer patients aged less than 45 years," says Dr. Christiane Martel worriedly.
53% of patients admitted to Victor-Gadbois come from their place of residence, 47% come from the hospital.
Palliative care problem
It is said that 77% of Canadians do not have access to palliative care, i.e. to a care that is supposed to alleviate the suffering of patients when the latter have reached the end stage of their lives, both at home, in the hospital or in a nursing home.
And several organizations, such as the Victor-Gadbois home and the Society for Domestic Palliative Care in the Greater Montreal area, specialize more or less exclusively in the care of cancer patients.
It is precisely this great inadequacy in Quebec’s health care that several physicians specialized in palliative care look forward to the passage of a law to regulate euthanasia and assisted suicide with concerns.
Since the month of October, a manifesto has been circulated signed by prominent palliative care figures such as Dr Balfour Mount and Dr Bernard Lapointe to express their opposition to such an initiative.
According to Dr Christiane Martel, Quebec’s health system is not powerful enough to ensure that everyone has the right to high-quality palliative care before agreeing to transition to euthanasia.
Recently, she says, I've seened a patient who has been in the emergency room for 14 days of great pain without taking any action on her relief.
I'm afraid patients want to die only because they don't get adequate health care.
And at the same time, several oncologists treat their patients until the last day, despite the worst prognosis.
Hélène Richard's chances of survival were already minimal when she aborted arduous chemotherapy.
When I told my oncologist that I was going to stop treatment, she replied to me that she was sorry that I'd give up the fight, she said.
However, she told me that I was finished!
No omnipotent care
Dr Martel believes that 90% of patients who have asked to die thank the nursing staff for failing to accept this desire after their pain was relieved by a palliative care team.
However, it must be clearly stated that palliative care is not omnipotent during pain management.
According to Elsie Monereau, head of palliative care at the Society of Domestic Palliative Care in Greater Montreal, 8% of patients do not respond to pain treatment.
At the very end of life, doctors often resort to terminal sedation, which amounts to euthanizing the patient either sporadically or permanently until his death.
We can no longer pretend that we don't perceive this side of suffering.
Patients without pain relief will increasingly have the opportunity to resort to this terminal sedation.
Patients whose pain is not relieved, all say the same sentence: "I want to die."
But that doesn't necessarily mean, "I want you to euthanize me," but "I want my pain to be relieved."
The realization of this report was possible due to a journalism scholarship from Canadian health research institutes.
Real estate scandals of large scale in Quebec
Officials from the road construction office, companies of the main construction industry, money procurers of political parties and specialists from the Italian mafia tell every day what they know about a great "system" in which construction industry, officials, politicians, trade unionists and mafioso are entangled.
An "industry" that was very expensive to Quebec's taxpayer, especially in the 1990s and 2000s.
"It's curious how the system's disintegrating since drastic action was taken," jokes Jacques Duchesneau, mP in Quebec and former head of Montreal police.
The scandal was uncovered by him as a result of an in-depth investigation into the embezzlement related to the contracts for road construction work in Quebec, to which the then prime minister had reluctantly agreed.
The "Duchesneau report" established a direct link between industry, secret financing of parties and bribery of officials.
"Since the beginning of the investigation in 2010," he insists, "the Ministry of Transport alone would have saved a billion dollars on the contracts," because some curbed their instinct in order to preserve their share!
The Charbonneau Commission "has already brought down two mayors," he adds, in the hope that it will succeed in "pointing out the tactics behind the individuals."
A permanent anti-corruption unit launched in 2011
Launched in 2011, the permanent anti-corruption unit is also making great efforts with its troops of auditors, government investigators and analysts.
In addition, the policemen of the "Marteau Force", which since 2009 has ensured that the "sewerage cartel" of Montreal proceeds in the artificial inflation of the treaties more restrained...
In recent weeks, she has carried out rows of house searches and made accusations of fraud and corruption against municipal officials such as Frank Tampino and Richard Marcotte, who is the mayor of a suburb.
The next one on the list would be Gilles Vaillancourt, who has just resigned from office as mayor of Laval, Quebec's third-largest city.
He is suspected of repeatedly accepting bribes in exchange for public contracts.
By the way, engineers from the Montreal road construction office, as well as entrepreneurs of Italian origin, such as Tony Accurso and Lino Zambito, were explicitly indicted.
The latter has caused a stir by declaring to the Commission the functioning of the "system" for the receipt of public contracts.
For several years, he himself paided 3% of the value of contracts obtained in Montreal to an intermediary linked to the mafia who transferred the money to Union Montreal, Mayor Gérald Tremblay's party.
Mr Zambito had his hand in play everywhere in the 2000s by giving more than 88,000 Canadian dollars (about 68,000 euros) to provincial parties, especially the liberals who were in power at the time.
He also confessed that an illegal fundraising for former liberal vice-president Nathalie Normandeau.
Sewage contracts, the costs of which were artificially bloated by him
In Montreal, the "system" of corruption worked smoothly.
Former civil engineer Gilles Surprenant has described this in detail before the commission: in ten years, he received gifts from the construction company, invitations to travel, golf tournaments, restaurants and hockey games, as well as bribes amounting to 736,000 dollars in return for sewerage contracts, the cost of which he artificially inflated.
Other officers from the road construction office have admitted that they allowed themselves to be lubricated by artificially inflating the bills with false additional benefits by 30-40%.
In addition, an organizer of the mayor's party, Martin Dumont, has blamed Mr. Tremblay that he deliberately overlooked a shadow household that filled his coffers with black money.
As a result of these revelations, Mr Tremblay resigned in early November, which plunged Montreal into a serious crisis.
Chantal Rouleau was one of Montreal's first deputies to sound the alarm.
As mayor of Rivière-des-Prairies district, in the east of the island, she opposed the sale of a municipal property in 2010 that was acquired for 5 million dollars and resold for 1.6 million to developers in the booming real estate market.
70% black money in the election campaigns
From the investigation, which is ultimately carried out, she says that she is "dragging on a thread that makes it possible to understand the functioning of the ant-immigrated system to stop the evil and denounce the culprits."
She says the procedure is "painful but positive."
We are in the process of cleaning up the wound, but we need an investigative unit intended only for Montreal, as well as oversight, in order to prevent a return of questionable practices.
How to cleaver.
Regularly.
Jacques Duchesneau, for his part, notes that "officials have stolen several hundred million dollars," but above all worries about the role of "mandate-bearers who are aware of the tactics" if they were not involved in the fraud!
Based on a share of 70% black money in the financing of the election campaigns in Quebec, he jokes: "I was told that this was only a faint clapp of reality."
Quebec's government is proposing to limit donations to parties to 100 dollars, which, according to him, wouldn't change the situation: "As long as campaign spending isn't severely limited, black money will be circulating in politics."
He recommends a complete overhauling of the system for awarding public contracts and financing the parties: "We can't fall deeper; by getting to the bottom of things with courage, we can re-establish the house on a more solid basis with more checks and laws."
If this history harms the international reputation of Quebec and that of Montreal, Mr Duchesneau urges those who are laughing to take a look into their own backyard...
"PSG isn't FC Barcelona."
This season you've taken on a new scale with PSG.
How do you explain this progress?
One can explain it through a personal insight, but also through the new dimension of PSG.
Great players have come.
I make progress every day by her side.
The technical staff also gave me a lot.
These elements encourage me to improve my playing level every day.
And with a game, this is even easier.
In football, everything goes very quickly.
But I don't burst into enthusiasm.
I have always made gradual progress since my beginnings at the heart of the preparatory training of INF Clairefontaine until my playing time at Saint-Etienne.
They thus take advantage of the competition introduced by Carlo Ancelotti...
This summer's new additions are used to hosting games of very high levels.
They also know that every training session is decisive.
This means that a player like me has to stand up and want to do his best.
By the way Carlo Ancelotti gives me a lot in terms of my playing position.
He is backed by co-trainers such as Claude Makele, who played in the same position as me.
Is Ancelotti the right person for the task?
Of course.
Ancelotti instills respect for all technicians.
There is currently no comparable person in Ligue 1, and he is one of Europe's best coaches.
He looks back on a rich experience and has won many titles with respected clubs.
He's been in contact with big players.
I think he'll win more titles in Paris.
I had an encouraging conversation with him in January.
At that time, I had just recovered from an injury streak.
The trust he has placed in me is also an explanation for my achievements.
What do you think about the first part of the PSG season?
In Ligue 1, Lyon took us the place of the front-runner.
We're lurking in ambush.
One of our most important goals is the Champions League: we have qualified for the eighth final in this way.
What is the club's goal in this competition?
We'll try to get as far as possible.
From now everything is possible.
However, we will have a say against very good European teams.
First of all, we want to be first of our group before Porto, so that we can play the return match of the eighth final as a home game.
Can the PSG become a big European club in the short term?
He's already got the budget for it...
In order to become a big European club, Paris has to win titles and make a name for themselves in the long term.
This is not the case today.
Financially, the PSG gives itself the funds so that this project takes concrete forms.
Would it be a major failure not to win the Ligue 1, as was the case last season?
Of course, this would be a huge disappointment.
This year, it's really important to us to see this title.
Last season we were close.
In May we were disappointed as we were strong enough to win.
We finished the season with a very strong performance.
We finished with 79 points.
Normally you should be a champion with 79 points...
But another squad, those of Montpellier, has scored an even better season.
I believe that this year is the right one.
Even if the big teams like Marseille, Lyon or Bordeaux are wrestling for the title, I believe we have what it takes to win.
Don't you think the mediary expects too much from PSG?
Considering what's been invested and the players we've haved, it's normal that a lot is expected from us.
We accept that completely.
However, if we win in a home game against Troyes 4-0, and you can still find something that you can accuse us, that's certainly a bit frustrating.
One wonders what people still expect.
We could never win every weekend with 4-0.
We'ren't FC Barcelona!
We're trying to create a game concept.
A team builds up over time.
In the Champions League, we've shown strength.
Look at Manchester City, a club that hasn't managed to qualify for the eighth final for two seasons, and who've also made huge expenses!
In view of the sums invested, you were believed at the break with 15 points ahead!
That would mean that our opponents and the French championship would be disregarded.
Lyon and Marseille, who were not good last season, were "boosted" by this new PSG.
This shows Ligue 1 is exciting.
I hope that in the month of May we will be the smiling victorious and tell ourselves that despite all the difficulties, we have finally won them.
The PSG appear to be fully dependent on Zlatan Ibrahimovic's performances.
All the better that one speaks of a "Zlatan dependency."
This means that Ibrahimovic is powerful and scores many goals.
That's why he's come and he's proving that he's Ligue 1.
Wherever he has played, he has shown that he is a great player and a world star.
Within the squad, we respect both the player and the person.
He also respects those who surround him.
What he's done is really extraordinary.
This spurs the others to improve their level of play.
Thiago Silva, who is one of the best defenders in the world, also allows everyone else to make progress.
How did you experience the 2012 European Championship with the French national team?
It's like a disappointment.
It was important for me to participate in this European Championship.
Unfortunately, my injury prevented me from knocking out some playing time.
I've seen things there from which I've emerged stronger.
Today I manage to achieve good games in the national team.
This has been my hope since my baptism with the "Bleus."
From what has happened in Ukraine, we have learned out, and today we have to demonstrate exemplary behavior.
What opinion do you have about the first months of Didier Deschamps at the head of "Bleus"?
He's achieved promising results.
We've got a good position in the qualifying group for the World Cup.
The national coach is strict, is close to the players and encourages victory.
As it was Laurent Blanc.
But I don't want to make a comparison.
Blanc had achieved his goal by qualifying us for the European Championships.
I hope Didier Deschamps will lead the "Bleus" to Brazil.
Is the draw forced on 16 October in Spain (1:1) a kick-off match?
This game has given us confidence.
Everyone has stood up for everyone else.
Ahead of this meeting in Spain, I have never experienced a comparable game in my career.
Paying and selling is possible with Bitcoin without a bank
The opposite of the current monetary exchange based on central banks, identified transactions and processing fees between stakeholders.
In addition, as is so often the case in these technologies, a political vision is palpable: the conviction that the current monetary system designed by banking monopolies leads to financial crises.
In fact, Bitcoin invented by Satoshi Nakamoto (a pseudonym) is both a virtual currency (but convertible into dollars, euros, etc.) as well as a protocol for secure exchange such as BitTorrent, which allows the direct exchange of files.
With 15,000 computers on the network, about 200,000 transactions have already been recorded.
A few thousand websites accept Bitcoin as a donation or as a means of payment.
After Bitcoin’s price reached a peak of 30 dollars (23 euros) in June 2011, it fell to 2 dollars five months later before recovering to around ten dollars today (prices are listed on bitcoincharts.com).
There is nothing particularly impressive compared to the world trade of real currencies or financial products.
Nevertheless, the European Central Bank (EZB) has shown interest in this in a report on virtual currencies published in October.
She describes Bitcoin as "the virtual currency with the greatest success," "in competition with the dollar or euro" and "similar to traditional currencies."
Bitcoin differs from other types of virtual currencies, such as “credits,” which are used to move forward in a video game that you can win (and sometimes exchange back) through gambling.
The social network Facebook has also developed such a system.
However, the trade is controlled and processed each time by a central authority.
With Bitcoin, all network nodes are simultaneously depositors of the invoice book, auditors, money issuers as well as buyers and sellers.
How does this network work?
Every transaction between two users actually takes place between two e-mail addresses in the form of an e-mail.
With the difference that the user can choose a different address for each payment, which ensures anonymity.
A number of the data related to this transaction is electronically signed by a double encryption system.
In this way, the network can verify the authenticity of the transaction.
With the help of the contents of the file, it is also possible to ensure that the traded Bitcoins are present in the accounting book of public accounts distributed throughout the network.
The decisive step is to enter this new transaction into this book.
This is done via a mathematical task that is started on the computers, with the winner, who is a kind of provisional central banker, having the privilege of adding that extra line.
It is a disassemblement of the file, i.e. the conversion of a large file into a shorter and unique electronic fingerprint.
The computers "take" the new transaction and add a number to it before the whole thing gets "chopped up".
The aim is to find the number that results in a special fingerprint (many zeros at the beginning).
If this number has been found, the other nodes can effortlessly check if it is the right one.
The transaction is thus permanently linked to the chain of all other transactions, and any modification would change the fingerprint.
If a user wants to pay very quickly (less than 10 minutes) with the same money to cheat, only one of the two transactions would be validated by the network and the other remains without linking, as both have different fingerprints.
The computer that solved the task receives 50 bitcoins.
To prevent inflation, this reward is regularly halved, probably before the end of 2012.
Bitcoins in circulation are thus limited to 21 million, but divisible except for one hundred millionth, which leaves some leeway...
In addition, the difficulty of the task is increased with each improvement in computing power.
The life of the network has had highs and lows.
The websites that provide services for Bitcoin have been attacked and the Bitcoin deposits have been stolen.
"The vulnerability used does not concern the actual protocol," assures Pierre Noizat, who has just introduced Paymium, which is a company for payment with real money and uses the Bitcoin network.
The ECB also reports on the possibilities of money laundering with the help of this anonymous service.
However, cash also has these shortcomings.
Important actors such as Wikipedia deny donations of this kind.
They are accepted by others such as the blogging platform WordPress.
Recently Adi Shamir and Dorit Ron from the Weizmann Institute in Israel analyzed the accounting book and showed that almost 80% of Bitcoins are not in circulation.
In November, a "mega-final sale" was launched.
"Thirty thousand dollars have been exchanged," said Jon Holmquist, who works for Coinabul, which converts bitcoins into gold.
Pierre Noizat, who is also the author of a book about this currency, firmly believes in the potential of this technology as a transaction network.
His Paytunia system corresponds to a credit card (with real money) or a contactless payment with a mobile phone, but uses Bitcoin to validate the transactions, which are cheaper.
In addition, the owner manages his identity and can therefore remain anonymous.
The system can be easily implemented at merchants, and the latter does not need to install new terminals or computer programs.
You simply have to specify an address that can be "photographed and recognized" by a phone, states Pierre Noizat, who assures that he has thousands of users.
There is a general trend in which hierarchical systems are reconsidered in favour of more horizontal systems.
“It’s going to take some time for Bitcoin to prevail, but 2013 could be a turning point,” he predicts.
Incidentally, the ECB’s report envisages re-evaluating the various risks, now regarded as highly regarded, in the event of a success of this currency.
We're withdrawn from Afghanistan.
And after that?
French troops have left their area of responsibility in Afghanistan (Kapisa and Surobi).
NATO and U.S. are expected to follow at the end of 2014.
It's time for the Afghan army to take possession of its territory again, and that the Afghan people decide their future without expecting anything from us.
In particular, we have punished the Afghan peasants by seeing them as terrorists.
And ourselves with our 88 soldiers killed, as well as the injured and wounded.
The Taliban consists of foreign extremists, former leaders who have fled to Pakistan, but often also from peasants who deny an armed foreign presence, as they did at the time of the Soviets.
They want to defend their centuries-old and archaic traditions, even when jihadists, Pakistanis, Arabs, Uzbeks and Tajiks have joined them.
Tolerated, sometimes backed by the local insurgents, the latter will no longer be there when Western countries become more discreet.
The withdrawal of French troops from the base in Nijrab, which I witnessed from a hill planted with almond trees financed with the help of French loans, took place properly.
The truck and tank convoys overwhelmed by helicopters have reached Kabul without being attacked.
From the end of 2014, there will be no more influence of the Taliban in Kabul.
Since their irresistible advance from 1994 to 1996, conditions have changed.
At that time Kabul was empty and the country overwhelmed by war due to the struggles of the various seditious groups.
Their seizure of power at that time was seen as a kind of liberation and a return to security.
Afghan women have paided the price for the obscurantism of farmers overwhelmed by al-Qaeda organization, but their situation has not improved to this day.
Former mujaheden, the Afghan government and current Taliban have a common desire to keep the women in a subordinate position.
The most important personalities of the anti-Soviet war regained power in 201.
They've turned into business-makers by seizing land belonging to the government to sell as building land to refugees from Iran and Pakistan, taking advantage ofrom the massive American award of subcontracts.
They've put themselves in disrepute, and most of them haven't been involved in the fighting themselves.
As I've heard in the campaigns, people want a government that doesn't consist of thieves.
Many young people want to leave the country, just as those who were able to take advantage of the American donations: capital flight is considerable.
Young people are tired of war and its ideologies.
During their exiles in Iran or Pakistan, they have had contact with the modern world and have enjoyed their benefits.
Approximately 65% of the population is under the age of 25; Kabul now has 5 million inhabitants, which is a fifth of the total population.
In cities, state schools are full of girls as well as boys.
These young people, who do not want to return to the obscurantism of the former parties or the corruption of certain executives, will have to be provided with jobs.
Everyone, including the armed parties, is obsessed with mobile phones; television with its Turkish series representing a modern world is watched everywhere.
An army is available now.
Are the authorities under whose authority she will be considered legitimate?
Former commanders of the anti-Soviet struggle are already striving to restore the militias in the province, which will evade the power of the central government.
Afghanistan, the land of mountains with strong local identities, should have benefited from a certain decentralization using the example of Western nations, but by aboliting the office of prime minister, which has existed since the constitution of 1964, the U.S. wanted to make it a centralized state with a strong presidential force.
President Karzaï does not want foreign checks, especially on the occasion of the elections planned for April 2014.
However, his country has been dependent on foreign aid since the 1950s and long before.
Industries have not been reconstructed, functional dams are not available and important irrigation systems have not been repaired.
Everything is imported; apart from fruits and vegetables, nothing is produced.
Priority is given to the private initiative.
In a country destroyed by 30 years of war, state control of the infrastructures would have been required.
The rumour has spread that Afghanistan has immense mineral resources.
This further reinforces the impression that Western countries were only on the ground in order to seize it.
No mining is possible without energy in order to process the iron or copper ore on site, or without means of transport for an export over the mountains.
The Chinese have already almost leaved Mes Aynak's copper mine, letting international archaeologists (funded by the World Bank) do their excavations on the vast Buddhist site and remain the province's major employers.
One day it will also be necessary for Afghanistan and Pakistan, on which imports and exports are largely dependent, to establish normal relations again.
The withdrawal of French combat troops was completed on November 20.
The new cooperation agreement envisages the continuation of traditional aids: girls' and boys' grammar schools, Institute of French at the Universities, French Institute, cooperation in the military, legal, medical and agricultural spheres, support of the Archaeological Delegation.
Since 20909, a "civilital-military action" service of the Ministry of Defense (Cimic), which was discontinued in 2012, has carried out numerous charitable work and measures for the rehabilitation of agriculture in dozens of mountain villages and had it carried out by a small French non-governmental organization to try to "gain hearts and spirits" and achieve the impossible task of reconciling aid and offensive actions.
This work, which has required numerous local workers, has helped to suppress insurrection: irrigation, wells, drinking water, reforestation, fruit trees, soil protection and increasing agricultural land.
What souvenir do we leave behind after military expenditures of two billion euros?
A much more modest budget will contribute to the improvement of local living conditions, which are often very hard at these valleys at an altitude of more than 2000 metres.
Dozens of written requests from small-scale agricultural projects by municipalities of the province of Kapisa have been received at the embassy.
In order for farmers to free themselves from the insurgency led by foreign groups, which according to their own statements is their desire, the continuation would be lower, well-checked and directly relating to civilian aid in their favour.
A constitution with the crowbar in Egypt
It is a new poker game by President Mohammed Morsi.
While Egypt remains more divided than ever in relation to the constitutional declaration, which temporarily grants him the unrestricted authority, he has decided to put everything on a map.
To everyone's surprise, he announced on Wednesday that the constituent assembly would adopt its definitive version the next day.
Just a week ago, the head-of-state had granted this plenary two additional months for the completion of his work.
Egypt has been relying on a tentative, multi-change text for almost two years now, weakening institutional stability and leading to legal confusion.
This new initiative has only provided for an even greater division of the country.
For his opponents, the president persists in his "autocratic delirium" by continuing to "break his word" and "trampling the right."
On the part of its proponents, one assures that it is a way to end the institutional and political crisis as soon as possible and accelerate the transition process.
A referendum should be held within 14 days.
A very short period of time, forcing the brothers to renounce their intention to explain to the Egyptians the text section by section.
For the president, it's also a manner to find his public and democratic legitimacy again as the protest rages across the country.
After reaffirmations in an interview with the American newsreak Time, Mohammed Morsi seems to be convinced that the Egyptians will vote benevolently.
Especially since the Egyptian people are given an ultimatum with this resort to a hasty vote: "Either you vote in favour of my draft or I retain the unrestricted authority," the latter was supposed to come to an end after adopting the constitution.
85 mostly Islamist members of this constituent assembly passed the text yesterday in a strange atmosphere.
The majority of liberals have not complied with the request.
Shortly before the constitutional declaration in mid-November, they turned away because they felt that they had not been able to assert their views.
Representatives of human rights organizations, religious minorities or civil society have acted equally.
In order to achieve the quorument, 11 people, deputy members, were added in a hurry yesterday morning.
Some are very close to the Muslim Brotherhood.
It is not surprising that the articles were mostly adopted unanimously.
The commentators, by the way, found it entertaining that the only disagreement of the day... was expressed at the time for prayer, since some members of the panel were of the opinion that the pendulum clock of the constitutive assembly did not go exactly.
The text, which was adopted last night, consists of 234 articles.
Article 2, which attracted everyone's attention, finally remains identical to that of the 1971 Constitution and states that "the principles of Sharia law are the main source of justice."
The Salafist parties, for whom the establishment of Islamic law is an important demand, had the hope of replacing "the principles" with "the rules," which would have made stricter application possible.
For the Islamists, the fact that this article has not been changed is a testament to their goodwill and for their respect for the other members of Egyptian society.
"Shydness" is echoed by the liberlians, who see this only as a propaganda strike.
According to them, the Islamization of the constitution takes place via other articles.
In doing so, they pay particular attention to Article 220, which assigns an advisory function to Al-Azhar University, in particular with regard to the verification of conformity with Sharia laws.
According to Egypt specialist Sophie Pommier, this is disturbing because "the individuals who are asked to comment have not been elected and have no democratic legitimacy whatsoever.
You can see the first signs of theocracy.”
Liberal fears are also nurtured by the fact that this university's next rector is likely to be far less moderate than the current one.
"Currently there is no concrete religious commitment.
With this constitution, we remain within the framework of the bourgeois state.
Most lawyers who have worked on this text are not ulema, but academics who have been partly trained in the French system," appeases Alexis Blouet, who writes a doctoral thesis on Egyptian constitutional transition.
He admits, however, "that there may be ambiguity in relation to Article 220, since the terms used are borrowed from religious vocabulary.
It refers here in particular to "Fiqh" [Islamian case law].
And in the future, the question could be raised to what extent civil judges are competent to decide."
In addition to its religious aspect, the text adopted yesterday is heavily criticised because of the far-reaching powers it grants to the president of the republic.
The Muslim Brotherhood argues that these are significantly reduced compared to the former regime.
Another point of contention: the army's powers.
In accordance with the desire of the military, the examination of the defence budget will not be submitted to Parliament, but to a national defence council.
Similarly, there will be no ban on civil proceedings in military courts, as have been demanded by associations in defence of human rights.
The latter also share their apprehensions regarding the text, which they see as threatening to freedom.
The facts of blasphemy are maintained, and insults are now prohibited, which could have serious consequences for the right to freedom of expression, particularly for the press.
In addition, no more refers to the protection of women, emphasizes Heba Morayef of Human Rights Watch.
In their opinion, the only positive point is the prohibition of torture by Article 36.
This word was not included in the previous constitution.
While the Egyptian president had to speak on television last night, demonstrations are planned for this afternoon.
Proponents of the head-of-state will march Saturday.
In Israel, Ukrainian tourists expect sacred sites, the navel of the world and a sea of brine
The Praised Land unites within itself the glory of biblical truths, modern comfort and an untouched nature.
AIF gives you the five most important reasons to visit Israel.
Bow in front of the sacred sites
Visit the Jordan River where Jesus was baptized.
It is said that all those bathing in this river receive God’s blessing.
Galilee is the place where Jesus accomplished his miracles: the transformation of water into wine at a wedding celebration, walking on the water, satisfying the sea storm and fishing.
This is where Jesus appeared to his disciples after the resurrection.
Most sanctuaries are found in Jerusalem.
Believers celebrate the Way of Suffering or the Way of the Cross of Jesus Christ (in Latin: Via Dolorosa).
The path begins at the Roman fortress Antonia, Pretorium, where the judgment was, and leads through the streets of the old town to the Church on Mount Golgotha, where there is the crucifixion site, the anointing stone and the tomb of Jesus.
According to the Christian conception, here is the navel of the world that symbolizes the salvation of humanity.
The Monastery of the Holy Cross in Jerusalem stands in the place where, according to Christian legend, the tree grew, from which the cross was made for the crucifixion of Jesus.
In Jerusalem is also the holiest place of the Jews - the Western Wall, the remnants of a temple that was destroyed by the Romans in 70 A.D.
According to tradition, people of different faiths leave notes with wishes, which are then fulfilled.
Taking a trip soar
The ruins of Massada Fortress form the remains of the secret hideouse from enemies built by Herod the Great for his family in 25 A.D.
They are located on a rock face in the mountains at 450 meters above sea level.
Only mountaineers can reach the ruins on their own.
Everyone else brings a cable car to this historic summit.
In the north of the country at an altitude of 1600 to 2040 meters there is the famous Hermon ski area, where it attracts countless tourists in the winter months.
From the foot of the mountain you can get there by shuttle bus.
The ski slopes have a total length of 45 kilometers.
According to an old legend, pagan gods supposedly lived on the mountain top.
Visit unique museums
There are almost 300 museums in this country.
With just one trip, not all of them can be visited.
But at least the five most interesting of them should be visited.
Among them is the Israel Museum not far from the Knesset (parliament).
There, old Qumran manuscripts or Scrolls of the Dead Sea found in rock caves in the Judean Desert are kept, as well as about 500 thousand archaeological and anthropological exhibits.
The Museum of Art in Tel Aviv is also worth a visit.
There, the collection of works by Impressionists and Expressionists Monet, Pissaro, Renoir, Cézanne, Sisley, Matisse, Modigliani, Chagall and Picasso will be exhibited.
In Akko you can visit the museum in Bad Al Basha, which houses several halls of old Turkish baths with sculptures of the visitors and bathers at that time.
In Caesarea you should visit the uniquite private Ralli Museum, where you can admire sculptures by Dali and Rodin.
There aren't museum guides or souvenir shops there.
Admission is free, and donations are generally rejected.
The fifth museum, namely the Holocaust Museum or Yad Vashem in Tel Aviv, tells of one of the most staggering chapters in history.
The most poignant division is the memorial to the children, built to commemorate the 1.5 million children destroyed in the concentration camps and gas chambers.
Upon entering the room there is complete darkness.
Stars sparkling.
And the names of the Jewish children and of the countries in which they died are heard.
Ukraine is also mentioned there.
Doing something for health
Israel's top three recreation areas are located on the shores of the Mediterranean, the Red Sea and the Dead Sea.
In each of the three places there are swimming pools, water parks, dolphinariums and ocean arias.
Surprisingly, in the Red Sea is also bathed in the winter months, since the water temperature there does not drop below 21 °C and the air heats up to 23 °C.
The Dead Sea is even warmer, where it is bathed all year round.
By the way, this unusual sea with a location of 417 meters below sea level is the deepest sea in the world.
Its turquoise-blue water has a high salinity and therefore carries even non-swimmers without problems on the surface.
The surrounding landscape is indescribably beautiful.
Visitors come here for treatments using salt water (natural salt brines) and healing sludge, as well as for the treatment of skin inflammation, allergies, asthma, eczema, arthritis, bronchitis, sugar disease and to soothe strained nerves.
Touching the secrets of antiquity
These mysteries arouse the old town of Tel Aviv, namely the city of Jaffa on the Mediterranean coast.
Through this city ran the famous sea route that connected Egypt, Syria, Anatolia and Mesopotamia.
The city is mentioned in ancient Greek and ancient Egyptian legends.
According to tradition, Noah built his ark just here, and Perseus saved the beautiful Andromeda here, with which he led a long and happy life in this place.
Tourists like to stroll through the narrow streets named after zodiac signs.
Fortuna is said to be, according to legend, any hold that touches the walls in the alley with its zodiac sign.
In Jaffa you can meet newlyweds from all over Israel and even other countries during photo appointments.
In Caesarea, the city of King Herod, you can roam through the Roman theatre and conquer the fortress of crusaders.
In the Roman period, Caesarea was the most important town of Judeas and the residence of the Roman governors, including Pontius Pilate.
In the carefully restored theatre, evening concerts and operas are performed nowadays.
watchlist for tourists
If you're going to Israel, don't worry about your poor English proficiency: About 30% of Israelis speak Russian.
The money in the travel is rather not taken in euros, but in dollars, which can simply be changed to shekels (current exchange rate: 1 dollar to 3.8 shekels).
In the city you usually make progress by bus, but in Jerusalem there is also an expressway and in Haifa the only subway line in the country with six stations, which connects the lower city with the upper city.
Strictly speaking, it's an underground funicular.
A trip within the city, regardless of the means of transport, costs 6 shekels, which is enoughed including changing trains for a trip of up to 1.5 hours
According to Jewish tradition the Sabbath is observed in Israel on Saturdays.
Markets and shops are closed from Friday night until sunset on Saturday, and public transport rests.
And on Sunday morning the working week begins.
In many cafes, restaurants and restaurants only kosher food is served, excluding pork, marine products, fish without dandruff and food containing both dairy and meat products.
Instead, you have the choice between different dishes with mutton and beef, soups and desserts (prepared with coconut milk), traditional Jewish humus paste, various sauces, fried balls of pureed beans and peas (falafel) as well as fruits and vegetables.
There are no stray dogs in the streets of Israeli cities.
There are many well-nourished cats streaking through the streets comfortably and undisturbed.
In the evening you can even see them sleeping on the roofs of parked cars.
Cats like busy places and don't say no to treats.
A rental car costs between 37 dollars per day (Hyundai Getz) to 188 dollars (Audi A6, Volvo S80), depending on the brand.
The insurance costs an additional 15 dollars per day.
And bike rental costs 15 shekels a day.
Tickets for museums cost an average of 30 shekels.
In numbers
In 2012, more than three million tourists from all over the world visited Israel.
Most adventurers and recreation seekers came from Russia, France, Germany, Italy, England, the USA and Ukraine.
From January to October 2012, 118.8 thousand Ukrainian tourists came to the Promised Country, which corresponds to an increase of 51% compared to the comparable period in 2010, prior to lifting the visa requirement on 9 February 2011.
'The big and powerful' leads to Moscow: Migrants save money for language
While MPs and civil rights activists are discussing what benefits the law brings on binding language tests for migrants, criminals are already dealing with fake allowances
Every year, 13 million guest workers come to Moscow, St. Petersburg and other cities.
Most of them are the citizens of Central Asia: Uzbekistan, Tajikistan and Turkmenistan.
Their only goal is to earn money for the upkeep of the families remaining at home.
On 1 December, a law came into force requiring every migrant worker to take a test certifying knowledge of the Russian language.
Until now, this regulation only applies to migrants who want to work in the service sector, the municipal housing industry and retail.
However, according to the federal migration authority, the tests should become mandatory for all guest workers over time.
You have to take not only language tests, but also tests into Russian history and the basics of the legislation.
Language proficiencies must be proven both for the receipt and the extension of the work permit.
Exceptions only apply to citizens of countries in which Russian is the official language.
Also excluded from this law are persons who have received their training certificates and diplomas prior to the disintegration of the USSR in 1991.
Sense, problem and protection of rights
The totality of examination bodies will be under the aegis of the Pushkin Institute for the Russian Language, the Russian University of International Friendship, the Moscow State University, the St. Petersburg State University and other Russian universities.
Migrants can take the tests in all cities; more than 160 corresponding centers have already been opened in Russia.
The initiative to introduce the tests was supported by deputies from the State Duma and the Federal Migration Authority.
Civil rights activists, on the other hand, asked the following question several times before the entry into force of the law in the press:
What changes does the requirement of having to show language skills bring, both for the Russians and for the guest workers?
Above all, according to spokespersons for the migration authority, it is about reducing the number of forced laborers.
"Many are now talking about protecting the rights of migrant workers," says Viktor Sebelew, head of the representation of the federal migration authority in Tajikistan.
However, their rights must already be protected from their departure.
Only organized recruitment will solve 90% of the problems of foreign workers.
Problems are encountered by migrants without an occupation, education, language skills and medical evaluation.
Sebelev is convinced that a future migrant without language skills will inevitably encounter people with evil intentions, putting him into a dark, narrow barracks under the pretext of help, where he'll endure without food and documents with dozens of consecutive months without food and documents and work 12 to 14 hours a day.
We receive a lot of complaints from our migrants.
"At homade, you are promised one, and on arrival you will be defrauded, get their passports removed and will not receive the promised wage," confirms the head of the Tajik Migration Administration's head of labour migration, Tolib Schwaripov.
Don't scold, boss.
Nevertheless, many citizens of the Central Asian republics, who want to earn their money in Russia, admit that they not only do not master the language of the target country, but also can hardly express themselves in writing in their own language.
Of course, this is not so much their fault, but rather the evil: only a few Turkmens, Uzbeks and Tajiks can only afford a reasonable primary education.
Because in their families there is not even money to feed the children, let alone to buy them decent clothes, shoes and school supplies.
As teenagers, these children immediately seize the first opportunity to make money.
"It's difficult without language skills," they admit.
"You feel humiliated and inferior."
Civic rights activists emphasize the following important aspect with regard to the law.
Only migrants with legal status have to take the test.
If you don’t have that status, there’s no exam and consequently no official work.
In the meantime, the majority of guest workers continue to stay illegally in Russia.
"Welcome" or "Obligerated for unauthorised"
Many migrants confirm that it is not easy to obtain official status in Russia.
The reason is bureaucratic hurdles and, of course, the linguistic difficulties.
In addition, legalization costs money: 12 to 16 thousand rubles.
Fake registration can be done quickly and only for one and a half thousands.
The employees of the Russian police know that our documents are mostly forged and we are not registered, and this then incurs cases of extortion.
"They want one or two hundred rubles for cigarettes and tea," Tajike Umed Hushkadamov told journalists.
"Just don't shy away for false papers"
Already on the day the law came into force, it turned out that not only the registration documents of the migrants can be forged.
Some fake certificates certifying the completion of the language test have already been seized by employees of the Federal Migration Authority.
The forged papers were printed with an ordinary color printer.
Of course, the new owners didn’t receive these for nothing: every migrant who hoped to be able to save himself the tests paid seven thousand rubles for the document.
That's more than twice as much as the cost of the official testing, which costs a migrant worker three thousand rubles.
Both officials and civil rights activists agree on the fact that the most important immediate task is to protect the system from corruption so that the certificates cannot be easily purchased.
For the moment, the authorities can only promise guest workers who fall through the test the first time that they will be given time for a basic language course.
In addition, newcomers without language skills are offered jobs in areas in which active communication with people is not necessary.
Interior ministry ignites illegal gun market
Proportion of legal guns in offences extremely small
Russia's Interior Ministry is planning a tightening of the gun law for private gun owners.
The authorities are responding to the latest events: injured in shootings at weddings as well as seven deaths in the rampage of Moscow lawyer Dmitry Vinogradov.
Police want to ban carrying non-leathing weapons in public and raise the age for possession of firearms from 18 to 21.
The proposal was supported by the chairwoman of the Duma Committee on Security and Corruption, Irina Yarowaya, and promised that the amendments to the Arms Act would soon be submitted to Duma.
Not everyone is satisfied that the Russian authorities want to combat the problems by "attracting the screws."
An open letter was published on the Internet, whose authors - representatives of various weapons associations - demand the authorities to refrain from "senseless intensifications."
The percent of crimes involving registered weapons is very low, the criminal lawyer, Vasily Lesnikov, told the BBC's Russian service.
According to the statistics of the Ministry of the Interior, 142 crimes with the use of registered weapons were committed over the course of six months in 2012, while for the same period a total of 1.168 thousand offences were recorded.
The authors of the open letter are convinced that tightening the law for private gun owners will not discourage criminals from using themselves on the black market.
According to the authors, you can currently find every possible weapon for little money in Moscow.
Meanwhile, the Interior Ministry stresses that the proliferation of illegal weapons is under control.
suppliers: from factories to officers
The weapons reach the black market via several channels.
There are five such main channels, recounts a former colonel Viktor Baranez, who worked for ten years in the Ministry of Education and the General Staff.
screenshot of a web page that accepts gun orders
The first canal is the army or the military, i.e. weapons that were stolen during the fighting in the Caucasus.
"The weapons were stolen by both the Russian officers and the Caucasians themselves," Baranez says.
On the second channel come illegal weapons, the order guards are stolen by criminals.
Baranez explains that this includes weapons that were stolen both from the armouries of the police and directly from law enforcement personnel.
Other illegally sold weapons come from military weapons depots.
There have often been explosions in many military camps.
"There is evidence that some cases were arson in order to cover up the lack of weapons," the former military official says.
According to Baranez, arms manufacturers also make a contribution to the black market.
"Today there are countless private armament factories that do not withstand the international competition and therefore squander the weapons on the black market, for example in Moscow," reports the expert.
Another channel of the black market is smuggling.
Many pistols and rifles in particular come from poor countries such as Kyrgyzstan.
"There's its own, sometimes artisanal production as well as a mafia that organizes smuggling," explains the former military member.
Where did the guns come from?
Experts calculated the share of each of the five channels in supplying the black market with illegal weapons approximately.
In 2011, a report was drafted by the Center for Problem Analysis and State Administration.
The experts analyzed reports from the Ministry of the Interior and the Federal Service for State Statistics, criminological literature and publicly available data on portals related to weapons.
According to investigators, the vast majority of illegal weapons come from the army and legal protection authorities.
Half of all guns on the black market were there through the "guilt of those responsible who dealt with weapons in their work," the report said.
According to the researchers' data, 17% of weapons come from conflict zones, 14% are attributable to thefts involved in weapons production, and 5% comes from illegal excavations.
A sales adviser to a gun shop who doesn't want to be named assures that guns found during illegal excavations haven't been burieving for a long time since they're obsolete.
According to him, it is more common for traders to get the supplies in a weapons warehouse.
Via an ensign, a gun, for example, a gun of type TT, is bought.
The gun is exhibited and handed over through the fence.
"The dealer brings the gun to the city and sells it there for 900 euros apiece with two magazines," he recounts
"Of course, the police knows about everything and therefore, if it can't comply with the plan to detect crimes, periodically conducts control purchases at dealerships that offer illegal weapons," the sales consultant adds.
"Like in a luxury shop"
Buyers and sellers most often find each other through acquaintances
I had looked at websites and blogs when someone became aware of me and suggested me come to the "Begovaya" station, where a person would be waiting for me that would take me to a certain place, where we could then negotiate the prize.
I didn't find out about the gun until there
Military commentator Viktor Baranez
"To get a gun, I need an acquaintance who has contacts," says the sales consultant. "I have such an acquaintance, but I don't trust him."
There are traders in the markets, but that's where you have to go and date a code like "Ivan Ivanovich, who lets the daughter lost a tooth."
I'm even coming to some striking rings about an acquaintance I trust.
He's also supplying me the striking rings only because he knows I don't whip him.
Newcomers are looking for guns in different ways.
Former military member Viktor Baranez tried as a buyer of illegal weapons in the mid-1990s when he wrote an article on the subject.
According to him, the schemes have not changed.
He was given a photo album depicting "everything possible."
"I felt like myself in the luxury shop," the military official recalls.
According to Baranez, the buyer does not get the cat in the sack, but can
As a potential customer, I don’t just buy, but drive into the forest with the seller, and that’s where we’re setting up a target.
"I can then shoot there, and if I'm convinced that the gun has a good shot, we'll negotiate with each other," the expert describes the crackdown.
shopping from the sofa
Internet search engines show search results for websites and groups in "Vkontakte", where weapons are offered "for various purposes".
Neither papers are needed nor are there face-to-face meetings.
"You only need the appropriate amount of money," reads the advertisement on the website "Buy fighting gun or rifle".
There, users place their orders and make enquiries.
Is it also sold to minors?
"Of course without a license," user asks "Ivan" (literal quote).
"Buy a TT. Moscow," reads "Fedorenkov's laconic request."
The Federal security service recently switched countless sham websites, and buyers who want to acquire guns go to the ne bustle of the service.
People come like hungry fish at feeding and then end up in Siberia and are allowed to shovel coal
Military commentator and former military member Viktor Baranez
I’ve heared of the following scheme: The website is usually registered abroad, and Russian laws don’t apply.
Those responsible accept the orders.
The buyer transfers the money through an ATM.
"In response, he receives a photo and a description of the gun's hideout," reports the press secretary of the non-proficient organization "Pravo na oryzhie" Dmitriy Kislov.
Viktor Baranez emphasizes that at the end you can also go out empty and come behind bars if you place a request on a website.
The Federal security service recently switched countless sham websites, and buyers who want to acquire guns go to the ne bustle of the service.
"People come like hungry fishes at feeding and then end up in Siberia's labour campaign and are allowed to shovel coal," he says.
A Makarov for US$100
According to experts, prices of 100 to 900 US dollars are to be expected in the purchase of illegal firearms.
According to Dmitry Kislov of the organization "Pravo na oryzhie" you can buy a Makarov pistol for 100 to 300 dollars.
You've had to wait up to one and a half months.
The gun is issued in mid-range overseer military weapons depots.
According to the official statistics of the Ministry of the Interior, the number of offenses involving illegal weapons across Russia declined by 7% to 22.9 thousand in the period from January to October 2011, while the number of uncovered cases of theft and extortion related to weapons, ammunition, explosives and explosive devices shranked by 7.8%.
In the U.S., fast-food and supermarket workers are on strike
One in four U.S. teenagers sits behind Mc Donald's checkout at some point
In the past few days, there have been massive protests everywhere in the U.S. against low wages in branches of Walmart and restaurants of popular fast-food chains such as Mc Donald's, Burger King, Taco Bell, Wendy's and Kentucky Fried Chicken.
No one can yet predict whether the protests will swell even further or whether they will soon run out of air again.
The protests are backed by unions and several left-leaning non-profit organizations.
In addition to increasing the modest wages of ordinary Walmart and fast-food workers, the organizers of the protests are pursuing the goal of starting unions.
So far, this industry of the economy has hardly been unionized.
46 cents a year?
The protests began last week after Thanksgiving, so-called Black Friday, when sales took place in the U.S. that attracted millions of customers, which at times led to scratches.
On this day, some Walmart employees, of whom there are 2.2 million worldwide, put down their jobs and, together with unions and left-wing activists, striked branches of the chain whose target group are low- and middle-income individuals.
Walmart sells all products, from diapers to hunting rifles and car batteries to vacuum cleaners, eggs and milk.
The goods in the Walmart stores are on average between 8 and 27% cheaper than in large supermarkets.
That's why many of the slightly paid Walmart employees only buy in their own workplace.
Prices and the range of goods made Walmart one of the largest companies in the United States.
According to critics, Walmart can often sell the goods so cheaply only because the employees are poorly paid.
The latter also complain about severe working conditions, for example missing forklifts or handheld scanners.
Participants in "Black Friday" protests called for wage increases and voiced their annoyance that the contribution to in-house health insurance had risen from 30 to 100 dollars a month.
A typical Walmart employee who earns US$9.5 per hour could not afford that increase, they said.
Researchers from California's Berkeley University prove that if the company raises the average wage to 12 dollars an hour, it would cost Walmart 3.2 billion dollars.
That's about 1.1% more than they're donating to wages now.
If Walmart were to completely transfer the wage increases to customers, each purchase would cost them only 46 cents more in the future.
For them, this would be an additional expenditure of only 12.39 dollars per year compared to today.
Proponents of Walmart are satisfied that there were protests only in nine states and that they did not cause any losses to the company.
Black Friday lasted in its store from 8 a.m. on Thursday until midnight the next day, and in that period Walmart sold about 5000 items a second.
In total, Walmart coffers carried out nearly 100 million transactions on "Black Friday."
The company's spokesperson, Dan Fogelman, claimed in an interview on the left-wing Huffington Post's website that a total of "less than five" Walmart employees put down the work and the protest action was merely "another PR trick" by the union that organized the protests.
"Free checkout!"
The protests continued this week in New York, where their target, however, was not Walmart stores (which are not wanted in this progressive city and are therefore previously non-existent), but were McDonald's and other low-priced restaurants.
McDonald's says the company sells billions of portions, and yet you're not granted sick days, and you don't get paid for your honest work!
Jumaane Williams, Member of the New York City Council
Currently, according to federal and New York legislation, the minimum wage is 7.25 dollars an hour.
In fast-food restaurants, the minimum wage is gradually raised, but not too strong, and on average, their ordinary employees in New York get 8.90 US dollars an hour.
No one earns less than them in this expensive city.
How it's possible to get by in New York with so little money is incomprehensible to me.
There was once a time when almost a quarter of all U.S. teenagers stood behind the counter at McDonald's after school, but the teenagers were still living with their parents.
Hardly anyone saw the main source of income or planned to stay there longer.
Now during interviews with employees of McDonald's, I constantly encounter people who complain that they have to get along with this wages and sometimes even feed their children.
On the other hand, there is a commementation in the Wall Street Journal forum, whose author notes that it is irresponsible to have children if you don’t know how to feed them.
Participants in the protests, which began at 6.30 a.m. on Thursday outside McDonald's on 40th Street and Madison Avenue, called for cashiers and chefs from fast-food restaurants to receive a US$15-dollar minimum wage per hour, more than a doubling of the current minimum wage.
They continued to call for the formation of unions in the fast-food industry.
U.S. law prohibits corporate governance from standing in the way of that goal, warning or dismissaling union members.
On the other hand, corporate governance doesn’t make it easy for them
However, the establishment of trade unions in the fast food industry is also difficult for objective reasons.
The most important reason is high staff turnover.
Unsatisfied
There were loud protests that day in front of many other fast-food restaurants in Manhattan.
The culmination of the protests was noon at noon outside McDonald's in Times Square where some local Democrats spoke, including city council member Jumaane Williams, who said: "McDonald's says it's selling billions of portions, and yet you're not granted sick days, and you're not getting paid for your honest work!"
The protesters were also backed by other prominent New York Democrats, for example, mayoral candidate Bill De Blasio, who said that "we should jointly support the fast-food employees so that they can receive a fair wage and achieve the economic prosperity that every New Yorker deserves."
According to New York Times, this was the largest action of its kind in the history of the U.S. fast-food industry.
However, only a few hundred people were on the scene, far from all of whom are employed in the fast-food industry, where tens of thousands work in New York.
It is not yet clear whether the spark of these protests will mobilize entire masses.
"The brain can only be outslated moderately so far"
The topic of augmented reality, which is popular with followers of modern technologies, has recently been usually perceived through the lens of special glasses.
At its annual conference in the summer, Google first presented a working model, in November it was announced that Microsoft had also filed for a corresponding patent.
Speaking to the head of the group for interactive 3D technologies at the Microsoft lab in Cambridge Shahram Izadi, however, it becomes clear that the glasses were only a transit station for the company's scientists.
It attracts the ability to manipulate virtual objects in the air with their bare hands and to create virtual open spaces.
- Please briefly describe what research your group conducts.
- We deal with the interaction of man and machine, but want to expand the boundaries of this interaction.
So far, humanity has mostly got stuck in the phase of working with pixels on flat screens, which are also sometimes touched.
However, we want to look ahead 5-10 years and foresee the radical changes of this interaction.
For example, the Xbox console and Kinect sensors are progressive, and these days no Xbox is sold without Kinect because everyone is interested in motion control.
- What awaits us in the future?
- Although Kinect has taken the interaction to a physical level, a lot still takes place on the flat screen, sometimes in 3D.
The input of information (the system receives more data) could be improved, the output has not yet been decisive.
We’re trying to change that, working on real three-dimensional imaging systems based on different technologies, including projections.
We need to release the computer world into our physical world and make it more comprehensible.
However, it is necessary that not only the user, but also his environment is recognized.
Then we could complement the real world much more easily with virtual objects.
And first of all, let's get rid of these stupid virtual reality helmets!
- What do you think about voice control?
She's popular, but won't she be overrated?
- She’s certainly not the all-purpose solution, as she raises questions about privacy because you don’t always want to inform all bystanders about his actions and intentions.
Actually, any kind of interaction with computers is good, but each in their particular niche.
For example, in the field of controlling objects in public places, we worked on a project in which we experimented with short and concise gestures instead of expansive gestures.
The gestures were not recorded by the object camera, but by a bracelet that registered the movements of the bones and muscles.
It is still relatively large, but could theoretically be reduced to the size of a wristwatch.
But overall, however, the future belongs to mixed control types, such as gestures and language.
- Like this?
We'll ask me, for example, to sufficient this bubble can?
They say it and at the same time
- I'm usually just saying it.
Oh, that'd be very hard to recognize.
- Does that mean you want the user to adapt to what a machine can and can’t do at the particular time?
- Not necessarily, but it's about a mutual rapprochement here.
I think that in the near future we will have to work mainly on the development of new sensor types, which will make it possible to capture reactions of people more closely.
For example, these can be laser sensors, as they offer good resolution in depth, which is very important.
- What demands do you make based on your work with Xbox Kinect sensors on modern cameras?
Is there a lack of dissolution, depth or anything else?
- The current generation is basically a good starting point for our work in the field of three-dimensional recognition.
Of course, 8 megapixels with a speed of 1000 fps wouldn’t be bad.
But the main thing is not the megapixels themselves, but the quality of the matrix and depth.
From this point of view, all current technologies are not good enough for us, as the developers of the algorithms have more work.
It has to be taken into account not only the resolution along the X and Y axes, but also along the Z-axis.
The speed and the number of frames per second is also important.
The movements of man are extremely dynamic and frankly, the current 30 fps are too little, especially for gestures.
Stephen Betishes developed a touch sensor with a controllable processing delay between 1 to 100 milliseconds, with today's commercial sensors closer to the second (60 to 100).
Not everyone understands how much this affects the interaction of man and machine.
For my work I needed just such a device, only without touch control and with more frames per second.
Wouldn't the number of cameras be increased?
- Kinect currently works with three “cameras”, one of which is actually an infrared transmitter and the second is a receiver for the reflected signal.
And the third camera is actually an ordinary RGB camera.
It is not used to calculate object depth.
More cameras could potentially solve the problem...
Or intensify the problem, as more calculations would be necessary.
Beautiful would be a flexible Kinect-like system where we could play with different camera inclinations to determine how this helps us in the three-dimensional determination of a position.
- If I remember correctly, unlike Google, Microsoft didn’t present its glasses to the public.
Don’t you think that with regard to the application of the technology of augmented reality in everyday life, the glasses have the best prospects?
- Of course it’s not pleasant to always hold a smartphone in the raised hand on the way, but I think this: The greatest variant would be a “changing” augmented reality, in which we could switch from the glasses to the smartphone via a cloud,
The glasses are a personal gadget and therein lies their strength (privates can only see you), but at the same time also their weakness, because an augmented reality by means of glasses prevents that you can work together with others on virtual objects.
- Let’s imagine for a moment that the manipulation of virtual holograms in the air is accessible not only Tony Stark from Iron Man, but also normal mortals.
There is one problem that critics of this idea often point to: there is no palpable reaction!
The hands don't feel anything!
What answers does your group want to give to this objection?
- In my lectures, I often talk that augmented reality represents the seventh breakthrough in the interaction between man and machine.
I think the eighth breakthrough could well become the integration of tactile perception.
An interesting method is currently the use of the second hand as a somewhat unusual projection document.
She perceives pressure excellently!
However, there are also technologies that are actually geared towards adding something tangible to the "images in the air", for example, the interference of some directed ultrasonic beams at a certain point, where the finger is located, creates a feeling that is still as weak at the moment, as if someone was blowing over the wrists.
There are also ligaments for the wrist, which act on the nerve endings in the fingers, even that is a potential direction.
Did you try to outwit the brain?
It makes you think it feels what it should feel when it sees something right now?
That's a good idea, that's not how we've tried yet.
Here hides another task that we will have to deal with for a long time to come, namely to let a person who is physically located in an enclosed space believe that he is instead in an open, virtually infinite space; here we are working on concepts with treadmills (other than in gyms), moving platforms as well as large spheres.
So far, the brain can only be outwitted moderately, and there are still many busy years ahead.
This is precisely what makes the work in the field of virtual reality so attractive for a researcher: We are only at the beginning of many topics.
Own taste instead of culture - rosbalt.ru
Rosbalt continues the "Petersburg Avant-Garde" project, dedicated to city dwellers who form the avant-garde of art and culture.
High-ranking representatives of the Petersburg art scene, whose successes go beyond the borders of the city and who often find recognition in Europe without being known in the rest of Russia.
Rosbalt's new hero is flamboyant artist Kirill Miller.
The whole city knows Kirill Miller, the beard dressed in red, who is sometimes found at the Russian Museum, sometimes at the summer garden, sometimes at scene meetings or at vernissages.
Wherever Kirill Miller's pictures are exhibited, they attract large crowds.
Kirill Miller is one of the typical Petersburg social-philosophical artists creating new myths.
Kirill Miller is a luminous personality of the St. Petersburg avant-garde at the end of the 80s or the beginning of the 90s.
He is also a man of the city and conjures a smile in the face through his presence on the street and ensures a good mood for them.
Recently, he snatched a spinning organ and became thain a St. Petersburg organist, as he finally felt ready for this difficult role with regard to his artist existence, his philosophy and his image.
- Kirill, why are you always in red and not in yellow or turquoise in the city?
I have chosen the color red as a fashion designer, which deals with the look and the image.
In this world, red is the compromise between artists, image consultants and society.
In society, anything that deviates somewhat from the grey norm evokes aggression and bad behavior.
But I want to talk to people through my provocations.
Each of my past provocations was an invitation to the conversation.
When did you know that you had to become a painter?
- At an exhibition at Newsky’s Palace of Culture, where my works were exhibited.
I realized I have to go down this path.
Then the time of the old free, but not officially recognized painters came to an end at that time, and new and free painters
I befriended the painters of the new generation, i.e. with the representatives of the "Gazonewschina" (Gazonevschina); from this "Pushkinskaya 10", and then it was over.
It draws me to the theater, to fashion, to the music, actually to everything, except literature.
- And all of this came together in your art clinic... it was important to me to be at the heart of Petersburg culture, where the best creating artists should gather.
In 1995 I occupied a room in Pushkinskaya 10, and there was a musical-artistical club for the Boheme until the renovation work.
Many were born there: N.O.M., tequilajazzz, and I also remark how Schnur got there with his "Ucho Van Goga" project.
Schnur and his friends sang simple songs to playback, wore tight jerseys and among them were the now trendy composer Igor Wdowin.
When the group started to play live, it became "Leningrad."
Trachtenberg moderated many programs before the time of "Halli-Galli."
We gave them Trachtenberg, and his great career began, but he learned the basics with us.
The gallery "D 137" or the club "Griboedov" also have their origins in the art clinic.
That's where our staff and regulars went.
I'm a hero of the past century, when culture still meant something.
In 2000 there was a survey in the press, which was about the "personalities of our city" award.
I won "Artists of the Year" category, and with that I had reached the peak.
It's not easy to work in new times according to old rules; I'm a person of the truth, honesty and culture of the past century.
In modern times it’s easy to become famous, but culture and popularity aren’t the same thing: you can be famous without making a cultural contribution.
Your works have an unmistakable style.
- Many of my pictures are real pearls and clearly express the topicality and sharpness of thoughts.
I'm going to have an exhibition with the theme "The Russian museum as clowns."
Clowns are timeless.
I used to be social, but now it hurts and scares to be like this.
In a clown, all this blurs and loses its tragedy.
I love the grotesques, and I have grotesque ideas.
For example, let’s save the world by pretending that everyone has to disguise themselves completely.
Now everyone pays attention to the clothes and orients on the exterior and not on the interior of man.
But maybe you can't give a person's hand, but should spit in his face.
The deception vanishes due to disguise.
- Recently, we've seened you in the role of an urban spinning organal player.
Who, if not me?
Can commercial art be beautiful too?
- Nowadays, commercial art must be cautious, considerate and flattering.
We see the disintegration of cultures.
In the past, people formed large groups: The Bohemiens loved one and the simple people loved the other.
Now there are micro-communities, and it's difficult to please everyone.
I'm not a one-hundred-dollar note everyone likes.
Now you have to wonder who you're going to please.
Now every cult star has about 100 followers.
- However, several thousand come to Stas Mikhailov's performances!
- Leased women come to him, that’s where the sexual-social sphere plays a role.
For cultural reasons there are 300 people and not 10 thousand, consequently there is less management, money, and everything perishes.
I've got followers, and the most important thing isn't defrauding them and not destroying what's achieved.
In my youth I have painted such pictures, which even hung with a collector in a row with the works of Falk and Larionow.
I started with pictures that usually don't come until the end of my career.
There are often misunderstandings nowadays.
It's called spiritual culture and consumer culture.
Consumption has no culture at all, that's something completely different.
I'm a person of past culture and grew up with artists who lived in poverty and died in poverty and rejected money to paint.
That's the culture I'm advocating.
Kirill, what's missing from Petersburg?
- Good cultural experts.
It is said: servants of culture.
However, not every one of them can deal with culture.
It was different with the real tsars, because the Tsars may not understand much of culture, but they understood that proper experts are necessary.
There are currently good advisors in Moscow.
In Petersburg, we have some that could be experts, but they are being blocked, as they are needed by higher-level experts who correctly evaluate the former and clear the way for them.
Now only one's own taste counts.
The same applies to the erarta, but with the difference that they honestly say that they don’t assume all contemporary art and some painters therefore have to look for other museums.
- What's Petersburg for you?
- Petersburg is not the cultural capital; in Moscow there is much more culture and a corresponding breeding ground.
It’s difficult for art to grow on our stones.
We need a cultural breeding ground because, for example, we now have more writers than readers, and that’s not right.
In Europe there are many curious people who attend exhibitions or concerts.
It's much less with us.
Art has to be modern again, just as at the beginning of the last century.
The project is financed by grants from the City of Saint Petersburg
Birth in space
Soil at risk.
Global warming or clash with killer asteroid.
Caravans of spaceships with Earth's inhabitants on board set out in search of a replacement planet.
In order to save humanity, the important question arises as to how we can propagate in microgravity or on the replacement planet?
In my opinion, there are not many choices.
De facto, there are only two real planets that we could hypothetically inhabit.
"Namely Mars and Venus," says the research associate at the Astronometric Sternberg Institute Vladimir Surdin.
However, while the conditions on Mars are comparatively life-friendly, a temperature of 500 degrees prevails on the surface of Venus.
Life would only be possible at high altitude or in the orbit of Venus, i.e. in space.
The researchers first dealt with the question of reproduction in space with regard to flora.
Already half a century before, experiments with plants were carried out.
The fourth generation of peas bred in orbit did not differ in the slightest from the earth's species.
This was followed by breeding insects, more precisely from tiny fruit flies (Drosophila), in orbit.
In 1979, quail eggs were sent into space to explore how microgravity affects the development of embryos.
A normal chick develops.
But then the problem begins.
"The problem is that the chick has to find support; it has to be able to stand on its legs and begin to move," explains the laboratory director of the Institute for Medical-Biological Problems RAN, Vladimir Sychew.
The chicks overturned wildly without a solid ground.
Ten hours later, the instincts of the newborns dulled completely.
The chicks didn't respond to light and sounds.
Four days later, they simply died.
"Twice we had chicks hatched and then hired the whole, because it's simply impossible to work with them there," Vladimir Sychev notes about the failed experiment with the chicks in space.
The last incubated cage with animals flew into orbit 16 years ago.
The experiments will continue in the spring of 2013.
However, only same-sex species will be located on the biosatellite "Bion".
There was an experiment with rats that have already been fertilized into space.
In principle, nothing special has been established.
"This was just with the biosatellites, but again it was just a single experiment, and the research must continue," notes Vladimir Sychew.
After landing, the space rats got offspring.
The problem of reproducing directly in space has not yet been solved.
The task is difficult.
Animals ripped out of their natural habitat simply cannot follow their reproductive instinct.
In contrast to animals, humans could do this in principle.
Homo sapiens is capable of abstract thinking and can produce a similar emotional background.
Such experiments are not carried out for ethical considerations.
However, women have been flying into space for 50 years.
There was the greatest risk at Tereshkova.
The most valuable thing humanity possesses is the female organism.
Our seagull flew away, and no one on Earth could have answered the following question: Does it survive the space flight?
Will she be able to give birth to a child after this flight?
"After all, no one answered that question," says space veteran Wachtang Watschnadse.
In June 1964, just one year after her space flight, the first female cosmonautine, Valentina Tereshkova, gave up a daughter.
The child's father, Andriyan Nikolayev, was also cosmonaut.
In 1988, the second woman in space, Svetlana Sawitzkaja, who was in orbit twice and even did work outside the spacecraft, gave birth to a son.
Nevertheless, there remains a risk.
We have very few cosmonauts where everything went well after long flights and they got healthy offspring.
"The more, the more dangerous it is even on flights into orbit," concludes cosmonaut Valeri Polyakov, hero of the Soviet Union and hero of the Russian Federation.
And yet humanity needs to look for new ways in biotechnology, radiation protection and the creation of artificial gravitation.
The hydro laboratory in the cosmonaut's preparatory center is an obligatory station during flight preparation.
Here in a limbo, the cosmonauts gain routine for working in open space.
The water represents weightlessness.
Even if adults perceive water as pleasant, it is a foreign environment; yet infants perceive water as a natural element.
It's as if the little ichtyanders bear witness that life on the land came from the ocean.
There is a connection between the fact that a child is in the mother’s abdomen in the amniotic fluid for about 9 months, and that it is easier to get used to water.
In principle, this is logical, since from the birth until the first swim, only 2 weeks pass.
"That's too little time to forget something," says baby swimming instructor Marina Aksenowa.
While for a newborn child weightlessness is therefore a much more natural state, the woman does not manage without gravity.
In the state of weightlessness, the abdominal and pelvic muscles regress enormously quickly and thus the ability to squeeze out also decreases.
All right, let's get around this through birth-initiating aids.
Birth, for example, could also take place in a special chamber to squeeze out the child.
"But then what happens?" is Valeri Polyakov's not insignificant question.
On the other hand, the child also needs artificial gravity.
Without feeling the Earth's attraction, the organism cannot form the skeleton and muscles.
A newborn child cannot be put into a suspense in orbit like an adult for training.
He will simply miss what is of fundamental importance for his life.
"This experiment, which we undertake together, namely the birth of a child in a foreign environment, will lead us to give birth to a disabled person, a completely unadjusted person," predicts Igor Pestov, head of the Bioethics Commission of the Institute for Medical-Biological Problems RAN.
So far, the birth of children in space is mere theory.
At some point, however, it becomes a reality, when the inhabitants of the Earth break into a distant planet in their spaceships, which becomes the home for their descendants born in space.
NKÚ boss: Control of the black system fails due to fault of politics
In connection with public contracts, the Czech Republic has good supervisory authorities and adequate laws, but falls behind in their application.
In an interview for Aktuálně.cz, the Vice-President of the Highest Control Office (Nejvyšší kontrolní úřad, NKÚ), Miloslav Kala, says this.
"A law is never perfect, but it has to be applied correctly - and I don't think that's the case with us," Kala comments.
A similar conclusion apparently also comes to a common control by Czech and German examiners.
As a negative practical example, he cites Prime Minister Petr Nečas’ attitude towards the so-called “black system”.
Recently, the prime minister said that the ODS would not harass the entrepreneurs with checks - so is the system now banned or allowed?
"A law must be valid, and if something is prohibited by law, then it is not easy for the head of the government to come, and the control office, whose duty is the control of compliance with the law, impedes in its activity," Kala notes.
At the meeting of the control committee of the Chamber of Deputies, you talked about a joint Czech-German projection in which the laws of both countries regarding public contracts were compared.
What specifically was it about?
It is a parallel test with which we started about two years ago.
In simple terms, it is a question of European legislature setting out how to handle public contracts. This is what the national legislation builds on. And after all, there is still the actual practical application.
We have compared all this with each other, and although the test has not yet been completed, we were able to notice extremely interesting differences. Generally speaking, the laws with us are perhaps even somewhat "stricter and more perfect", but the actual application is better in some parameters in Germany.
This confirms that it is not enough to establish new and more precise rules again and again. One must also deal with the actual application of law.
To what extent can the project be of help to you? What arises for you from this project?
Just such joint control could help us stop constantly striving to perfect the laws and the lowering and balancing of thresholds if this apparently does not have a particularly positive effect.
Profitability means acquiring one thing at a reasonable price (which is not always the lowest price) and excluding unlawful enrichment or even criminal acts.
But lowering thresholds for orders does not guarantee that we will achieve this goal as well.
Maybe it's even that in this way we're only burdening the system with excessive "paperwork," and those looking for gaps in the system have it much easier to take advantage of the system than if the thresholds remained higher.
You are talking about the problems in the practical implementation of the Czech Procuremence Act.
How does the control system in Germany work?
Is there an authority similar to our NKÚ here, or does the system work differently?
As far as the authorities are concerned, the German counterpart to the NKÚ of the Federal Court of Auditors, which is structured in a similar way as with us and also has a quorum that is merely formed slightly differently. In principle, however, both authorities work similarly.
The competences are also comparable to a certain extent, although Germany is a federal state, i.e. that there are these Courts of Auditors also in the individual federal states - in which the German system differs from ours.
The BRH is only allowed to control federal funds, which corresponds to our state funds.
Public funds managed by the districts and municipalities are under the control of the state audit offices there.
As far as German legislation is concerned, is it simpler than ours?
In general, I would hate to compare this without concrete information. Nevertheless, Germany is often referred to in some respects as a role model, although it is certainly not possible to say that Germany is better in everything.
Maybe it's because the examiners are better there?
I don’t like it, but I don’t like such comparisons.
It should be noted that even those countries, which are an example for us, also have to deal with problems.
Otherwise, they wouldn’t have any cooperation with our authority, wouldn’t they?
As far as Czech legislation is concerned, the significance for your authority to amend the Procuremency Act, do you already feel any effects?
In view of the fact that this amendment is only in force for a short time, it has not yet had an effect on our examinations.
Since our checks take place retrospectively, one must expect a certain delay here.
So far, we are more likely to feel the effects in the preparatory phase of future checks - this is how we introduced the new control instrument "Identification of risks of inefficiacy" and examined and analysed almost 14,000 public contracts with it - the changes will have a natural effect here, as the thresholds change, the conditions for certain types of tenders have been modified, e.g.
Do you see the adoption of this law rather positively, or is the system burdened with further bureaucracy?
I think that this law is certainly a step in the right direction and hope that this assumption is confirmed.
However, the problem may arise that the rules become "stricter" and can no longer be adhered to.
Under the previous rules, the persons subject to an examination are accountable to the institution (for example, in the case of an operational programme of regional policy, the Regional Council) in such a way that any non-compliance with the Procuremency Act is equivalent to a violation of budgetary discipline.
But does it make sense to make the law even stricter?
I don't think in that way.
The system must exclude those who want to attack and abuse the system, and not punish those who have committed a formal error that does not affect actual decision-making.
Such a system only puts even greater pressure on bureaucracy.
Where's the way out here?
Let’s first say which path isn’t expedient.
The prime minister recently said that ODS wouldn't bother entrepreneurs with controls on the so-called "black system" - what exactly does that mean?
Is the black system now prohibited or permitted?
A law must have its validity, and if something is prohibited by law, then it is not easy for the chairman of the government to come, and hinder the control office, whose duty is the control of compliance with the law.
He can say, "We're changing the law and allowing the matter," but he can't say we're pretending.
The Procurement Act contains relatively strict rules on the formal execution of certain transactions - and this is also correct for the protection of public competition.
On the other hand, however, it is also tragic if the bidder with the best offer is excluded solely due to formal errors.
The law will never be perfect, but it must be applied correctly - and that's what I think we lack.
On the roads there is occasional ice smoothness, but they are mostly dry.
On the roads of the Czech Republic there is partly snow and ice smoothness.
Predominantly, however, the roads are easily passable, sometimes increased caution is required.
Karlovy Vary
In the Karlovy Vary district, the roads were easily navigable this morning, occasionally there was snow and ice smoothness.
Temperatures dropped to -5 to -10 °C, during the day it is supposed to get a little warmer.
In the region it is no longer snowing now, and only a thin blanket of snow remains in the lower altitudes.
On the ridges of the Ore Mountains, however, there is about 30 cm of snow.
According to the district road guard, there was limited visibility because of fog.
The R6 expressway and the traffic routes of the first road class in the region are currently fully accessible.
Attention, however, is required, for example, on bridges on which the roadways are sometimes icy and smooth.
All traffic routes of the second and third road classes, including the mountain roads, are also passable.
On these roads, however, sections of iced or extended snow remains.
Increased attention is therefore required, especially in the higher altitudes.
Pardubice and Hradec Králové
On some streets of East Bohemia there is danger due to ice smoothness, at higher altitudes and in the mountains there is sometimes an extended layer of snow on the roads, informs the road and highway directorate on their websites.
The road masters warn riders of ice smoothness, which can occur especially at higher altitudes in Pardubice district.
Ice smoothness can occur in particular on second- and third-range traffic routes in Laškroun, Ústí nad Orlicí, Polička, Svitavy and Vysoké Mýto.
Streets I/43 and I/34 at Svitavy are chemically treated.
Snow is located especially on the roads in the Giant Mountains and in the Orlické hory mountains.
An extended layer of snow lies at high altitudes on the roads of Rychnov nad Kněžnou and Trutnov.
It is mostly sunny or serene in eastern Bohemia today, and it remains largely free of precipitation.
Temperatures range between -3 and +1 °C, there is a weak wind.
Pilsen district
The roads in the district of Pilsen are easily navigable today, but there is increased local attention, and the procedure must be adapted to the weather.
This is frosty in the morning, with temperatures between 3 and 9 degrees below freezing.
Since it snoved before and the temperatures subsequently fell, it can lead to ice smoothness on the roads.
Fog can also be expected locally, but the visibility will improve in the course of the day.
This information comes from the district road maintenance companies.
The highway D5 is practically unrestricted passable, but increased attention recommends the road masteries between the 80th and the 131st kilometer.
The roadways of the first street class are predominantly dry, but frozen.
In the south of Pilsen and Tachov districts, however, it came in sections to ice smoothness.
The roadways of the second and third road classes are damp, and therefore the indication of possible icy sections also applies here.
Drivers should be particularly careful mainly on the less frequented roads in the Bohemian Forest area.
Olomouc district
Snow mud has to be counted against drivers today, who are heading towards the high altitudes in Olomouc district.
After the use of scattering vehicles, Schneematsch remains on the Červenohorské sedlo pass as well as on the road towards Videlský kříž.
Nocturnal snow drift forced the road masteries to work, in the district of Šumperk about 3 cm of fresh snow fell, according to the road administration.
In the other areas of the district, the carriageways are usually unrestrictedly navigable.
"In Šumperk County, there are still remains of remnants of snow at high altitudes.
On the Červenohorské sedlo pass, drivers have to reckon with snow mud in the direction of Jeseník," an employee of Šumperk road administration told ČTK today.
Her colleagues in Jeseník district also had to go out at night, according to their data, the roads are free and wet up to the high altitudes after chemical treatment.
In Olomouc district, the roads are unrestrictedly accessible, but in the Šternberk district, the drivers have to drive cautiously in forest sections, as the roadways are still damp here.
Ústí nad Labem district, Liberec district
In northern Bohemia, the road masteries report several poorly passable road sections.
In addition to several places with snow or ice smoothness, the mountain road from Telnice to Knínice in Ústí n.K. district is closed, according to the traffic police.
Temperatures remain below zero even at lower altitudes, so snow and ice will stay on the streets. No problems are reported from the lowlands, especially south-east of the Bohemian Central Mountains, on the other hand, and the roads there are usually dry.
So far, no traffic disruptions have been reported.
Ice smoothness is reported in particular from around Štětí.
According to meteorologists, there were ideal conditions for this at night - during the dayly rain and melting snow, at night cloudless skies and frost.
Poor traffic conditions are reported from the main routes as well as from I/13 on the Ústí n.L
Closuring the road between Telnice and Knínice was necessary due to branches pushed from the snow load to the carriagewalk.
Šimon Ornest: At our concerts we want to gather positive energy
What do you think that in less than a month's time the doomsday will take place?
That's one of those disaster reports that we're so excited about.
With our band The Tap Tap, we're more likely to make fun and say that we're the only band in the world that can gather enough positive energy to stop or prevent doomsday.
In December, you will also realize a unique project with three concerts against doomsday.
Can you introduce the project to our readers?
It is a nationwide charity event that we have been planning for two years.
For this, we want to take advantage of the marketing potential of the Mayan calendar, the end of which is forecast for December 21 at 11:10 in the morning.
The day before, i.e. on December 20, there will be three concerts in Prague, Brno and Ostrava from 21:00 onwards.
The end of the concerts is supposed to be around the time, when the Mayan calendar ends on the Pacific island of Kiribati, which is in a 12-hour time zone ahead of us.
From whom did this idea come from?
Originally probably from me, then we designed the idea together with our graphic artist Honza Augusta.
In doing so, we not only want to gather enough positive energy to prevent the end of the world, but we also want to think together with the public about the state in which we hand over this world to our children.
On the occasion of the end of the Mayan calendar, we have also developed a unique collection of shoes, t-shirts, bags and keychains against doomsday, whose purchase via www.e-tap.cz can also support us.
Together with other performers, the band The Tap Tap has also recorded the so-called hymn against doomsday with the title "Doomsday falls out".
Even today, the hymn is very popular on YouTube. Will it also resound at the benefit concerts?
Of course, at the very end, provided that everything works out and the doomsday does not take place...
The anthem should be sung by all performers at all three concerts at the same time.
In addition, the hymns will be televised live together in a direct circuit from Czech television.
The text of the hymn comes from Tomáš Hanák, who also plays the main character of Jesus in the video clip, Xindl X also sings with...
How did you come to cooperate with both of them?
We also cooperate with other personalities of the Czech cultural scene, which is related to the fact that we realize many charity events and concerts...
We make every effort to involve these people intensively in our projects.
It has been shown that most are interested in working with us and have fun.
What is the benefit of the proceeds of the concerts against doomsday?
For the establishment of the barrier-free education center Studeo, where we have been working together with the association Tap at the Jedlička Institute for six years.
The students of the Jedlička Institute are regularly supervised by editors who help them with different leisure activities.
Since the students themselves do not have the means to visit the lecturers, we want to help them in this way.
As part of the completion of the Jedlička Institute, a separate building is to be built, into which we then move with this project.
At each concert several groups and performers appear.
According to which criteria did you select them?
We wanted to put together a program that appeales to all generations including children.
In Prague, for example, Chinaski, Support Lesbiens, Illustratosphere with Dan Bárta, The Tapp, Marián Bango and Jiří Suchý occur.
You can find out more on the website www.kpks.cz.
Are similar "large events" also planned for the future?
In May we perform for the first time at the Prague Spring, and also for this concert we prepare an interesting program with interesting guests.
Next year we would also like to perform at the Czech House in New York, and I personally - if we are already in the USA - this would also like to connect with performances in Washington and Chicago.
Your foreign plans aren’t exactly small, so far you’ve already appeared in Madrid, Brussels, London, or in Moscow.
The Tap Tap, however, is a group composed of people with disabilities.
How do you manage these journeys logistically and organizationally?
It's not as bad as it seems at first sight.
Five of our members are wheelchair users, i.e. the wheelchairs have to be transported in the luggage compartment. In addition, of course, we have to carry a lot of instrument cases and other luggage...
Nevertheless, it has always worked without any problems, ČSA and British Airways have always been very well prepared for us that I have sometimes wondered.
Even in Moscow, where we were recently, there were no problems.
Due to your trips abroad, you have a comparison of what it looks like in the individual countries with accessibility, how to deal with disabled people, etc.
How have you experienced these things so far?
After Madrid, Luxembourg, London and other cities, where everything works better than with us, we were just witnesses in Russia that everything in the east is still in its beginnings.
Opposite Prague, Moscow is absolutely non-disabled, where it is still uncommon when someone in a wheelchair is travelling alone in the city centre.
It is not even self-evident there that a wheelchair user, for example in the elevator.
Luckily, there are also associations that try to point out the problems of people with disabilities.
And to what extent do we have to catch up with the more developed countries?
There are still a lot of things where we're hanging behind...
In addition, however, it should be noted that an improvement of the situation always depends on the effort of those affected.
In London and Madrid it is absolutely common for people with severe disabilities to move independently in public spaces, go to the museum, use toilets, etc.
However, it is not exactly common for a larger group of disabled people to actively participate in the social events there, in which we are a little ahead with the band The Tap Tap!
Respection in public and accessibility is one thing, but it's only when important athletes, artists, actors, politicians or lawyers come from our ranks that things will really begin to change.
So far, these are only individual cases of particularly strong-willed personalities.
The Tap Tap is very popular right now, but if we go back a few years: What motivated you to form a band in 1998?
At that time I started as a supervisor at the Jedlička Institute, where I met many young people who were interested in dealing with something intensively.
And since I am a musician myself and play saxophone, among other things, I founded a Musik-AG together with a colleague.
Well, and over time, as our moderator, Láďa Angelovič, tends to say is a bit out of control (laughs).
However, the band has only become known in the last few years, or are I mistaken?
It is true that the cooperation with well-known singers has helped us; we have also started to actively promote ourselves.
We have become aware that if you do something and this cannot be seen on the outside, one’s own performance does not exist in a certain sense.
Thanks to EU funding, we can also afford good editors, high-quality devices, etc.
Is it your goal to bring it this far with The Tap Tap?
From the very beginning, I felt the potential that you can approach things differently.
In show business today, one often copies the other.
This is logical in its own way, as everything new is viewed with caution and is only accepted after a long time.
There are only a few truly unique things, and I dare say that tap tap is one of them.
The first impression that the viewer usually has is, of course, pity; that's only natural...
But this pity is absolutely superfluous, because people with disabilities are not lonely and abandoned beings that we would have to regret.
It is people who can live and develop a full-fledged life, provided they have the necessary means.
I always say that when people with disabilities succeed, this is not only progress for themselves, but for the whole of society.
Was it also helpful for this success that you are a strict boss, as you hear from a lot of people?
If you want to do something well, you must not compromise on many things and have to demand a certain discipline.
In my opinion, this is completely normal.
Some come to us with romantic ideas, and if they then find that they have rehearsal twice a week, have to complete preparation phases and spend a lot of time on concert tours, then the enthusiasm of many quickly extinguishes.
But that’s how it works everywhere, in every band that wants to work seriously and achieve something.
The Tap Tap currently has twenty members.
How many of these were there from the beginning, i.e. since 1998?
Only one: Láďa Angelovič.
We're an open group, and people come and go, you can't prevent that.
Whoever has the will and the desire to participate is always welcome with us.
It takes place the day before the projected doomsday, Thursday 20.12.2012 at 9 p.m
Venues are: Incheba Arena/Prague, Fléda/Brno and Plynojem/Ostrava; 12 Czech bands and other musicians
All three venues will finally be connected via live connection and a common interpretation of the hymn "Doomsday drops out" of the band The Tap Tap resounds.
The aim of the concert is to raise the funds for the equipment of a multi-functional disability-accessible educational center, which is to be created under the name STUDEO at the Jedlička Institute in Prague
Admission costs 400 CZK, children up to 12 years free of charge, tickets are available in advance at Bohemiaticket.
Poland and space.
Last week, at a Council of Ministers meeting of the member countries of the European Space Agency ESA, Poland was admitted as the twentiest member of this space agency. Poland is thus the second member country from the former Eastern Bloc (according to the Czech Republic, which became an ESA member on 12 November 2008).
Poland began a close collaboration with ESA in 1994 and took part in several ESA projects in the following years.
Poland's path to space, of course, began much earlier.
Even before the Second World War, Polish cosmos enthusiasts dealt with space travel, whereby they did not always meet with understanding.
For example, I am reminded here of A. Šternfeld's lecture of December 6, 1933 at the Warsaw Observatory, at which the author presented thoughts from his pioneering work "Entrance to Cosmonautics".
However, the ideas of the young engineer (born 1905) left the listeners cold, and after years Šternfeld still remembered that only Dr. Jan Gadomski had shown interest in his work.
Šternfeld won the Prix REP-Hirsch donated by Robert Esnault-Pelterie and André Louis Hirsch in 1934 for "Entrance to Cosmonautics".
Dr. Jan Gadomski (1899 -1966) later became an important advocate of astronomy and space travel.
In Polish journals, he published hundreds of articles and wrote several books on these scientific disciplines.
Gadomski’s globally recognized contribution to the development of space has been honoured, among other things, by naming one of the craters on the side of the moon facing the earth.
Already in 1925, a railway draisine was built in Poland, for which a rocket propulsion was planned.
Unfortunately, the author of the design and the details of this project remained unknown.
It is not even clear whether the rocket should be used to launch or brake the draisine.
The information comes exclusively from the contemporary press.
In 19393, the Polish infantry began to deal with rocket-powered projectiles.
The research work was carried out by Komórka Techniki Uzbrojenia in cooperation with Prof. Mieczyslaw Wolfke and Prof. Gustaw Mokrzyckiegi.
It is clear from the documented documents that the research had penetrated to the stage of practical tests.
Of course, the research was then interrupted by the invasion of the German Wehrmacht.
In 1937, in the trade press, the design of a photo-electric hunting rocket by Ing. Rohoziński appeared, and the following year the work "Rakieta - torpeda powietrzna i rakietobomba lotnicza" by the author Leliwa-Krzywoblocky appeared.
Both projects dealt with the military use of missile propulsion.
Shortly before the war, all projects for the military use of missile technology were coordinated by the Temporary Scientific Advisory Committee (Tymczasowy Komitet Doradczo-Naukowy).
The committee was set up in 1937, but after two years of activity, his work was terminated from the beginning of the war.
Further articles on space travel appeared in the Polish press after the war thanks to the work of the Polskie Towarzystwo Astronautyczne.
The first mention of this company takes place in 1954 in the November issue of the magazine Problemy, in which four extensive articles are devoted to space travel.
Prof. M. Subotowicz's article contains the proposal to establish a space society.
At the time there were already the first projects on satellite technology, and it was foreseeable that the exploration of space would have a future.
Already in its beginnings in 1956, the Polish Space Company PTA tried to join the International Federation for Space, which was founded in 1951, and in fact the PTA became a full member as early as the autumn of 1956.
The following year, Kazimierz Zarankiewicz (1902 - 1959) became Deputy Chairman of the International Federation of Spatinopia.
He held this office until his death in 1959.
Since 1956, PTA has been instrumental in the development of the extremely successful meteorological rocket (Rakieta Meteorologiczna), the first Polish rocket for scientific research.
The first type, the RM-1, was completed in 1957, the first launch took place on October 10, 1958.
The rocket with a maximum flight altitude of 1800 m was about 80 cm long and weighed not quite 5 kg.
Later, an improved version, the RM-1A, was built, and in 1959 flight tests began in the BłĂędów desert with the two-stage rocket RM-2.
The rocket was 1.4 m long and weighed approximately 11.5 kg.
A further stage of development should already be able to carry out real scientific work - the RM-34 rocket should reach an altitude of up to 14.5 km and be used for the observation of high-altitude winches.
In 1962, however, further development was discontinued.
Successor to the first missiles of the type RM was the Meteor 1 rocket developed between 1962 and 1965.
The rocket was designed as a two-stage rocket, its total length was 510 cm and the take-off weight was 32.5 kg.
Of this rocket, three types have been developed (Meteor-1A, -1B and -1C), which differ from each other in the size of the transport space for scientific instruments.
The Meteor 1A rocket had a 0.4-litre transport room with a capacity of 0.4 liters, Meteor-1B had 0.34 liters and Meteor-1C over 0.62 liters.
The maximum altitude for all three types was 37 km.
Between 1965 and 1968, the aviation institute developed the meteor 2 rocket, whose first flight tests began in October 1970.
Meteor-2 had a take-off weight of 380 kg and was able to carry a payload of 10 kg to an altitude of approximately 60 km.
After thain, the Meteor-2H and Meteor-3 versions were
It is also worth mentioning Poland’s accession to COSPAR (Committee for Space Research) in 1960, followed two years later by the establishment of a national COSPAR committee.
Poland also participated in space exploration with Soviet satellites as part of the Interkosmos programme, and in 1978 Polish pilot Miroslaw Hermaszewski became the second intercosmonaut after Czech Vladimír Remek.
Repealing the law on charitable work is not a solution.
Last week, the Constitutional Court overturned the law on charitable work
This decision provoked a lively discussion in public.
It is certainly interesting to look at the problem from a broader perspective.
The liberal-oriented economic systems in the EU and the globalized world are based on the principle of unregulated economic competition.
In the end, this means that the individual economies are in permanent conflict with each other.
The reason for this is the principle of free trade and the free, unrestricted movement of private capital, which goes hand in hand with rampant financial speculations.
Due to the great differences in the value of labour (wages), there are price tensions.
This term is understood to mean a condition in which the manufacturer seeks to exist in economic competition by importing favourable goods by "undercuting" the competition, in order thereby to seize a larger market share and to increase its profits.
On a large scale, this means for most companies to move their production abroad or to shop cheaply there, or to give up their business. The result is high unemployment in countries with comparatively high labor costs.
Since private capital bears no social responsibility and thus no responsibility for the unemployment caused by it, government spending necessarily rises.
This situation is reinforced by the fact that the entrepreneurs are absolutely unwilling to pay their taxes and thereby compensate for the economic and social damage caused by them.
This situation is generally known, so that concrete statistics are superfluous at this point.
The ruthless practice of private capital creates a situation in the individual economies in which governments are forced to compete with each other in order to artificially reduce the social level of their own populations and thus attract foreign capital into the country.
In other words, the governments deliver their own people to private capital without regard to the social consequences.
This is particularly reflected in amendments to applicable laws.
The aim of these measures is to force its own people to accept the dictation of private capital, especially in the wage sector.
On the one hand, this is done through economic compulsion with long-term unemployment, on the other hand through the circumcision of workers’ rights.
The result is growing poverty, as well as ever-increasing differences between rich and poor.
In Germany, there have been public food levies for several years for poor people who are no longer able to feed through their own work.
The number of those affected is already in the millions.
In the course of increasing the competitiveness of the German economy, there are regular cases in which employees receive such a low wage that the state has to increase its income up to a subsistence minimum.
Such a scandal has also been uncovered in the case of assistants employed in the German Bundestag.
Austerity measures of virtually all EU-Southern countries will undoubtedly amount to the same state in which, under the pressure of a catastrophic crash in their social levels, people are forced to emigrate (as was the case in the 19th century), or for a starvation wage on the margins of society, hoping that private investment capital will eventually flow into the country.
Here, of course, the question arises as to where this capital comes from?
If it comes from another EU country, only poverty migrates from one country to another; or the capital fails completely, because in China, India, Brazil, Turkey, Morocco or Egypt or in Africa at all, employees work for a fraction of European wages.
This applies throughout Latin America.
Liberal theories and media claim to the point of exhaustion that the state must not participate in its own economy with capital, and that planned economy leads to the economic catastrophe.
Private capital insists narrowly on its view that the state must not intervene steeringly in the economy.
But then one has to ask oneself whether private capital does not influence politics and thus the entire state in one’s own selfish sense.
Here's the unequivocal answer: Yes.
Evidence of this is the existence of a virtually almighty lobby ubiquitous in all states.
The result is a desperate situation in the realm of corruption, mutual preference, legislation in which virtually anything is punishable but nothing.
In Germany, the situation is such that, due to the lack of financy, private legal companies closely intertwined with the industry are tasked with drafting bills.
These bills are subsequently passed in the Bundestag.
All violence then no longer proceeds from the people, as proclaimed by the Western constitutions, but by strong financial groups that prefer their own interests.
Apparently, liberal democracies are rapidly getting into a stage, as Appian describes in his work on the crisis of the Roman Republic at the time of Caesar and Pompey: "The state has long been in disintegration, and the offices have been occupied by violence.
Against bribes, through unlawful sneaking of benefits or with stone and sword.
Corruption proliferated unrestrained, and the people went to vote with already bought votes" ... "People with character did not apply for offices, so that the state, as a result of this demise, had no consul for eight months" ... "You really started to talk about the fact that the only medicine in this difficult situation was a sole rule, and that a strong man had to be elected"...
As it turns out, today’s society, as well as in antiquity, is based on the ruthless preference of one’s own advantage without paying attention to overall social interests.
By its very nature, private capital is unable to recognize and enforce the advantage for the entire society.
The result, today as well as then, is an unprecedented decadence of elites who do not, in the slightest, strive for in-depth reforms.
We must therefore seek the cause of the emergence of fascism and communism in the ruthless liberalisation of economic systems in the 19th and 20th centuries.
Viewed from today’s state, the demise of these regimes in favour of liberal democracies can be regarded as a certain pause in which the next round is already looming.
Particularly catastrophic is the fact that today's elites are in complete ignorance with the possible losses of hundreds of thousands of human lives, the humanitarian and social catastrophes of which we are already witnessing today, as well as the crimes against humanity as we know them from older and more recent history.
Repealing the law on charitable work is not a solution here, at least.
Under pressure from global and European economic competition, the Czech Republic will be forced to continue to seek ways to push the social level of the population down.
This trend is therefore system-related.
The only solution is targeted political-social reforms, which increase the state’s participation in the economy, increase the influence of citizens on the government and weaken the monopoly position of private capital in society for the benefit of the state.
Israel: Laboratory of Chaos.
"...that nothing arises out of violence, or ever arises," sings Sting in the song Fragile, whose most important verse in chorus reads: "...so that we don't forget how fragile we are."
"If my sons didn't want a war, there wouldn't be one," says the driving Gutele Schnaper, wife of Mayer Amschel Rothschild, in 1849
After the last wave of violence between Israel and the Gaza Strip, as always, there was a myriad of reactions.
Some stand behind Israel and argue with its right to self-defense, while they refer to the Palestinians as terrorists; the others, in turn, support the Palestinians, argue with the racism of the Israeli state, with the genocide committed to the Palestinian Arabs, and refer to the state of Israel as terrorist.
At this point, I do not want to write about who, in these ever-recurring waves of killing, is more culpable and who is a victim; ultimately, the present-day inhabitants of Israel, including self-government, were born into the current political situation and have not witnessed the beginning of violence itself.
It’s about giving readers a look behind the scenes, looking at whom the 95-yea-old suspense (calculated from the Balfour declaration in November 1917) in the small patch of land in the Middle East are especially useful.
My thoughts are partly based on accessible historical facts, others are derived from them and emerge from my own understanding of who or which group of people significantly influences the events of modern history.
History of humanity is primarily a history of the struggle for power.
In any epoch, we can make an Alexander the Great or a Napoleon.
That which is less obvious is the question of whether it was really always these personalities themselves who determined their steps autonomously, or if behind their throne was someone who steered their deeds to a predetermined goal.
We must admit to ourselves that we live in a time when the wealth of the world is concentrated in the hands of fewer individuals, and that this concentration of money and the resulting power has not arisen within the lifespan of a generation.
Among these astronomically rich families, one stands out in particular, which one could call the head of the octopus (I don't know if there's still someone standing above them, but I don't rule this out) - the Rothschilds.
There's not much being written about them.
Understandingly.
The first press agency (Reuters) was already bought by the Rothschilds in the 90s of the 19th century in order to prevent the combination of their name with highly criminal deeds, in the background of which the consolidation of their power, the multiplication of their assets or both stood.
The Rothschilds are involved in almost all central banks in the world, and against the countries in which they are not involved in the central banks, they are waging wars or are gearing up to these (before the raid on Afghanistan there were 7 countries, after attacking Iraq there were 5 countries, after the overthrow of Ghaddafi only four remained, with Russia in the meantime being subordinating its central bank to the Russian government).
Whoever tried to oppose this family had to pay for it with life.
Abraham Lincoln declined to renew Rothschild's Bank of America's status as a central bank, began to issue its own banknotes during the civil war and was assassinated in a theater in 1865.
