Here is Wisdom! He who has understanding thinks about the number of the beast.
For it is a man's number, and its number is six and sixty-six."
The movie Argo: If it's the worst, call Hollywood
In November 1979, a mob of demonstrating Islamist students occupied the U.S. embassy in Tehran and took 52 diplomats hostage.
For their release, they demanded the extradition of the ousted Shah Mohammad Reza Pahlavi, who had fled to the U.S. after the revolution, who for decades had saved the patronage of his regime.
There was no positive way out of this situation for the American government - she could not afford to throw the Shah overboard, as this would have massively damaged the trust of its allies in other countries.
At the same time, she was unable to enforce the release of hostages in Iran, where the revolution led to the establishment of a theocratic regime.
It was a major blow to the reputation of the United States, and the subsequent fiasco in the forcible attempt at hostage liberation only made things worse.
The captured diplomats were finally released after 444 days after negotiations had been mediated by the Algerian government.
The fate of the hostages evoked a wave of solidarity and anti-Iranian moods at home.
Iran's debacle contributed significantly to Jimmy Carter's defeature against Ronald Reagan in the 1980 presidential election.
The film Argo by the director and actor Ben Affleck describes an episode of this story that gave the U.S. a small sub-victory.
Immediately before occupying the embassy, six embassy employees managed to escape.
This ended after numerous complications at the residence of the Canadian ambassador.
In cooperation with the Canadian authorities, the CIA managed to bring the refugees from Iran; in doing so, an extravagant story helped them to cover up their true identity - they traveled disguised with Canadian passports as members of a filming staff who allegedly visited locations in the country for a science fiction film.
Combination of genres
Developed by the expert on "exfiltration," Tony Mendez, the plan also necessared Hollywood's collaboration.
In order for the camouflage to become credible, articles about this film project appeared in trade journals, press conferences were held, and the fictitious production company had a real office.
The details of this operation have been kept secret for a long time. The film draws on Tony Mendez's memories.
Affleck's film is a special combination of several film genres.
On the one hand, he is a realistic political thriller, but at the same time also a so-called "Caper Movie", i.e. the story of a successful hussar piece, such as the film Ocean's Eleven.
As a result, the mood levels alternate in the film - on the one hand the documentary scenes from Tehran (in the title sequence, iconic contemporary photographs are shown, and there are no major differences in the film version of the same situations either).
On the other side, the loose passages from Hollywood, which come ironically and slightly exaggerated.
And then there are the scenes from the CIA headquarters and other institutions - men in suits at the meeting table, on the office hallways, on the phone...
Ben Affleck has achieved a remarkable reboot of his career with this film.
A ridiculed actor has become a respectable director, and even as an actor he is no longer the target of ironic remarks.
Argo is his third feature film after crimina Kein Kinderspiel (Gone Baby Gone, 2007) and the Thriller City without Grace (2010).
At the same time, it's Affleck's first film that doesn't set in the director's native Boston.
The flair for the atmosphere of the place was one of the features that distinguished this director's previous films from Standard Hollywood.
Affleck, however, proves this flair also in Argo, where Tehran is "played" by Canada.
The best scenes of the film take place in the streets and show the reconstruction of events that really took place - the initial sequence of storming of the message is very impressive in its clarity, and still conveys the feeling of confusion and unpredictability that occurs when the story moves somewhere.
Affleck and his team also have a similar effect in fictional scenes (the wrong film stick on a bazaar in Tehran).
Too much action in too many places
The director also had to deal with the fact that the story actually does not offer many exciting scenes in the cinematic sense.
However, he works quite successfully with the few, sometimes helping reality a bit - which then does not always seem so elegant (the scenes in which the looming crisis at the airport in Tehran is averted by a phone call in America, or even the chase on the runway, seem a bit contrived).
Argo's weakness is his all-too-broad scattering caused by the need to show too much action in too many places.
Alan Arkin and John Goodman are very charming in their roles as Hollywood employees, but their characters deserve more space, and that’s why they’re not alone in the film.
Affleck’s film loses a bit of traction. It’s a film that you can follow with a certain interest and gout the design and the retro style reminiscent of a thriller from the 70th year.
However, hevering enthusiasm is difficult.
However, as a reminder of a piece of history, which is still alive in a certain way to this day, and as proof of how exaggerated the notions of the omnipotent and all-knowing intelligence services are, he is sufficient.
Guidelines for inflating balloons, bananas and circus
The server www.bankovnipoplatky.com, which proclaims an annual survey of the most absurd bank fees, has decided this year to host the "Most Absurdest Regulation or Idea from the EU Workshop".
"This is the idea brought to us by the plan recently put forward by the EU to introduce a women's quota of 40% in the management of large European companies," Patrik Nacher, who takes care of organising this survey, told Právo newspaper.
Among the absurdities nominated, for example, is the judgment of the European Court of Justice on the unification of insurance rates for men and women.
Until then, women enjoyed an advantage in life insurance because they objectively pose a lower risk for insurance companies.
"Further unbelievable ideas from the EU workshop can be nominated until the end of this year.
The actual vote will then take place until the end of February 2013," Nacher said in a statement.
Controversial EU regulations include, among other things, the binding admixture of biothenol into fuels, which is subsequently harmful to natural nature, furthermore the prohibition of reliable mercury thermometers, simply because they contain a relatively small amount of this hazardous substance, or the directive on the size of chicken cages, because of which eggs became significantly more expensive this year.
Criticism in the past also reaped the factual prohibition of the use of the term "butter spread" or also the ban on the sale of conventional light bulbs.
Bananas must be 14 cm long
The EU apparatus often decides under the pressure of this or that lobby from trade and industry, whose demands are usually represented by one or more member countries in Brussels (for example, the Czech Republic is currently trying to enforce the demands of Czech banks even veto).
Lobbying interests were also behind the fact that bananas of the highest trade class in the EU must be at least 14 cm long and must not have a "curvature anomaly".
The European Commission defended itself by arguing that it harmonises only the non-uniform national standards that would impede trade.
Standards relating to fruit and vegetables have already been softened by the EU against the resistance of some countries by pointing out that the previous directives would lead to the waste of food.
A candidate for the victory in this survey may be last year's EU regulation, which according to balloons at the sale must be indicated that children under the age of eight are not allowed to inflate the balloon without the supervision of an adult.
The EU cited an American study, which according to balloons is the leading cause of child suffocation deaths.
A similar restriction already applies to children up to the age of 14 when using carnival rolls.
Curious incursions are also available at home
Relatively absurd is also a directive that affects European officials themselves - anyone who assumes an official function in the EU must not use the designation of Macedonia because of the sensitivity of the issue for Greece and must instead say FYROM (Former Yugoslav Republic of Macedonia).
In addition to the aforementioned absurdities, Bankovnípoplatky.com has also nominated the EU regulation on the scope of food stockpiles in the area of a member country.
The EU thus determined the maximum volume of food stockpiles, which were allowed to take place in the territory of the Czech Republic on the day of our accession to the EU.
The Czech Republic then exceeded, for example, the fixed volume for mushroom preserves, for which we were threatened with a hefty fine.
The organizers of the survey were also impressed by the idea that certain countries should receive money for not having a sea, or from the idea of giving funding for applications for funding.
Of course, these ideas did not come from Brussels, but from Prague.
"We're disadvantaged because we don't have a sea.
We will request reimbursement from the EU," then-Agriculture Minister Jaroslav Palas (ČSSD) said in autumn 2004.
As an argument, he cited that the grain harvest had been very high and the state camps were overcrowded as a result of the so-called intervention purchases.
Since the Czech Republic has another route to seaports, according to Palas, the EU should pay hundreds of millions of euros to us.
The European Commission finally met the Czech Republic so far that it organised a tendering for the purchase of cereals from countries that do not have access to the sea.
Funding for applications for funding was offered to the municipalities by the Ministry of Regional Development headed by Minister Pavel Němec (U.S. DEU), in concrete terms of funds for the setting up of funding applications to Brussels.
EU: Bizarre regulations are the exception
Regulations in the member Member States are easily becoming the target of criticism, but according to some experts the EU's efforts for regulation and thus the effectiveness and development of the entire European Union deserve recognition.
According to these experts, a more serious problem is the EU funding for projects that have absolutely nothing in common with deepening European integration, but which were enforced in the budget negotiations of individual member countries.
Emotions in this country also evokes the fact that, like other member countries in Brussels, the Czech Republic has to fight for the right to use specific designations of traditional products and does not always win this battle.
After a six-year battle with Germany and Austria, the Czech Republic was able to defend the designation "Olomoucké tvarůžky", the inlanderum (Tuzemský rum), whose tradition goes back to the 19th century, but our manufacturers had to rename "Tuzemák".
Rum may only be used for sugar cane distillate products, not sugar beet products.
In the official list of products registered in the EEU, in the company of the world-famous cheese varieties Feta and Gorgonzola, the Lübeck marzipan or the Parma ham, the Karlovy Vary Zwieback, carp from Pohořelice and Třeboň, or the Saazer hops now appear.
With an EU brand, the Pardubic gingerbread or the Hořice rolls can boast.
"People want me to save the republic, but I'm just an amateur," Okamura claims.
Senator, how does one come up with the idea of running for the office of president?
I'm not about being a senator or president.
If everything in our country would work without any problems, I wouldn't run anywhere.
But I can't see how the republic has been plundered for 20 years as thieves roam freely as people are raised taxes and retirement age.
I didn't have any ambitions to become politicians.
But when I see something I don't like, I try to propose a solution to change that.
And since I'm already over 40, and independent and non-partisan, I have no other way to influence things than run for the office of a senator or president.
You made it to the Senate and then immediately made your way to the castle.
Doesn't that swept the back on your constituents?
I've been telling myself all along that I'll start the battle for the castle after the result of the Senate elections.
Later, I clarified this and said that I would run for the office of the president, provided I am elected senator.
But my goal is not the function. The function is an instrument with which I can realize my visions.
For this, I need as much influence as possible and as strong a mandate as possible.
However, the difficulty is not only that we, as a people, scold in the pub and in front of the TV, but put down everyone who wants to change something.
In addition, there are the mediaries who want to rob you of honor and who confuse the freedom of the word to lie.
About me, for example, it was claimed that I would bribe journalists or that I was an adviser to Jiří Paroubek.
Let's talk about your visions.
You have taken up the fight for the castle with the thesis of the material and criminal liability of politicians as well as with retroactive asset declarations over 20 million crowns.
To do this, you need to change laws.
As president, you have no powers in this regard, and only the Senate as a whole can bring legislative proposals.
How do you want to solve this problem?
As an ordinary citizen, I have campaigned to ensure that the guide remains a free trade, and this could be enforced.
The problem is small-minded politics - if someone from the right or from the left comes with a good proposal, it is deliberately rejected, and that only stops the population.
As an independent non-partisan, I have a far greater potential to gain support from all parties represented in Parliament.
My advantage is that, without drawer thinking and party dogms from any party, I can take what is best for our country.
As a person in politics, do you see yourself more right-wing or on the left
From the Czech point of view it seems to me that people see me more on the left.
For me, however, this right-left thinking is completely unimportant.
It's important for me that we move forward.
It's not a question of whether someone belongs to the left or the right camp; I want to connect people.
I always publicly support every good solution, even if it comes from KSČM or the ODS, and I will turn against bad ideas.
You don't like it when someone calls you populists.
But don't confirm what you've just said?
If you draw up a business plan in a company, then you also have an ideal goal and a vision.
And that's what you'd like to approach.
This can be called populism because of me, but all the suggestions I’ve spoken of are already working in the world, or they have been discussed by important experts.
But without support in Parliament, you only have keywords.
And you don't get far with that in politics in the long term.
Or do you think that if you only talk to the people long enough, you will be able to enforce material and criminal liability, for example?
I don't have any other option.
Convincing politicians, journalists and the public and trying to bring them to our side in order to achieve these goals.
As president, it would not be a problem for me to call on the leaders of the parliamentary parties live on television to legally enshrine material and criminal liability for politicians, civil servants, judges and prosecutors.
And those addressed would then have to say, if necessary, why they reject this.
A strong personality pointing to grievances can put pressure on the political scene.
Only take once the direct election of the president, which has been a merit of public pressure.
I'm clearly saying I'm an amateur; I'm neither a genius nor an intellectual.
I'm trying to find allies for my views and visions.
With politics, I have only only just started and are trying to find the support of the majority for my programme.
I'm always trying to judge things myself; and if it doesn't work out, then I'm going back to the private sector in six years.
That sounds a bit, as if Okamura should save the Czech Republic.
I'm not a savior.
I know I don't achieve anything alone; that's why I've talked to several well-known personalities about whether they don't also want to run for the Senate.
For example, I spoke to Radim Jančura, who, however, declined due to overwork.
In addition, I supported the investigative journalist Jana Lorencová, who uncovered the scam in the trade in light heating oil.
My candidacy I started with the assumption that people are deeply unhappy, but now I'm not sure anymore.
Sixty percent of eligible voters did not go to elections at all, and those who left have mostly elected representatives of the establishment.
In the Senate, we're only two independents.
People have chosen such a Senate composition that changes will be very difficult to enforce.
Nevertheless, I will fight for my visions, e.g. for the direct election of the mayors and district governors.
Are you thinking about the creation of your own party?
I haven't thought about this yet, because I don't have time to check the pasture of every potential party member, and I don't even have the money for it.
I don't even have money for the presidential campaign yet; in my transparent account there are only 20,000 CZK.
You don't have any money?
You're talking about asset declarations, what'd yours look like?
My private assets I estimate at about 60 million CZK.
In Prague, I have a plot with a value of 25 million, an apartment for about 10 million, another for 8 million, an art collection that is worth about 10 million, as well as an Aston Martin for 3.5 million and a Škodu Superb that is worth about a million. And then I still have a few million in the account.
The Aston Martin, by the way, was a children's dream - James Bond I've always liked how he drove in the car, was gallanting to women and at the same time fighting evil.
You drive an Aston Martin, you have a fortune of 60 million and don't have any money for your campaign?
They say that you want to change the republic, but you don’t want to invest your own money in it.
That's not exactly convincing.
I don't have 15 million for the campaign.
Do you want me to pay debts?
I've already put 2.5 million into the campaign.
The fact that I don’t have any sponsors shows that my program doesn’t arouse too much interest.
And I'm not obligated to contest the campaign from my own resources.
The cost of the campaign is basically covered by my salary as a senator.
But I couldn't make a living from the senator's salary alone; for example, I couldn't finance my son the English school, which costs 30,000 a month.
If I just wanted to make money, I wouldn't run for anything.
So do you continue to work as an entrepreneur to get along?
Weren't you saying you're going to drive these activities back?
But that's not related to the amount of salary.
As promised, I partially scaled back my activities.
Thus, for example, my deputy in the spring becomes managing director of the travel agency.
People want to see me as Samaritan who saves the republic.
But I've got to live on something, too.
Which monthly income were you used to as an entrepreneur?
About 200,000 to 400,000, and it continues to be.
However, as president, I would completely cease my ventures.
You can find the full conversation in the Saturday edition of Právo.
The MVRDV architects prove that the real adventures are not only in mind - using the example of Spijkenisse and the recently built Bücherberg - 2 photos
"I find the building funny, it looks futuristic, and finally there's something interesting to see again," says Lisette Verhaig, a passer-by-roader.
And Stefan Spermon, IT technician in a nearby large company, says: "Yes, it's nice, the house."
But I'm wondering why we still need a library today.
All have internet, iPad and e-books.
No one still voluntarily goes to one of these old-style libraries today, right?
Spijkenisse, a sleeping town just in need of sightseeing at the gates of Rotterdam, is a strange record holder.
The 80,000-inhabitant community has the lowest education rate in the entire Netherlands.
In order to counteract this stupid circumstance, a few years ago they decided to contribute to general education and to recreate the seven fictional bridges depicted on the Euro bills as beautifully painted reinforced concrete miniatures.
The success of the educational offensive was limited.
And so the city fathers realized that there was only one way to cope with the statistics: a library had to come!
Winy Maas of the Rotterdam architectural firm MVRDV, master of the daring bar charts and producer of witty, yes often cynical buildings, took the thing with the usual serenity and appeared at the competition hearing in 2003 with five books under the arm and a grin in the face.
And while the jurgy was still looking around and shrugging with his armpits, the cheeky Meuse piled up his exquisite souvenirs of greatness to a pyramid and ended his actionist underpinned speech with the words: "Dear city community!"
So that's what he is, my suggestion for Spijkenisse's book mountain, for the so-called Boekenberg!
Nine years later, the 30-million-euro mountain is piled up.
It is part of a revitalization project, which also includes underground parking, supermarket, post office and a few adjoining residential and terraced houses with a total of 50 apartments.
At the beginning of November, the Bücherberg was awarded "Best Library of NL 2012".
In addition, the projecture is nominated for the Dutch National Wood Award
Thus, the faceless small-town retort, which until now had nothing more to offer than a post-modern pedestrian zone and a stunnedly ugly town hall, behind whose white facades one would be inclined to suspect a dairy factory to enrich a piece of contemporary architecture.
Above all, Spijkenisse now has the first public cultural building in the history of its existence.
The Long Road to the Book
The first impression: Büchereldorado under cheese bell.
Although there is a lift, which leads through the middle of the mountain massif, but the true joys of space and reading are revealed when climbing the topography on foot.
The all-round glazed interior is bright and clear, the burnt clinker floor and the elegant streetlights unequivocally speak the language of a public town square.
The urban atmosphere is perfect.
You are already on the lookout for park bench, dog and football-playing boys and girls.
Books, books and books everywhere.
"Usually the bookshelves stand along the façade, and in the middle lies a large, dark room that is mostly uncomfortable and impersonal," says Winy Maas.
We turned the classic room configuration upside down and turned the reading area from the inside out.
The interior of the Bücherberg is cleverly used: in the middle are offices, Internet library, chess club, environmental center and building technology headquarters.
A special joy are the black bookshelves, which are sometimes wall cladding, sometimes parapets, sometimes staircase railings.
Optics, haptics and smell are foreign.
Even die-hard architects and civil engineers shake their heads when it comes to unknown building material.
"We wanted to work here with recycled materials," explains Joop Trouborst, project manager at the municipality of Spijkenisse, at Standard's request.
And so, in a Frisian farm, we one day encountered a suitable waste product from agriculture.
For many years, a millimeter-thin plastic fleece has been used as a root base in greenhouses in the Netherlands and in fields.
That's cheap and time-efficient.
The thin textile lasts one or two seasons, after which it is ripe for bulky waste.
For the library, the fleece - for the first time in these quantities - was pressed into four centimeters thick slabs.
Under exposure to heat and pressure, the so-called Landbouwplastic (KLP) discolors into a dark, homogeneous, load-bearing material that smells a bit of a new car and a bit of a sports shoe.
After 105 steps, you have reached the summit.
At the end of the almost 500-meter-long hike, you will not only be rewarded in the Literaturcafé with a fantastic view of the city, but also with Dutch croquettes and potted ficus trees.
They provide atmosphere, but above all for the right humidity in the literary mountains.
Donations for the new soul
"You wouldn't believe it, but despite the many glass surfaces, this house is a showcase project in terms of ecology," says Trouborst.
It is heated and refrigerated with geothermal energy.
Even though the book mountain is under a loss of glass, the sun shines only briefly inside even on sunny days.
The wide wood glue binders, which stand transversely to the glass façade, serve as shading and intercept a large part of the sunlight.
The indoor climate is very pleasant.
The rest is done by fully automatic blinds.
Stefan Spermon, the initial skeptic from the IT industry, has already ventured into the new library.
Lisette Verhaig was already there.
And also the TCM teacher Cynthia Bogarde, who even describes the Boekenberg as Spijkenisse's "long overdue soul".
The reason: At the opening a few weeks ago, every resident was invited to donate a book from their personal holdings.
For the time being, this is intended to fill the optical gaps in the library, which has not yet been fully stocked - currently 70,000 pieces.
The concept has risen.
Shelves are filled all the way up.
"Nothing is worse than a half-empty library," says architect Winy Maas.
"I think thanks to our invitation, every resident now has some relationship with this new house.
Everyone knows that their book is part of this building.
And if it's only dedicated.
In this way, MVRDV has succeeded in the supreme discipline, which is called identity foundation in technical jargon.
Spijkenisse has written literary history.
As young and uneducated as she may be.
This is finally a starting point for identity.
Szabo: "Germans have to play a bigger role"
Germany abstained in the vote on the inclusion of Palestine.
According to Stephen Szabo, an expert on U.S.-European relations, Berlin is balancing on a narrow degree of diplomacy.
Deutsche Welle: Earlier this week, Germany had initially signaled that it would vote against the request for observer status of the Palestinians at the United Nations.
But then Berlin abstained.
Why? Why?
Stephen Szabo: Germany doesn't support what the Israelis did in Gaza.
However, due to its special relationship with Israel, Germany must now be cautious.
But at the same time, I don't think it's supporting the American position either.
Germany wanted to demonstrate its independence - but without being too critical of Israel.
During the uprising in Libya in March 2011, Germany also abstained when it came to setting up a no-fly zone.
This was eventually enforced by NATO.
Is it difficult for Germany to take a clear position on important international issues?
Yeah, that's right.
This is due to the fact that it is currently changing its foreign policy away from a policy that has been managed by the USA, so to speak, towards a German foreign policy.
Complicating matters further is that Europeans do not have coherent and uniform policies.
So the Germans find themselves between the fronts.
They are expected to play a more independent role, but they are not used to it.
I think they're still approaching their role, but they're on their way to a "more normal" foreign policy.
A foreign policy, as they have France or Britain.
What does a "normal" foreign policy mean specifically from a German point of view?
It shows the willingness to take positions on national interests that are independent of those of the USA or European partners.
I believe that German foreign policy is motivated by economic policy, i.e. by exports and its relations with certain regions, such as Russia, China or the Middle East.
Germany's economic interests are to a certain extent different from that of the other great powers and that's why Germany must protect its interests.
Have these economic interests had an influence on attitudes in the Middle East conflict and voting in the UN?
On the one hand, Germany has large sales markets in the Middle East, especially in the Gulf states.
That is why it must be careful not to confront the public, but also the elites, in the Arab countries.
That definitely plays a role.
But I wouldn't want to attach too much weight to that either, that's not a completely one-sided relationship.
But it definitely plays an important role in German considerations.
Has Germany damaged its relations with the United States by abstaining in important decisions, such as voting on Palestine?
I think there is great understanding of the German position in Europe and even in the United States.
That's why I don't think this was such a dramatic rupture as in the Libyan question.
Maybe Germany will even earn a certain respect for it.
Because it signals that it is taken seriously as international players and that its interests have to be considered.
There are different opinions on the Palestinian initiative in Europe.
The U.S., on the other hand, has clearly advocated veto.
Are there differences of opinion between the United States and many European nations?
Due to American domestic politics, these differences have always existed.
I believe that the administration under Obama secretly secretly has quite a lot of understanding of the European position.
But due to the political situation here, of course, the government cannot represent this position publicly.
I don't think the actual disagreements are as great as they always seem.
If you're looking at relations between Obama and Prime Minister Netanyahu, then Obama isn't that enthusiastic about Netanyahu's policies at all.
Is it difficult for Germany to reconcile its close relations with Israel and the USA on the one hand, and the position of the most important partners in the EU on the other?
I think that's what makes it so difficult for Germans.
It would, of course, be a bit easier for Germans if there were a coherent and unified European policy, which is not currently the case.
So they can't be part of a broader line and instead have to push things out of their own position.
That's what they're doing with the euro right now.
I believe that in the future Germany will play a leading role in pushing Europe into a European position.
Of course, this is not easy for Germany because of the relationship with Israel.
This has always been a sensitive issue.
But I believe that it is clear to the Germans that they have to play a more independent role.
Does Germany see themselves in the role as an important international player - does Germany even want to take on a leading role?
Or is Germany still having a hard role in leadership?
Germany isn’t used to it yet, this is still unpleasant and, for obvious reasons, it is still difficult for the country to play a bigger role.
If, for example, we look at the euro-crisis, a variety of anti-German sentiments are released every time when Germany assumes a greater role.
That doesn't make it easy for the Germans.
That's actually the old problem: you don't want to be surrounded by hostile countries.
From this point of view, Germany is in a much more difficult position than the USA.
It has to be receptive to various neighbors and opinions and that's not easy.
Now the influence of the U.S. in European politics is steadily decreasing, but the EU is not filling this vacuum at the moment, who else is staying there?
Germans will simply have to play a bigger role.
Even if they don't like it, they're awkward and make them much more unpopular - c'est la vie!
Stephen Szabo is deputy director of Transatlantic Academy in Washington, an institute where academics and policy experts from Europe and North America are jointly researching the challenges of the transatlantic community.
Szabo is also a member of the German Marshall Fund, where he specializes in German politics, U.S. foreign policy and transatlantic relations.
'Brand protection' in China: When Puma and Armani suddenly become Chinese
Armani is a world-renowned brand, Polo Ralph Lauren also.
But what is Armani Polo?
Behind it lies an officially registered name in China, which, however, has nothing to do with the originating companies.
Nevertheless, it enjoys protection as long as the actual namesakes do not complain.
And even then, it's not clear if they're right.
"It's becoming increasingly difficult for foreigners to protect their trademarks in China," says Thomas Pattloch, a lawyer at law firm Taylor Wessing, who specializes in intellectual property rights violations in the Far East.
Every week, a new case lands on my desk.
The imitators are already handed a few additional letters so that they are allowed to register their trademarks.
So Gucci simply becomes Lu-Gucci, instead of Prada you register Prada-Kny.
German companies are also legally copied in this way, for example the sporting goods manufacturer Puma.
Pattloch opens a folder with registrations of the trademark office in Beijing.
On September 14, 2010, a Chinese company had the Zegna DF Puma trademark protected there, an art name that was also used by the fashion house Ermenegildo Zegna.
It is well known that the Chinese are world champions in copying and injured intellectual property.
In the big cities there are multi-storey department stores selling almost exclusively counterfeits.
Pattloch’s cases, however, are different: On behalf of his clients, he argues that Chinese companies officially receive the right from the trademark office to use a designation that is already protected elsewhere.
The Chinese call this "Bang Mingpai," a passenger brand.
The word is based on "Bang Dakuan."
This refers to women who catch rich men.
The Chinese authorities are unaware of any guilt
"This is business-damaging, you have to fight it," Pattloch urges.
"The brand is diluted, the solutement disappears, the image damage is enormous."
The financial failures and litigation costs of the affected industries go into the millions, especially for expensive flagship products.
According to the market research company CLSA, with a volume of 15 billion euros a year, China is the third-largest market for luxury items and the fastest-growing.
But deletion of dubious entries in the brand register is difficult to achieve and goes into the money.
The procedure can last up to nine years, with an uncertain outcome.
Pattloch reports cases in which the court dismissed lawsuits because, after the long duration, the contested name had become the "market reality."
If the plaintiff is unlucky, he would even have to pay the plagiarist because he violated his trademark in China, says Pattloch.
Sometimes there is the law of the jungle here.
Famous cases also affect graphic elements.
In 2009, Daimler lost a legal dispute with the construction machinery manufacturer Sany, the company that recently took over the German concrete pump builder Putzmeister.
Therefore, the Chinese are still allowed to use an emblem similar to the Mercedes star.
Volvo buyer Geely originally used a blue-and-white logo reminiscent of BMW; the dispute was settled, Geely had to change it.
Fashion maker Lacoste lost in China to imitators from Hong Kong and Singapore, who let the famous crocodile look in the other direction.
The Chinese authorities are unaware of any guilt.
The CTMO brand office in Beijing admits that there were bottlenecks there in 2010 due to insufficient staff and room equipment.
Last year, however, one had returned from the "emergency situation in the working process to normality".
For examples, the existence of unprocessed opposition proceedings fell by 22 percent.
Nearly 57,000 such cases have been completed, 75 percent more than in the previous year.
Nevertheless, 81,500 objections are still awaitinging completion in the office.
Takinging against it costs a lot
As so often in China, the numbers are impressive.
Last year, CTMO received more than 1.4 million applications for brand protection, almost a third more than in 2010.
This is a record and means that for the tenth time in a row, China is leading the list of new registrations around the world, the authority said.
This also applies to the inventory of all valid trademarks, a total of 5.5 million pieces.
In 2011, 1.8 billion yuan had been collected in fees.
Simplified, this means that each registration costs an average of 1280 yuan or 160 euros.
Addressing against this costs a multiple, as the fall of the German family-owned company Freudenberg shows.
For more than seven years, the company has been battling a Chinese plagiarist.
Although the Germans were able to achieve that its illegal fabrication of imitated motor vehicle parts was excavated.
But the imitator continued to secure the Chinese rights to the Freudenberg brand.
One had missed this himself, because in Germany family names could not be protected, says Hanno Wentzler, the chairman of the management of Freudenberg Chemical Specialities in Munich.
The trademark office CTMO also rejected the objection of the people of Munich.
In the next two instances Freudenberg was right, but the opponent of the trial continues to fight until today.
You've got to be careful
Now the matter is pending before the Supreme Court.
Wentzler is confident to bring the matter to a good conclusion, and praises the professionalism of the courts.
But he also says: "The procedure is extremely elaborate and costs us a lot of time, money and nerves."
The internal costs could hardly be quantified that the company archive even had to look through one hundred-year-old files in order to provide evidence.
Five years ago Freudenberg offered the other side in vain a "high six-figure euro amount" for resolution.
"That's how much we're worth," Wentzler says.
The dangers in the Far East even threaten to slosh back to Europe.
This is when impersonators secure unprotected brands there.
For example, a Chinese manufacturer in Germany wanted to register Freudenberg for shoes and leather.
The Group has long since abandoned this field of business, yet it was able to avert its registration.
"You'll have to pay hellish attention," Wentzler says.
Both he and lawyer Pattloch advise German companies to be very cautious about dealings with China.
It is not enough to rely on international trademark rights, rather the foreigners "should register everything that is somehow worthy of protection, even in China," as Wentzler says.
Otherwise, this can become much more expensive than registration.
In fact: If Freudenberg is subject to his brand drama in the last act, you would probably have to pay royalties for your own name to the other side, explains Wentzler.
Or we are forced out of the market in the segment in question.
World Aid Day: Stomping, singing, helping
In Heidelberg the choir Imbongi rehearses - and in Swaziland Aid orphans are happy about it.
The story of a connection that overcomes far more than 8733 kilometers.
At first, there's pounding: cowboy boots, basketball shoes, women's pumps and men's slippers look for the beat on the parquet floor, find it quickly.
One-two-three-four.
Only then the voices of the singers slowly swerp - old, bass, tenor and soprano - harassing, beguiling and chasing each other.
And Fiete Hopf, the 29-year-old conductor, almost lifts it out of his cloth shoes when he brings them together with his supple wild gestures.
It is Monday evening, in the music room of the Institute for Medical Psychology in Heidelberg the choir Imbongi rehearses a new song.
About fifty singers have come, between 23 and 69 years old, from the human geneticist to the Hausmann.
"Om'Obani" is not a simple piece, each choral voice has a different text and this also in a very foreign language: Zulu, which is spoken by eleven million people in South Africa, Botswana, Lesotho, Malawi, Mozambique and in parts of Swaziland.
Self-help
There are around 34 million HIV-infected people around the world, Unaids estimates the United Nations programme to combat AIDS.
23.5 million of them live in the south of Africa.
There are 245000 Aidis orphans in Swasiland.
More than 40 percent of the population is now HIV-positive.
For 180 AIDS orphans in the village of Esitjeni, the association Voices for Africa has found godparents in Germany.
70 of them are attending a secondary school.
For 15 or 20 euros a month you can take over a sponsorship.
It guarantees the child's tuin fee, school uniform and a hot meal daily at the Gogo Center.
Imbongi on Zulu means storytellers or even praisers.
No one in this room can speak the Bantu language fluently, but sing.
For almost ten years, the chorus has been practicing songs in this foreign soft language and occasionally brings them back where they originally come from: to the south of Africa.
Because 8 733 kilometers airline from Heidelberg, in the northwest of the Kingdom of Swaziland, there is the village of Esitjeni, which depends on the voting power of the German choir.
Fortie percent are infected
About 2000 people live there, some still in simple clay and straw huts, a majority of which are children.
More than 300 of them no longer have parents because they have succumbed to the HIV virus.
Esitjeni shows on a small scale what the whole of Swaziland suffers from: the highest HIV infection rate, lowest life expectancy worldwide according to Unicef.
Circumcisions of the male foreskin, which demonstrably reduce the risk of contagion by half, are hardly accepted by the population.
More than forty percent of people in Swaziland carry the immunodeficiency virus in them, dying in their mid-thirties is not uncommon.
On a trip to Africa together in the spring of 2005, the choir discovered the village, but above all, the Imbongis saw many children on the streets who not only lacked parental care, but simply everything: food, clothes, education.
Without a school leaving certificate, there is no chance in a poor country.
Initially, it was the private commitment of individuals to send a child to school for a few euros a year and allow him a hot meal a day.
But already a year later, the choir founded the "Voices for Africa" association, which has taken care of the Aids orphans in Esitjeni almost professionally ever since.
Facts about sexually transmitted infections
Which are the most important sexually transmitted diseases?
Bacterial STI include syphilis, chlamydia and tripper (gonorrhea).
Frequent viral STIs include HIV, human pappiloma viruses, genital herpes or hepatitis.
Felt lice and scabies belong to the parasitic STI.
Who are the main stakeholders?
Syphilis and trippers occur mainly in men who have sex with men.
The Robert Koch Institute estimates that at least four out of five of all syphilis cases reported in Germany are transmitted through sexual contacts between men.
In heterosexual adults, chlamydia infections, trichomonades, candidosis (mushroom), trippers and human pappilomaviruses are common sexually transmitted diseases.
The spread of HIV is comparatively low among heterosexual adults in this country - yet: Around 20 percent of new HIV infections are accounted for by this group.
Chlamydia infections are much more common among adolescents than in other populations.
According to European surveys, three-quarters of all infections affect young people between the ages of 15 and 25.
In this country, humane pappiloma viruses are common in adolescents.
How has the number of infections developed?
Not all sexually transmitted diseases are reportable.
The number of syphilis infections has more than doubled from 1697 illnesses in 2001 to 3698 illnesses in 2011, according to the Robert Koch Institute.
The number of new HIV infections has been declining since 2007.
There were around 2700 cases in 2011.
That's about a tenth less than in the previous year.
What Symptoms Indicate Stem Disease?
The infectious diseases can cause ulcers in the genital area, discomfort during urination, discharge, lower abdominal pain as well as bubbles or warts.
Often, however, they do not cause pain or other symptoms and therefore go unnoticed.
How to protect yourself?
Condoms can reduce the risk of contagion, but don't provide one hundred percent protection.
In part, pathogens of sexually transmitted diseases can also be transmitted through smear infections and close bodily contact.
Therefore, experts recommend regular examinations, especially with frequently changing sex partners.
Diagnosed prematurely, most STIs can be cured and late consequences avoided.
Through sponsorships, donations and, last but not least, the fees that the chorus enjoins throughout Germany, a lot comes together.
"Overall, we've already sent around 200 000 euros to Esitjeni," says Annette Lennartz, the club's chairwoman.
In the village itself, Zodwa Dlamini, a self-confident and assertive woman, manages the funds from Germany.
She also takes care that the orphans accommodate well, with one of the grandmothers, for example.
The Gogos, as the ancient women on Zulu are called, are the pillars of the village.
Some of them have taken up to 14 orphans with them, give them a roof over their heads and make sure that the children in their uniforms appear on time for school lessons every day.
Those who no longer have anyone come to Khanyisile, a single woman who is paid just as much by the association as the two chefs, who cook every day for more than 200 hungry children.
In addition, "Voices for Africa" has launched a sewing school, built two chicken houses and organized together with the American health organization PSI that many in the village have an HIV test done.
This is not a matter of course, because as obvious as the disease is throughout the country, one prefers to remain silent.
King With 14 Wives
"Aids is an absolute taboo," says Annette Lennartz, "because it's related to sexuality."
It's actually strange in a country where the king officially has 14 wives.
The last absolute monarch of Black Africa, King Mswati III, is known for his excessive lifestyle.
polygamy rather than democracy.
Among other things, it is also due to this state-sanctioned form of life that the HI virus has spread so quickly in recent decades.
But also to the many migrant workers carrying the virus through the country.
Free condoms would exist on every corner, says Annette Lennartz, "but they're hardly used.
The culture is different - flesh-to-feather."
In order to promote cultural exchange, the Imbongi Choir travels together through the south of Africa every two or three years and sings songs full of melancholy, fighting spirit, confidence and black self-confidence, which many on the southern tip of the black continent still know from apartheid times.
A bus full of whites singing songs in a black language - so much recognition not only brings mood and joy, but has also defied a few tears for some grim border soldiers.
The journey always leads to Esitjeni, where the singers visit their godchildren.
Even if you can hardly find the small village on maps, in the valley of the Ezulweni River it is more than known.
"Go to Esitjeni, there's the light," the people there say.
And if you return the 8733 kilometre airline to Heidelberg, to the stomping singers in the rehearsal room on Bergheimer Straße, then you can also see it shining there.
Messenger: NASA discovers ice on Mercury
The Messenger probe has found evidence that there is ice on the planet.
The ice cover may be up to 20 meters thick.
The U.S. space agency NASA has proven the existence of ice on the planet Mercury.
Although closest to the sun, there is also frozen water there, according to three studies published on Thursday in the journal "Science".
The messenger probe has found evidence that there is an ice cover in the region of the planet, which is permanently located.
This should be at least 30 centimeters and possibly even up to 20 meters thick.
The water presumably came from comets or possibly asteroids smashed into Mercury.
No one associates the discovery of ice but with the existence of life on the planet, said the chef scientist of the messenger probe, Sean Solomon.
The temperature on Mercury can reach up to 426 degrees Celsius.
However, the finds could help clarify how water and other building blocks of life have reached other parts of the solar system.
Unnoticed by Earth citizens, probes, telescopes and small robots such as Phoenix are exploring the depths of the universe.
From time to time, they send images to Earth: small peepholes into infinite expanses.
The image comes from a camera developed by German researchers from the Max Planck Institute.
The eight planets of our solar system, as well as the dwarf planet Ceres.
Like Pluto, which orbits the sun behind Neptune, Ceres is not a "planet" according to the new definition of term of the International Astronomical Union of 2006.
This image excerption of an infrared image of the Spitzer telescope shows a "family portrait" of uncounted star generations: The oldest stars can be recognized as blue dots, more difficult to identify are the pink "newborns" in the star circle hall.
This star-forming region - unromantically called W5 by scientists - has discovered the telescopic spiker in the constellation Cassiopeia, at a distance of 6500 light-years.
This dazzling embers of a dying star has taken up NASA's Spitzer telescope.
The doughnut-shaped ring is made of material that the star hurled out during the dying process.
In the giant trifid nebula, 5400 light-years from Earth, new stars emerge from gas and dust.
NASA's Spitzer telescope shot this photo of the galactic birth room.
The Pleiade star heap, also called "The Seven Sisters," can be seened with the naked eye at night.
With the telescope, however, the colors come into their own better.
Like a red age, the Helix Nebular looks towards the viewer on this infrared photo.
It is 700 light-years away in the Aquarius stellar image.
Its resemblance to an earthly continent earned this nebula the title of North America.
A combination of normal and infrared photo provides the spectacular colour scheme.
This baby star could only be recorded in its full beauty by the infrared detectors of the Spitzer telescope.
Saturn and its rings: How these came about is one of the great mysteries of astronomy.
Possibly, they are the remains of a Saturn moon that disappeared without a trace 4.5 billion years ago.
One of the biggest and sharpest shots of the Hubble Telescope: The Whirlpool Galaxy
Depending on the colour scheme, shots of spiral galaxies become true works of art.
Released by the European Southern Observatory, the image shows the trifid nebula several thousand light-years away in the constellation Sagittarius.
The name Trifid comes from the Latin word trifidus (three-divided, three-divided), since dark strips of dust divide the glowing heart of the star birthplace.
In the constellation of snake carriers, astronomers have photographed the harbingers of a cosmic carambolage: 400 million light-years away from Earth, the nuclei of two merging galaxies race towards each other and will inevitably collide with each other.
This star birth was recorded by the Hubble Telescope in the spiral galaxy M83.
If you don't have technical abbreviations, you may call them by their nickname Southern Fire Wheel.
The photo of the Hubble Space Telescope shows an excerpt from the Iris nebula in the constellation Kepheus.
The nebula, 1400 light-years away, consists of dust granules that are ten to a hundred times smaller than ordinary house dust.
This image has been assembled from optical and X-ray images of different telescopes.
It shows a ring of black holes, 430 million light-years from Earth.
This group of galaxies, called Arp 273, recorded the Hubble space telescope for NASA.
Scientist UGC 1910 calls the greater spiral galaxy.
In this starry nebula is the brightest group of young stars of our Milky Way.
The Sternenwiege is always producing new youngsters.
Also, this star cloud, combined with the rosette nebula, constantly produces new baby stars - 5000 light-years away from Earth.
In this bright galaxy with a small black hole there is still no dust, but only gas.
Researchers suspect that it didn’t form until shortly after the Big Bang, when mainly hydrogen was found in the universe.
Our insights into space: The most important telescopes
The telescope is said to have been invented by Hans Lipperhey in 1608 - even before Galileo Galilei used it for stargazing a year later.
Since then, the mirrors of the optical telescopes have become ever larger - and the insights they provide ever deeper.
For 30 years, from 1947 to 1975, the Hale telescope was the largest telescope in the Palomar Observatory near San Diego.
The mirror, here in the picture, had an average of five meters.
The Large Binocular Telescope is located in Arizona.
It allows views into space over two mirrors, each with a diameter of 8.4 meters.
The inner workings of Gran Telescopio Canaria on the Canary Island of La Palma are huge - the mirror alone comes to 10.4 meters in diameter.
The Southern African Large Telescope mirror in South Africa is segmented - to save costs.
Nevertheless, it reaches a diameter of about eleven meters.
Disadvantage of favorable construction: The telescope is lashed at its height angle - and is thus limited in its mobility.
The Hobby-Eberly telescope in Texas is also fixed at an altitude angle.
Its peculiarity: the high light collecting force.
Despite a comparatively small mirror diameter, this extends to that of the world's largest mirror telescopes.
With the help of a radio telescope in Arecibo (Puerto Rico), researchers listen to space for extraterrestrial signals.
The radio telescope has a diameter of 305 meters.
"Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence" (SETI) allows any computer owner to provide computing power.
View of the observatory of the European Observatory (ESO) in the Chilean Andes.
Here's the Very Large Telescope - which lives up to its name.
With a total of four mirrors, the telescope can also illuminate the medium infrared spectrum.
European Extremely Large Telescope is also planned at the ESO Observatory in Chile.
Its main mirror is said to comprise a whopping 42 metres - and composed of just under 1000 mirror elements.
However, pictures are not expected until 2018 at the earliest.
Until 2007, the two Keck telescopes on the Hawaiian volcano Mauna Kea were considered the largest in the world.
They offer two mirrors, each with an average of ten meters.
The Keck telescopes are part of the Mauna-Kea observatory, which looks into the sky next to them with the Subaru Telescope and the IRTTF.
Also on the Mauna Kea, a new, huge telescope is to be created - with a mirror average of thirty meters.
Here you can marvel at an illustration.
However, the most important impressions from space are provided by the Hubble space telescope.
Since April 24, 1990, it has been supplying images of distant worlds.
Since March 2009, the Kepler space telescope has been searching for extra-solar planets - primarily those that are habitable.
On February 2, 2011, NASA announced that 1235 planetary candidates had been identified since mission began.
In the picture, the last launch preparations are documented at the Kepler space telescope.
The James Webb Space Telescope (JWST) will be launched into space in 2018 at the earliest.
The primary mirror of the space infrared telescope has a diameter of 6.5 meters.
One of the telescope’s tasks is to look for light from the first stars and galaxies after the Big Bang.
Scientists assume that ice also exists at the south pole of Mercury.
However, there is no reliable data for this, as the messengers circulates far closer to the north pole around the planet.
Radar measurements for decades have indicated that there is ice on Mercury.
Due to the messenger probe launched in 2004 - the first that revolves around Mercury - the scientists now have certainty.
Drink butter every day - and turn 168
In the sounth of Azerbaijan, many people reach a biblically old age.
There's even a museum for longevity.
A search for traces in the country where 97-year-olds are still comparatively young.
In the sounth of Azerbaijan, many people reach a biblically old age.
There's even a museum for longevity.
A search for traces in the country where 97-year-olds are still comparatively young.
The journey through the Talysh Mountains can be called wildly romantic.
The minibus rumbles across the winding road, past densely wooded hills, raging rivers and simple farmhouses.
Everything is green and juicy, you might think you're in the Black Forest.
But this is the deepest south of Azerbaijan, the border with Iran is only a few kilometers away.
Here lives the Caucasian ethnic group of the Talysh, of which not much is known, exparenced that it speaks perfectly Persian as well as Azeric and is very durable.
Terminus is Lerik.
The small town struts with too powerful architecture from Soviet times, which does not want to fit into the picturesque mountain landscape.
Tourists from Europe hardly come here, too arduous is the approach of Azerbaijan's capital Baku.
For the 323 kilometers you need eight hours, because the road is only one-lane.
The fabled wealth that the country owes to its oil in the Caspian Sea has not yet arrived here in the province.
But Pilata Fatulayeva (48) is convinced that Lerik has what it takes to attract tourists.
"Baku became famous with the Eurovision song festival in May - and next year we have a festival of the world's oldest people here," Fatulayeva says.
She is the director of the museum of longevity, arguably the only one in the
Here, the Vita of about eight dozen Talysches from the area is documented, the older than 100. Fatulayeva points to a black-and-white photo.
That's my grandfather, he's 120 years old.
At the age of 136, another child conceived
But controversial star of the museum is the shepherd Şirali Müslümov, who is said to have turned 168 years old.
However, there is no birth certificate.
And in view of the oldest documented person, who turned 122 years old, Müslümov's age seems extremely doubtful.
"In 1805 he was born here in the region, he died in 1973," Fatulayeva explains.
The man was married three times and had 23 children, at the age of 136 he is said to have begotten another daughter.
So did Şirali Müslümov miscalculate by a few decades at his age?
Rembrandt Scholz, an age researcher at the Max Planck Institute in Rostock, has also heard of the elderly in Central Asia.
"Strikingly many high-aged people also exist in some areas of China, in Japan or in the Hunza Valley in Pakistan," says Scholz, "even in Sardinia live extremely many very old men."
Due to a lack of documents, however, there was no scientific evidence of age, especially since there was a lack of civil status registers.
Every daily melted butter
However, the fact is that the people of the region around Lerik often reach a biblical age.
There are currently 20 people older than 100 years.
So why are there so many elderly people living here in the south?
The Azerian travel guide Farid Mugimzadeh explains this with the special genes of the Talysh.
Museum director Fatulayeva, on the other hand, believes that it is due to nutrition.
But that the high-calorie diet of the Talyshes is supposed to be so healthy, the meat, bread and especially dairy products and of which many drink a glass of melted butter every day, does not seem plausible from a nutritional point of view.
Or is it the original way of life that keeps people young? In Cengemiran, a tiny settlement not far from the town of Lerik, Rubaba Mirzayeva resides.
At the age of 97, she is still comparatively young for these latitudes.
Mirzayeva, who claims to have 143 descendants, lives in a simple wooden house that is typical of the entire Caucasus region.
She sits on the floor with a butter keg, which she tirelessly rolls back and forth.
Eight people live under one roof here, including one of Mirzayeva's sons and a daughter, both of whom have long been grandparents.
Two toddlers are also jumping around.
In the kitchen, guests are prepared tea, which is served in the typical, belly-shaped Armadu glasses.
Mirzayeva's white teeth stand perfectly in row and link, under her headscarf she hides long, dark blonde braids that her son proudly reveals in front of us.
I've always washed my hair with milk, so they've never run out and kept its color.
"Shampoo I've never used either," Mirzayeva says.
Monthly pension is enough for life
She's only eaten what came from her own farm, tomatoes, potatoes, peas.
All my life, I haven't bought food in the supermarket once.
Then she talks about her husband, who was in the army.
It was the worst in the period after the Second World War.
But everything got better when the "beloved father" Heydar Aliyew took the helm.
The propaganda sounds strange from an old woman's mouth.
Yet the cult driven around the nation's father figure, who ruled his country like a dictator, barely knows borders in Azerbaijan.
He was in power until 2003, and his son Ilham later took the helm.
After all, there is no plight among Azerbaijan's ancients.
230 manat (about the same sum in euros), Mirzayeva receives monthly as a pension, for local ratios an amount from which you can live well.
And perhaps Mirzayeva's long-grey son is right: "The elderly enjoy respect in our culture."
They live in the middle of the extended family, are loved, cared for and are happy.
If that's no reason to stay alive for as long as possible
The word "human right" is missing from the constitution
The revolution has returned to Cairo.
Rival rallies in Cairo reveal the country's deep division.
The future constitution with Shariah as a base is fiercely disputed.
The Egyptian president didn't save with pathos.
We've got to make the transition.
"And that he succeeds is my responsibility, in front of the people and before God," he said on state television.
His speech was addressed to the whole people, but above all to the Christian Copts, the liberals, enlightened Muslims and the secular.
Because they are all, hitherto hopelessly quarreled in a confused opposition, are afraid.
Fearful of a divine state on the Nile by graces of the powerful Muslim Brotherhood.
He had temporarily curtailed the competences of the Constitutional Court and expanded his competences "to save the revolution."
But what the 61-year-old engineer, who holds a doctorate from the American University of Southern California, really wants to saveve, the Egyptians are, the world is not quite clear.
Should the judiciary be disempowered?
In fact, the 234 articles whipped through by the Islamist-dominated 100-strong Constituent Assembly in a nocturnal, 15-hour parforce ride are partly worrying.
As in previous constitutions, the draft states that the jurisprudence is based on "principles of Islamic law".
But what does "principles" mean?
This was and is a matter of interpretation and it is to be feared that the Islamists will use the vague formulation and the accompanying legal scope for a stricter interpretation of Shariah.
At least one new article suggests: Al-Ashar University, the country’s most important Islamic institution with great radiance, must be consulted in all matters relating to Shariary.
This does not have to happen, but it may mean that the clergy supervises the legislation, which de facto would result in disenfranchisement of the judiciaries.
Many things in the draft constitution are a matter of interpretation
Also problematic: The civilian military jurisdiction should continue to be maintained.
These tribunals, during Mubarak's rule, served to suppress opposition activists.
After the dictator's overthrown, up to 11,000 civilians were in military custody.
According to the draft, the state should also protect "the true essence of the Egyptian family" "and promote their morality and values."
This is legally so blurred that with the help of this article, state institutions could even control the contents of film art and literature.
In plain language, this means nothing more than censorship.
By the way, no article explicitly stipulates equality between men and women.
For this, another forbids insulting or slandering the Prophet Muhammad and his envoys.
But what an insult is and how it should be punished remains unclear.
Equally dubious, the wording that the "insultation of people" is prohibited.
Is there already a caricature of the president or a joke at the expense of a legal scholar?
A matter of interpretation, like so much in the draft, the Morsi for signature and according to his words "very soon" will be presented to the Egyptians for the referendum.
"The revolution has returned"
The opposition has been campaigning against the superiority of Islamists for weeks.
Tens of thousands rallied in unfamiliar Eintrach on Friday night in Cairo's Tahrir Square and vowed to bring down the charter even before it can come into effect.
"The revolution has returned and we will win," said Hamdin Sabbahi, a third-place finisher in the presidential election.
Nobel Peace Laureate and ex-head of the International Atomic Energy Agency Mohamed El-Baradei declared that the draft constitution belonged "to the garbage mountain of history."
Via short message service Twitter, he accused Morsi's supporters of wanting to lead a "coup against democracy."
"If he proclaims the referendum, we'll go to his palast and overthrow him," said opposition leader Jasser Said.
"We aren't tired yet, the blood of our brothers isn't atoned yet," Egyptian media quoted opposition politician Chaled Ali as saying.
And several judges announced that they did not want to supervise the referendum, which would invalidate it.
“The Qur’an is our Constitution”
The well-organized Muslim Brotherhood, in turn, blewed to counter-demonstration, as a precaution but not in Tahrir Square, but after a mass prayer on the other ŁâíâíĨí outside Cairo University.
Many veiled women and supporters of the Salafists took part and shouted: "The people demand the application of God's law."
They demanded from Morsi: "Cleanse the land!" and said, "The Quran is our constitution."
A struggle for sovereignty over the emblematic Tahrir Square, in which everything began, would probably have conjured up civil war-like conditions.
Morsi's supporters obviously didn't want to risk that.
The Muslim Brotherhoors stated that both the opponents of the draft constitution and the proponents had spoked loudly and clearly.
Now is the time to let the people at the ballot box decide in which direction the country should go.
A majority in favour of the Islamists' design is considered safe.
"The word human rights doesn't even occur"
Hafez Abu Saeda is angry about this forced constitutional process, which should actually have gone until February and involve all social forces.
The 48-year-old human rights lawyer and chairwoman of the Egyptian Organization for Human Rights (EOHR) defended the Muslim Brotherhood as they were jailed under Mubarak or were on trial.
It is not because he would share their worldview, but because human rights are indivisible for him.
For this, he was beaten, convicted, imprisoned.
"And now the word human rights isn't even in the new constitution," he laments in conversation with the "Welt am Sonntag."
Morsi's expansion of power to all three forces in the state had the lawyer resigned.
These measures are blatant violations of democratic rules of the game and will lead Egypt into a new dictatorship.
"Instead of strengthening civil society, the president de facto overrides them," Saeda laments.
But without civil society organizations, a democracy cannot function.
Saeda feels left alone, also by the international community, which looks at the ideological struggle on the Nile with a mixture of curiosity and tension.
That could take revenge.
A protester on the Tahrir warns: "You're riding a monster that you can't control anymore."
Rakfisk from Norway: Is this the world's foult-smelling fish?
Norway's five-million residents enjoy one of the highest standards of lives - not only in Europe, but around the world.
Could the country's secret of success have anything to do with the local appetite for rather foul-smelling fish?
Take a selection of overripe cheeses.
You place them in the midst of a pile of dirty, wet sportswear.
And leave them there for a week.
That’s how you have to think of Rakfisk, one of Norway’s great delicacies.
I am located in the small town of Fagernes, about three hours from Oslo.
There's snow, spectacular landscapes here - and that stubborn smell in the air that just doesn't want to disappear.
Rakfisk is trout sprinkled with salt, which - depending on how smelly you would like your fish - is fermented into water for up to a year.
As the darkness comes across the country in Norway and the weather gets colder, Norwegians flock to a festival here in Fagernes, which is dedicated to this, now, really very captivating specialty.
"You eat it raw and then rinse it down with a glass of aquavit," says Havard Halvarsen, who actually works at the local fire station, but as a so-called "Rakfisk general" also hosts the festival.
Around us, people consume small fish cubes and consume large amounts of alcohol.
"Some people like the aquavit more than the rakfisk," Havard says.
Spirituose can displace the smell.
I'll cost some appetizers.
If one avoids bringing the Rakfisk close to the nose, it's not bad - like sushi, which has a quite long bus ride behind it.
Rakfisk is the product of a completely different, much poorer Norwegian era, when there were no cooling options and fish was soaked in airtight saltwater barrels in autumn.
In the deepest winter he was then taken out after a long fermentation and - undoubtedly under senses subdued with the help of alcohol - consumed.
Even a generation ago, thousands of Norwegians were forced in search of work to leave their land and mainly emigrated to the United States.
Today, the population is growing rapidly - more than 13% are impedial, attracted by a thriving labor market, high wages and comprehensive healthcare.
Even from Sweden, the old rival who was far richer than Norway not long ago, immigrants flock.
Rakfisk is seen as something important - as an essential, albeit strictly-smelling, part of Norwegian history.
He's one of the more expensive dishes you can buy here.
But actually everything here is expensive - a small glass of beer or a sandwich costs a proud 10 euros.
Norway doesn't often make it into the world press, and most people don't seem to mind.
Here one still hates to call Anders Breivik by name - the racist right-wing extremist who shooted 77 men, women and children last year.
Instead, one refers only to the "July 22 incident."
Norwegians find it difficult to accept that someone in their peace-loving country is capable of such brutal murders.
Since the early 1970s, one of the world’s largest oil and gas industries has been primarily responsible for Norway’s current wealth.
"But oil isn't the only reason why we're doing so well," says Anna, our waitress, who distributes round trays with ripe rakfisk and embodies the Nordic ideal with her long blonde hair and pungent blue eyes.
We're, as they say, prudent people.
Just like that of most people here, their English is flawless.
We'ren't very speaky, we don't like bragging.
Norway has handled its oil wealth very cautiously - a large part of the industry's earnings are invested in a special fund for the benefit of future generations.
When in the years leading up to the world economic crisis, everyone was throwing money that they didn't own at all, Norway kept its purse tightly closed.
"As long as we can ski in the winter and go hiking in the summer, we are happy," Anna says.
"And, of course, Rakfisk eat," she adds with light-hearted laughter.
I'm standing in the snow, lining up for another dishot - I've had enough of Rakfisk.
There's definitely something else like a moose burger, the juicy meat mouths me.
But in the evening, the stink fish is on the menu again.
The hotel where I stay is one of several venues organizing a rakfisk dinner where guests can tune for the best - or perhaps the sense of smell most challenging - fish.
There's a live TV shift to a bow fly-clogged showmaster surrounded by plates of rakfisk.
The spectacle is similar to the Eurovision Song Contest.
"What score do you have up there in the mountains for the best fish, Thor-Jürgen?"
"Here's our points, Havard."
You clap and laugh.
A man falls from his chair, possibly overwhelmed by the aquavit.
But maybe it's the strict haze of all the fish after all.
Mexico's Enrique Pena Nieto faces difficult start
As Mexico's future President Enrique Pena Nieto prepares to take office, Will Grant of the BBC analyzes the challenges imminent to him and the mixed feelings of his people.
Currently, the traffic in Mexico City is particularly bad.
Even on better days, this is an ever-clogged city, but on Monday a ring of steel was erected, which cordons off several important traffic routes into the capital and causes chaos on the streets.
The goal, however, was not to disrupt commuters on their way to work, but preventing protesters from reaching parliament.
On Saturday, Mexico's new president Enrique Pena Nieto will receive the presidential bandage and take over the nation's leadership.
He faces a complex task.
Under the outgoing Felipe Calderon government, Mexico has proven itself economically well, but the country is under the control of a drug war that has already cost 60,000 lives over the past six years.
"My administration is heavily committed to reducing violence," Pena Nieto explained to U.S. President Barack Obama earlier this week in the Oval Office.
I'm going to propose a new security strategy that allows us to achieve that goal.
Before going on an equal footing with the U.S. president, Pena Nieto served as governor of his home state of Mexico.
In this densely populated, sprawling part of the country that surrounds the capital, opinions about the new head of state are divided.
A direct man
In the remote small town of Valle del Bravo, for example, one likes to remember it.
Residents of the resort attribute the development of tourism and the construction of the infrastructure to him.
To reach the city, you can drive on one of the highways that Pena Nieto had built and which represent a great improvement compared to the brittle, bumpy roads that once existed here.
Plaques with its name also hang outside a modern sports center and an impressive interactive museum on climate change.
"We expect real and lasting changes from him," according to his friend and political ally Gabriel Olvera Hernandez, who works as a congressman for Pena Nieto's party.
In particular, in terms of security and the economy, we hope for an interesting and real change that our country so desperately needs.
After 81 years in power, the PRI was superseded by Vicente Fox in 2000.
Congressman Olvera admits that after 12 years outside Los Pino's presidential palace, the party places high expectations on Enrique Pena Nieto.
He rejects accusations by the opposition that the new president lacks substance.
He is a very direct man, very committed and has big plans for the country.
He is an outstanding statesman, but above all he is also someone who can listen.
But on the other side of the state, not many people share this view about their former governor.
In Nezahualcoyotl, also known as Ciudad Neza, the contrast to the paved streets of Valle del Bravo could not be greater.
The place is hidden under highway overpasses and in many ways represents a suburb of Mexico City itself.
And the problems in the community are crucially urban in nature.
Earlier this year, the military was turned on to combat drug gangs operating in the neighborhoods, and violence against women is a particularly acute problem.
In the last two years alone, the bodies of dozens of murdered women have been left behind on a patch of wasteland near a large landfill.
During Governor Pena Nieto's tenure, more than 1,000 women were murdered in the state of Mexico - a statistic that surpasses even that of Ciudad Juarez, which is notorious for its violence and the murders of innocent women.
Critics Pena Nietos are of the opinion that he has at least not adequately tackled the problem of women's murders during his tenure.
But in the worst-case scenario, his government has closed its eyes to the problem.
In a concrete building, typical of the run-down neighborhood, Irinea Buendia struggles with tears as she shows me photos of her late daughter Mariana Luna.
According to the official version of the events, Mariana committed suicide in 2010.
But her family believes that she was murdered by her significant other.
"When I arrived at her home, it seemed like her body had been washed," recalls Senora Buendia.
There were signs that she was beaten, and the corpse rigidity had already deployed.
As her mother tells her story, from the wall a picture of Mariana looks down at us. It is attached next to a cross that carries a single word: justice.
But these, according to the family, have been denied until now.
The authorities treated me as if I was a chatter, troublemaker, nairy woman.
They want their verdict to simply be accepted and their mouth shut.
"But that's not possible if there were so many formal errors and omissions," she says.
When President Pena Nieto receives the presidential bandage on Saturday, he also assumes a great responsibility.
Tens of thousands of Mexican families have been affected by violent crimes over the past six years, and the new president has pledged to take special care of this issue during his tenure.
"I hope as president he's the way he was as governor," says PRI congressman Olvera in Valle del Bravo.
But that's exactly what the families of victims in Ciudad Neza are most afraid of.
Prosecutors assert: Bradley Manning didn't complain about mistreatment
Prosecutors object to Bradley Manning's accusations that he was abused in prison
The hearing deals with Manning's time at the military prison in Quantico, Virginia
The defense is seeking a rejection of the indictment due to harsh prison conditions
The U.S. army is accused of stealing thousands of confidential documents
Prosecutors on Friday tried to establish that Army-free Bradley Manning - charged for the largest disclosure of Confidential information in U.S. history - failed several occasions to complain about the mistreat he allegedly suffered in military custody.
During a cross interrogation of Manning during a pre-trial hearing in Ft. Meade, Maryland, Maj. Ashden Fein explained that documents on weekly visits made during Manning's nine-month detention period in Quantico, Virginia had no complaints whatsoever about his treatment.
A day before the cross-examination, which was held as a result of a request by the defense to dismiss the indictment - Manning had already been sufficiently punished by the hard prison term - Manning had testified that he had considered suicide in prison.
U.S. Army intelligence analyst, who was arrested in June 2010, is accused of stealing thousands of confidential documents during his service in Iraq.
The material was then published by WikiLeaks on the Internet.
WikiLeaks has never confirmed Manning as the source of the information.
In Friday's hearing, Fein went through with Manning through the forms that officials had filled out after their meetings with Manning during his detention at Quantico military prison, where he had been seated under heightened prison conditions from July 2010 to April 2011.
Officers asked Manning questions and wrote down his answers.
When Fein asked him about the forms on Friday, Manning admitted that he had judged treatment by his guards to be "excellent" and treatment by the facility as "very professional" overall.
The forms, according to Fein, contained no complaints of ill-treatment, even though officers had questioned Manning directly about his treatment.
Manning replied that he had expressed his concerns orally and that the officials had discussed them with him and indicated to deal with them, but they had not been recorded.
"They wrote "no problems" (after discussing the concerns), and that didn't necessarily mean I didn't say anything at all," Manning said.
The judge, Colonel Denise Lind, also asked Manning why he didn't complain about his treatment when he met in January 2011 with a committee that was supposed to examine the suicidal thoughts he mentioned on a form a few months earlier.
Manning replied that his intention during this meeting was to downgrade his observational status to "prevention of injuries."
The military testified that it had assigned him this restrictive status - the one stage under the risk of suicide - for his and the protection of others.
"I wanted to convince employees that I was doing well and get rid of the observational status for the prevention of injuries (...) in order to be able to achieve a higher quality of life in my view," Manning said.
Manning testified Thursday about his arrest and relocation to Kuwait, where he was held for nearly two months before being transferred to the Quantico naval base prison in Virginia in July 2010.
He said he had contemplated suicide in Kuwait and had once fallen unconscious due to the heat.
Not being allowed to know what was happening to him or in the outside world had been incriminating.
"My world shrank to Camp Arafjon, onto this cage together," Manning said Thursday.
I thought I'd die in this cage.
When he was transferred to Quantico, Manning had spent most of his days in a small cell - at least 21 hours and often more than 23 hours - and without society.
He was only allowed a mattress, a blanket, flip-flops, clothes and his glasses.
Manning said he had tried to keep moving, because during the day it was forbidden to sleep or even lie down.
He always had to sleep with light, which penetrated from the outside into his cell.
If he had turned around at night and guards could no longer see his face, they woke him up so that he would turn back again.
In January 2011, Manning's lawyer formally objected to Manning's treatment.
Manning was transferred to Fort Leavenworth's military prison, Kansas, in April 2011.
On Friday, the judge Manning also questioned about an allegation he had made in his testimony on Thursday - so he had to sleep one night naked in his cell in Quantico and stand naked in front of guards and other prisoners at a morning count.
Manning had testified that he had never had the opportunity to envelop himself with his blanket during this count.
When questioned on Friday, Manning stated that he had concluded from an order from his guard that he should have dropped the ceiling with which he could cover. However, he admitted that no one had urged him to do so.
On Thursday, Manning had testified that he was forced to sleep naked the previous night. He had previously tried to show an officer that he posed no danger to himself.
Manning had told the officer that he could have used the waistband of his underpants or his flip-flops to hurt himself, but did not.
That night, Manning testified, his underpants, flip-flops and glasses had been removed from his cell.
His lawyers hope that the judge will at least take into account his sentencing experiences and drastically reduce his level of imprisonment should he be convicted at his trial in the military tribunal, which is scheduled for early next year.
The defense has indicated that Manning will plead guilty to minor offenses, while other charges that are considered too extreme are to be contested.
The continuation of the hearing is scheduled for this weekend. A justification of prison conditions is expected from the prosecutor's office.
The Pentagon sticks to its position that Manning has been helded in accordance with the provisions that apply to all detainees stored under the highest security precautions in Quantico.
Charges against Manning include the benefit of the enemy, unlawful publication of intelligence information on the Internet, the transmission of national defense information, and the theft of public property or documents.
If he is found guilty on all charges, he faces a life sentence.
Mexican-American identity crisis.
Due to the lack of opportunities, many people are forced to leave Mexico
Mexicans tend to accuse emigrants - they're reminding them of hard times, he says
Navarrette believes that Mexican Americans are trapped between two worlds
On a recent trip to Mexico City, I had just crossed the hall and had arrived at the passport control, as perplexity spread within me.
Signs referred to two queues: One for "Mexicanos" ("Mexicanos"), another for "Extranjeros" ("foreigners").
I stood there for a few seconds, unsurely which one I was supposed to go to.
I grew up in Central California and have been nicknamed "Mexicans all my life."
An ethnic abbreviation - just as my friends call themselves "Iren" or my friends in New York "Italian."
Later, I chose "Mexican-American."
But this was Mexico.
And in my grandfather's home, there was no need for abbreviations or hyphens.
I've just been American.
I speak Spanish, at least well enough, to assert myself in this language at both ends of an interview.
But I don’t have the vocabulary of a native speaker, and I can’t get rid of my American accent either.
So I took my American passport and put myself in line for Extranjeros.
I was thinking about that moment of this week when future Mexican President Enrique Pena Nieto visited the White House to meet with President Obama.
On the agenda were all those issues you expect when the minds of these two countries meet: immigration, drugs and trade.
Pena Nieto was also keen to talk about the growth of the Mexican economy. It’s one reason that Mexicians are equally likely to stay in Mexico these days rather than emigrating to the United States.
He wants to ally himself with the United States and Canada and create a North American trade blocluk modeled on the European Union.
And Pena Nieto swore to continue Mexico's war on drug cartels, even if he didn't give any details in this regard.
Mexico's relationship with the United States is complicated and full of resentment.
Most Americans probably never waste a single thought that the United States invaded Mexico in 1848, forcing the country's leadership underlined rifle to leave more than half of their territory to them.
But for Mexicans who think in centuries rather thanks in minutes, memories of this event cannot be overlooked.
So as soon as a U.S. government official criticizes even the mildest criticism of Mexico, one hears - in the Mexican press and among the country's elites - complaints about how the Americans are harassing the sovereignty of their neighboring country.
And Montezuma's children set out on the war path.
And yet Mexico's biggest challenge in truth lies in the relationship with the more than 35 million Mexican Americans living in the United States.
Do you want to talk about resentiment?
There's more than enough.
Mexico has winners and losers. People for whom the country offers opportunities, and others for whom it doesn't.
The only reason why so many people of Mexican origin live in cities like Los Angeles, Las Vegas, Phoenix, Denver, or San Antonio is that at one point in our family tree, a person - perhaps a parental or grandparent part - didn't get a chance in Mexico and emigrated to the north.
And in most cases, this person corresponded to a profile - dark-skinned, uneducated, from a poor village, etc.
We are the descendants of these men, and our faithfulness goes to them.
Not Mexico.
And even though we may now live the American dream, have attended good schools and received good jobs, we must never lose sight that it is the American dream that we live - not the Mexican one.
Our identity may sometimes be blurred, but in terms of our loyalty, that's not the case.
This clearly applies to the United States.
In addition, we are aware that many members of the Mexican leadership class don’t like us.
This is reciprocal.
They see us as a memorial to a humbling defeat and look down on us because we aren't Mexican enough.
Our Spanish will never be good enough, our ties to Mexico will never be strong enough.
In their opinion, our existence is a symbol of failure.
If our families hadn't failed in Mexico, they wouldn't have left.
And then we wouldn't see ourselves trapped behind the silk curtain now - with a pleasant life in the United States, but somehow lost.
My wife, who was born in Guadalajara and legally immigrated to the United States as a child, reminds me that there are tensions between Mexicans and Mexican Americans because Mexicans have a clearer idea about who they are - and Mexican Americans resent them.
While she is an American citizen, she sees herself as part of two countries.
However, many Mexican Americans I know have the feeling of not belonging to one country or another.
We love the Mexican band Los Tigres del Norte, but also Bruce Springsteen.
One enjoys the best of both worlds, but none of them are rooted.
In Mexico, we're seen as Americans.
And in the United States, we're seen as Mexicans.
As I should learn during my journey, there's something that makes this relationship even more complicated: some Mexican leaders and parts of the educational bourgeoisie would like to resume their connections to the diaspora.
They want to use Mexican Americans as provisional "ambassadors" to Mexico who represent their interests in the United States.
We are supposed to tell our American colleagues about how great a visit to this country is and put pressure on the political leadership to intensify their ties with Mexico.
Well, well.
That won't happen.
Too many hurt feelings.
And in view of the unjusty distribution of income, unbridled corruption and drug violence, many of us are not so sure whether it is really a great country.
I'm afraid you'll have to go through it alone, Amigos.
That's fair.
If at least some Mexicans aren't willing to forgive the United States to deal with Mexico a century and a half ago, they must also accept that some Mexican Americans harbor a grudge for how their family members were treated not so long ago.
Hmmm.
Maybe we're "Mexican" than I thought.
Old battles, New Middle East
The ceasefire between Israel and Hamas could prove to be an unexpected pillar of peace
Can there ever be lasting peace between Arabs and Jews in the Middle East?
The latest bloody events seem to nip such hope in the bud.
In the midstitute of the usual blaming, countless buildings were reduced to rubble, more than 140 Palestinians, most of them civilians, and six Israelis were killed - and for the first time, rockets from Gaza smashed near the Israeli metropolis of Tel Aviv and the holy city of Jerusalem.
Yet even as Israelis and Palestinians seem trapped in their historic conflict, the Middle East around them is changing.
The Arab Spring has created a new, uncertain situation, and whether they like it or not - Palestinians and Israelis have become part of these turmoils.
Perhaps this will only lead to more bloodshedding.
But there are reasons to believe that the deadly stalemate could finally come to an end.
A war that is neither lost nor won
At first glance, optimism in view of the current situation seems anything but appropriate.
Even if the armistice agreed on November 21 continues, this week's fighting has boosted the hard-pressed on both sides.
The leaders of Hamas, the Islamist movement that has been controlling the Gaza Strip since 2007, are claiming to have pushed back the Israelis, even though his grouping itself has suffered major casualties.
Although several Hamas leaders are killed and the 1.7 million inhabitants of the Gaza Strip are cooped up in one of the planet's most desolate and densely populated corners, Israel has so far failed to crush Hamas.
In fact, Hamas is gaining in the West Jant, which is controlled by the rival, temperate Palestinian group Fatah.
And Hamas leaders could come to the conclusion that they have time on their side.
Just as Islamists have gained influence in the Arab world, Hamas has been able to draw powerful and rich friends to its side.
Turkey, a resurgent, regional power that was once considered Israel's closest Muslim ally, has seized sides for Hamas - just like Qatar, one of the richest and most dynamic Gulf states.
Jubilant Hamas supporters believe that an Islamist crescent forms around Israel - from Lebanon in the north, where militant Hezbollah has power, over Syria, where rebels of an increasingly Islamist imprint could face the overthrow of Bashar Assad, to Jordan, where Hamas allies threaten the king.
And in particular the rise of the Muslim Brotherhood under President Muhammad Morsi in Egypt - by far the most populous and most important Arab country - on Israel's southern flank has permanently changed the balance of the region.
Hosni Mubarak, the secular despot who ruled Egypt for 30 years until his 2011 overthrow, had little time left for Hamas.
In contrast, the Brotherhood is contributed with Hamas and its leaders are more dependent on popular views.
In future diplomatic negotiations, Hamas could prove to be an actor that cannot be pushed out even by Israel and the United States.
Israeli hardliners, on the other hand, are drawing an opposite conclusion.
From a military point of view, Hamas is no longer in danger.
The Israeli missile defense system, "Iron Dome," has proven itself and many of the Hamas missiles have been destroyed.
At least for a while, Israel will be able to sleep more peacefully.
In diplomatic terms, the United States unwaveringly adhere to their position. Many European countries have blamed Hamas for the latest escalations of violence.
Israel has fared well in recent years, particularly under Prime Minister Binyamin Netanyahu, who has largely ignored the peace process.
Although rockets from the Gaza Strip have killed about 30 Israelis since 2004, the country has remained relatively free of suicide bombers - not least thanks to the barriers that run into the West Bank and thus into the main part of an eventual Palestinian state. However, these protect the Jewish settlements, which have been declared contrary to international law.
Netanyahu is doing well. His Likud party allied itself with an even more militant group in the run-up to the elections on January 22 under Avigdor Lieberman.
Why also these devious Palestinians coddle and leave them their own state?
If they actually had control of the West Bank, wouldn’t they fire rockets just like their comrades-in-arms in the Gaza Strip?
No, it's better to keep them behind the wall at bay and punishing if they dare to raise their heads.
Perhaps the hardliners will be able to prevail, but the Arab Spring could give them a dash.
Even if the Islamists, who are now in power in Egypt and in other countries, have little left for Israel, they will initially focus on the difficulties in their own country.
Israel's defense budget is greater than that of its four Arab neighbors combined.
A war with the local superpower will hardly help the new Arab governments repair their economies.
That pragmatic President Morsi teamed up with Barack Obama to achieve a cease-fire could be an auspicious sign - and perhaps symbolize the beginning of a new era.
Israelis should also think more long-term.
While the rest of the Arab world is taking tentative steps towards democratization, Palestinians continue to be deprived of their right to self-determination - a powder keg that will undoubtedly explode one day in Israeli-occupied territories, just as a bus exploded in Tel Aviv this week.
Oppression is already undermining democracy in the Jewish state, and with the growth of the Arab population, this situation will only worsen.
Bloody missions to push Hamas back in the Gaza Strip will increasingly take their diplomatic toll over the years.
Both sides need an impetus from the outside
The correct answer is those represented by the more rational representatives of both sides, the majority of the rest of the world and by this newspaper: two states in which Israel cedes territories for security purposes.
The - for the near-future small - hope is that the ceasefire grants outsiders more influence to enforce this solution.
Egypt, together with Turkey and Qatar, must now stop arms shipments to the Gaza Strip and is better positioned than ever before to convince Hamas of the idea of a Jewish state based on the 1967 borders, supplemented by territorial exchanges and a common Jerusalem.
Outsiding Arab nations should also persuade Hamas and Fatah to reach an agreement.
This would do more for the creation of a Palestinian state than with the imminent request for UN observer status.
Obama, too, needs to do his part to persuade Israel to engage in negotiations.
During his first tenure, he failed to present his own peace plan.
Far away in the white house, it seems like he's also averse to involvement.
But that's a regrettable, short-sighted perspective.
The United States has an essential interest in a stable Middle East.
And that includes a peace agreement between Israel and the Palestinians.
Australian laws for uniform cigarette packaging come into force
health warnings and diseased body parts on neutral green packs, which are the same for all tobacco brands
The world’s first laws for uniform cigarette and tobacco packaging have come into force in Australia, replacing brand logos and paints with neutral, olive-green lids printed with drastic images depicting sick body parts and cigarette-smoked infants and children.
Apart from the different health warnings and images, from next Saturday there is the only difference between the packs in the brand names, which are imprinted in identical, small font.
Thus, Australia introduces the world’s strictest regulations for tobacco packaging.
The Australian federal government wants to dissuade young people from smoking by eliminating the glamorous image of tobacco use.
In doing so, the government assumes studies that have shown that people who haven't started smoking at 26 are 99% more likely not to start even afterwards.
"You can already see in children of young ages that they understand the embassy that tobacco corporations are trying to promote with their brand image," according to Federal Health Minister Tanya Plibersek. She cites studies that have shown, for example, that children associate the crown of a logos with the idea of a princess.
Although Australia has one of the lowest smoking rates in the world and the changes will have little impact on the profits of multinational corporations, other countries are already considering similar steps.
The tobacco lobby fought hard against the laws.
So it was said that the laws would favor the black market trade, which would lead to cheaper and easier-to-reach cigarettes.
"The laws will lead to serious, unintended consequences," says Scott McIntyre of British American Tobacco Australia.
Chinese and Indonesian counterfeiters will offer far larger quantities of these products on Australia's streets.
Others, on the other hand, say that the laws have boosted their business.
Sandra Ha of Zico Import Pty Ltd, a small family business, notes that demand for cigarette cases and silicone lids have risen enormously to cover unbearable packaging since corporations British American Tobacco, Britain's Imperial Tobacco, Philip Morris and Japan Tobacco failed two months ago at Australia's Supreme Court with their appeal of laws.
Zico has sold up to 6,000 units to wholesale stores and is waiting for new inventories.
For us, that's good business.
According to experts, the catch in the popularity of social media lies with exactly the population group to which the plan is addressed.
A number of Australian laws have banned tobacco corporations from TV advertising, prohibited them from appearing as sports sponsors and forcing most traders to no longer interpret cigarettes in stores. Now, tobacco advertising has switched to the internet.
The Australian government has banned internet advertising by local companies and sites, but has no influence on international sites.
"Those who market tobacco and only have a small area left to promote their products can't get around the internet," says Becky Freeman, a healthcare researcher at Sydney University.
On social media such as YouTube, Twitter and Facebook, Freeman has noticed an increase in testimonials about brands written by "normal people."
You have to ask yourself the question: Is this just a private individual who finds such great pleasure in Marlboro cigarettes that she even records a video for it - or does a marketing agency have to do with it?
British American Tobacco Australia says that instead of marketing, the industry is focused on dealing with the new laws.
It has even gone so far as to pay Ukraine, Honduras and the Dominican Republic to challenge the new regulations - those countries are complaining to the World Trade Organization about unjust trade restrictions, even though none of the countries engage in greater trade with Australia.
A WTO decision is expected in mid-2013.
According to Plibersek, the government has held talks with other countries considering similar packaging laws.
In 2001, Canada was the first country to issue photographic warnings.
These have now been extended to more than 40 countries, including Brazil, Turkey and Ukraine.
Tougher laws are being considered in Britain, New Zealand, South Africa and India.
Many Australian smokers are defiant.
The pictures don't affect me.
I'll just ignore them.
"You just take a butt and put the box away," says Victor El Hage while buying a cigarette box, in which a photo with a mouth tumor is printed.
Honestly, there's only one reason I'd quit, and that's my little girl.
James Yu, operator of the "King of the Pack" tobacco trade in downtown Sydney, says uniform packaging has made it more difficult to replenish its shelves.
"It used to take me an hour to sort in a shipment, now I need four," Yu says.
"The government should have simply banned cigarettes completely - then we'd have told each other okay, that's it, we're closing," he adds visibly angry.
In a constantly electrified world, it’s nice to be bored
This Thanksgiving I unexpectedly spent five hours at the airport when our plane dropped out due to mechanical problems and we had to wait for another plane.
So I've had plenty of time to make a few thoughts about boredom.
I don't want to lie.
Waiting half a day at the airport for a flight is quite annoying - even if books, magazines and iPhones (and, of course, tax-free shopping) are available.
But more and more academics and child development experts cite boredom as something positive.
If it goes according to them, it's okay for us - and our children - to be bored occasionally.
Thus, the brain is forced to get lost in interesting trains of thought, which possibly promotes creativity.
And because most of us are almost constantly staring at one or the other screen these days, the benefits of boredom no longer benefit us.
Should we welcome boredom with open arms?
Yeah, yeah.
And no. No.
I'll come back to that again.
Like many people, I have always assumed that boredom is a relatively recent phenomenon that has developed with increasing leisure time.
But Peter Toohey, professor of Greek and Roman history at the University of Calgary in Canada and author of "Boredom: A Lively History" (Yale University Press, 2011) doesn't see it that way.
"In fact, boredom has a very long history," he says.
Pompeii's house walls adorn Latin graffiti over boredom dating back to the first century.
And then there's the question of how we're actually defining boredom.
According to John D. Eastwood, an associate professor of psychology at the University of York in Ontario, Canada, the problem is that boredom has been defined and debated in many ways.
After a long reading of research literature and the presentation of the idea in front of a focus group of about 100 people, Professor Eastwood and his colleagues came to the conclusion that boredom can be defined as the experience of "wanting to carry out a satisfying activity, but not being able to do so."
Boredom differs from apathy so that the person is not busy, but wants to be.
In apathy, however, there is no urge to do anything.
The key experience of boredom, he says, consists in a "disruption of the attention process, coupled with a bad mood and the feeling that time passes only slowly."
Boredom can sound almost like depression.
But Professor Eastwood believes that even though these two conditions may be related, bored individuals see the problem in their environment or the world, while depressed individuals see the problem in themselves.
Sometimes we think we're bored, even if we're just struggling to focus.
In her study "The Unengaged Mind: Defining Boredom in Terms of Attention", Professor Eastwood and his colleagues point to an earlier experiment, in which participants listened to a band on which a person read a magazine article in September.
Some groups listened to a noisy television program from the neighboring room, which had nothing to do with the contents. Others heard it at low volume, so it was barely perceptible, while the third group didn't hear those noises at all.
The individuals who witnessed the barely audible television said they felt more boredom than the other two groups - they had trouble concentrating, couldn't find any reason for these and attributed their difficulties to boredom.
Attempting to focus on a difficult or strenuous task can lead to boredom, says Mark J. Fenske, an associate professor of neuroscience at the University of Guelph in Ontario and one of the study's authors.
If, on the other hand, a monotonous activity is carried out - "just like the search for faulty products on a factory conveyor belt, music can distract and help not to feel bored".
In fact, he says, we now know that fidgeting and scribbling, often interpreted as a sign of boredom, can even combat them by keeping people physically more observantial.
"Studies have shown that children who are allowed to babble around learn more and retain more information than those who are forced to sit still," according to Professor Fenske.
All of us will get bored at some point - my late flight, a booming loudspeaker, a particularly tiring movie.
But some people are bored easier than others.
To measure these differences, researchers in the 1980s developed a "scale to susceptibility to boredom."
On the scale there are statements such as "Many things I have to do are repeated and are monotonous" and "I have so many interests that I don't have enough time for everything".
Using such scales, researchers have found that boys are getting bored more often than girls, according to Stephen Vodanovich, professor of psychology at the University of West Florida - in particular, in terms of the need for more and a greater variety of external stimuli.
But in general, teenagers are a rather strained bunch.
In 1991, Reed Larson, professor of human and community development at the University of Illinois, conducted an experiment in which he contacted nearly 400 teenagers and their parents seven to eight times a day with a pager.
He found that 32% of adolescents were bored at school and doing their homework, while 23% said they were bored if they weren’t in school.
However, only 3% of parents said they felt bored.
Professor Larson says today, 21 years later, that he does not know whether the percentage has risen or decreased in terms of boredom.
However, he is sure that "the youth is perceived as particularly boring", mainly because children and teenagers do not have a large influence on their daily routine.
So back to my origin question: Is boredom healthy?
Sometimes it doesn't, because in extreme cases it causes people to take extreme physical risks and forfeit gambling or drugs to alleviate them - that's what studies have shown.
On the other hand, many philosophers and authors examine the relationship between boredom and creativity. This opinion is Professor Vodanovich, who has been researching the topic for more than two decades.
"With boredom, the brain wants to tell you that you should do something different," according to Gary Marcus, a professor of psychology at New York University.
But the brain doesn't always know what best to do right now.
If you're bored and you're using that energy to play guitar and cook, you'll be happy.
But television can cause feelings of happiness in the short term, but these evaporate again in the long term.
So if you extend an iPad to your bored child, you can dissuade it from boredom. However, it has not learned to entertain or control yourself, says Professor Fenske.
And he adds: "Self-control translates from one situation to another."
The child not only learns to talk, but also gains more self-control in other areas.
I don't think we should celebrate boredom.
Nor should we see them too critically either.
Instead, our goal should be to make us feel comfortable away from constant activity and technology sprinkling.
Professor Eastwood agrees.
"It sounds like we've had to be bored more often. But boredom is the excruciating, incessant desire to do something meaningful," he says.
He believes that in truth, people are only looking for a way to switch off and relax.
"In an environment where we're constantly overstimulated, it's hard to get busy once the noise's gone silent."
In Colorado, there is no clear guideline for the new Marijuana Law
The 19-year-old communal college student and football player Anthony Orozco is on trial in south-eastern Colorado for an offense that will soon be legal in this state: the possession of a fewer chunks of marijuana and a whistle with which he smoked it.
According to Orozco, he and some friends were travelling by car in Lamar near the border with Kansas on a September day when they were stopped.
After the police officer found marijuana in the car, Orozco was summoned for the possession of drugs and drug utilities - cavalier offenses, each with a US$100 penalty - and received a court date.
"We are treated like criminals," Orozco said.
But is he one too?
In the uncertain weeks following the state’s decision to legalize small amounts of marijuana for personal use, the response for hundreds of minor drug offenses depends less on the law than on location.
Hundreds of misdemeanor offences that revolve around marijuana are already dropped here and in the Washington state, which approved similar measures.
Police agencies no longer prosecute adults over 21 years for possession on a small scale. This will be permitted by law once the new laws come into force in the next few weeks.
But prosecutors in Colorado's more conservative counties have announced to pursue existing marijuana offenses and are still citing people in court.
At the same time, several cities, from Denver's suburbs to the Western Mountains, are voting to prevent new government-licensed marijuan marijuana stores from opening in their communities.
"This situation evolves so quickly that I don't even know what's going to happen next," says Daniel J. Oates, police chief in Aurora, which lies east of Denver.
And regulators in the state of Washington are also scratching each other perpetuously.
They are waiting for guidance on the establishment of a licensing system for the production, manufacture, distribution and sale of the drug - and in doing so have a deadline of 1 December 2013 in mind.
In Washington, one thinks that in any case, Colorado is ahead of most states when it comes to the regulation of marijuana, where the drug was first legalized for medical purposes and then for self-consumption.
"Colorado has a more regulated market. The state is therefore well suited for orientation," says Brian E. Smith, spokesman for the liquor spirits regulator in Washington state.
But Smith also admits that no place or system can do more than serve in the sense of a rough guide.
"We don't have a real precedent that we can follow," he adds.
The Washington Law, called I-502, comes into force on December 6 and thus also forms the end point of a gray area in which the state licensing system does not yet exist, but ownership is legalized.
And awkward practical issues must be clarified during this time - such as how the state's requirement of "reasonable access" to licensed marijuana is to be agreed with the prohibition of cannabis shops within 300 meters around schools, parks, playgrounds and childcare facilities.
"Nowhere will it be harder to find a location for a licensed cannabis business than in urban areas, especially in the metropolitan area of Seattle," said Ben Livingston, spokesman for Center for Legal Cannabis.
On November 21, Chief Oates emailed his staff in Aurora in which he said the prosecutor would no longer prosecute minor marijuana offenses in those over the age of 21, and that police "as of now" will not seek criminal charges for these offences.
Chief Oates added that the police will continue to enforce urban guidelines to regulate growers of medical marijuana and drug traffickers will continue to be prosecuted.
In Weld County in northern Colorado, district attorney Ken Buck takes a stricter view.
After the vote, he stated that his authority would continue to prosecute cases of marijuana possession, mainly to persuade consumers to withdraw.
Currently, 119 people have to answer in court for the possession of about 50 grams or less marijuana, although many have also been indicted for other offenses.
"Our authority has a duty to prosecute violations that were offences at the time of the crime," Buck said in a statement.
Reactions are ambiguous even in places like rural Mesa County, where voters rejected the marijuana initiative.
Police in Grand Junction, the county's largest city, no longer instruct adults for owning smaller quantities.
District Attorney Pete Hautzinger supported this decision, but also decided not to drop all pending possession proceedings.
"I don't think I'm wasting my time enforcing the right until it changes," he says.
Although 55% of voters in Colorado supported the measure, it was clear from the outset that it would not be easy to reconcile the personal consumption of marijuana with the complexities of the government and legal system.
And the contradictory responses across the state produced a profound discord among local officials with regard to the great green experiment.
District prosecutors are a "cultural barrier," says Sean McAllister, a lawyer from Denver, who represents defendants on trial for marijuana offenses and acts locally as a spokesperson for the national organization to reform marijuana laws.
"They've spent so much of their lives prosecuting individuals for still not being able to accept legalization," he said.
As the first states to treat smaller amounts of marijuana such as alcohol, Colorado and Washington are traded as national model cases for the legalization of drugs.
While advocates and state officials are preparing for an age of legal sales, they are also eagerly awaiting instructions from the federal government, which wants to continue to treat the sale and cultivation of marijuana at the federal level as a criminal offence.
Advocates of the legalization movement hope that the Ministry of Justice will give in.
Despite several eye-catching arrests made by consumers and sellers of medical marijuana, the federal government has largely allowed the sale of medical marijuana in Colorado, Washington, and 16 other states.
Kevin A. Sabet, former drug policy adviser of the Obama administration, is of the view that criminal investigators are unlikely to enter doors to seize smaller amounts of the drugs, but presumably willing to condone legal, state-regulated marijuana deals under the new legislation.
Several cities in Colorado aren’t even waiting for federal agencies to act.
Even before Election Day, some local municipal governments approved moratoriums for new marijuana stores, although these could not open until about a year.
Last week, Montrose, a city in the west, passed a six-month ban, presumably passed next week.
"We don't want to get into a position where we're licensing someone and then facing a big problem at the federal level," according to Bob Nicholson, a member of the city council.
Our community voted against this change in the law.
We depend on what the municipality has chosen and not according to what the state has chosen.
There are still many questions open.
Petronella Wyatt: I've been bullied from Oxford because I'm Tory
It's not just today's university students who are attacked for
It had always been my greatest dream to maintain a place at Oxford University.
Both my father and my older brother had visited that university, which I once considered to be the world's greatest stamp of knowledge, to be a modern Greek symposium that promoted the great pillars of civilization - free thinking and tolerance.
But within two weeks of my move into Worscester College at the end of the eighties - that's where I had planned to study history - I'd packed my suitcases again and brought about the first scandal of my life.
My dad collapsed in tears.
My friends were confused.
The Evening Standard said I had left because I would have had a problem with fellow students having sex in the neighbouring room.
Writer A N Wilson mischievously declared that I had left the university because I was forced to drink from damaged cups.
But the truth was less amusing.
I've ran away.
Yes, ran, because I suffered from systematic bullying and threats.
Not because of my rather unusual name, or because I had come from a private school.
I had only been harassed for one reason - a reason that seemed all the more narrow-minded and cruel in this alleged cradle of enlightenment: my now-defunct father Woodrow Wyatt had been an important adviser to Margaret Thatchers and I was a proponent of the Conservative Party.
Why am I mentioning this right now?
Well, according to current reports, a new generation of students of temperate rights are suffering from similar persecution.
Organised and rising hatred of Tory students at Oxford, meanwhile, has developed such dimensions that a group of these last week called for equal equality protection that benefits gays, disabled people and ethnic minorities.
Conservative members of Corpus Christi College's Junior Common Room (JCR) say they are "often actively isolated, personally assaulted and declared unwanted" because of their political views.
They call for a place on their college's equality committee to ensure their opinions can be voiced freely.
Her situation was exacerbated by a recentively aired documentary about political students on BBC Two, "Wonderland: Yown, Bright and on the Right," portraying Tories as an eccentric and neo-Nazis.
Among others, graduate Joe Cooke, former president of Oxford University's Conservative student association, who drove in a Rolls-Royce and boasted silver-coloured suit and silver-studded walking stick.
At other universities, conservative students say they are considered "scapegoats for the introduction of higher tuitions."
20-year-old Luke Black, vice president of Nottingham University's Conservative Student Association, said in a Sunday newspaper: "There is a growing left-wing tendency at universities.
People think we’re like the Bullingdon Club without even getting to know us.”
Samuam Roberts, 21 and history student in Corpus Christi, who initiated the request for greater protection, believes such a climate is "unpleasant." 19-year-old Stephanie Cherill, president-elect of Oxford's Conservative student association, says she has witnessed a growing rejection among JCR members towards students of temperate rights.
"This poses a danger to intellectual discourse and the well-being of members," she says.
In my first few weeks at Oxford, I belonged to such a minority.
As an incredibly shy 18-year-old, I had arrived in September 1986.
Hatred of the Conservative Party had peaked.
In the previous year, the university had voted not to award Margaret Thatcher - a former student - an honorary degree due to cuts in higher education.
The atmosphere would even have spooked Stalinists.
In the first days of the first semester week, during which new students get to know themselves and the tutors, I received a taste of the future events.
I had the experience that the tutors not only tolerated the mocking of Tory students, but even participated with joy.
Political issues such as the miners' strike, privatisation and the government's rejection of sanctions against South Africa's apartheid regime found their way into the wood-panelled rooms of the tutorium.
At my first of these events, I was supposed to translate 18th-century French texts into English. I hadn’t been prepared for what should come.
"Miss Wyatt," said the tutor Harry Pitt (meanwhile deceased), "please translate the first paragraph."
I stumbled with it.
Pitt was a small man with a face as if from cake dough, and quite naughty.
"Don't Thatcher supporters refuse to learn French, or are they just stupid?" he asked me.
The other students chuckled.
In my eyes, tears rose.
"I suggest that you take a few French lessons in your spare time - so if you're not too busy with your social activities," Pitt growled at me.
I returned to my room with my head hanging.
That night, I was sitting alone at dinner in college when someone tapped me lightly.
It was a second-year English student who introduced himself as a member of the Conservative student association.
"I know who you are," he said kindly.
Unfortunately it's what it's like.
Anyone suspected of being Tory is being harassed.
It's bad enough for me, but they know that your father Margaret Thatcher's close, that's why it's going to be worse for you.
Most Tory's first-tears pretend to be supporters of the Labor party.
At a local pub, I later tried to disguise myself.
I insisted that I didn't agree on all points with Mrs. Thatcher.
This trick should prove unsuccessful.
A first-year student in philosophy, politics and economics, ironically attending Eton College, said to me: "You are the daughter of a fascist pig."
You're polluted.
Other students shared his opinion.
I've been perverse and dirty.
"How do Tories have sex?" someone asked me.
They're beating each other, aren't they?
I felt the way homosexuals must have felt before the more liberal legislation of the United States.
Would I ever be able to live a normal life at Oxford University?
Would I be able to meet like-minded people only after dark?
Should I have to turn to the Labour Party and suppress my natural inclinations?
The three years ahead of me stretched in front of my mind to a purgatory of ostracism and isolation.
The only tutor who openly revealed himself as Tory was Norman Stone, professor of contemporary history, based in my college.
He was hated not only for being a conservative, but also because he had advised Thatcher in matters of foreign policy and wrote speeches for her.
He's rarely been there.
He hated the place, found it provincial and petty, and rejected it for his Marxist-deterministic historical perspective.
In 1997 he accepted a professorship from the University of Bilkent in Ankara, Turkey.
"You won't be happy here," he told me.
I started commuting between Oxford and my parents' house and found refuge with more open-minded friends and family members in the city.
I told my dad I hated Oxford, and why I did it.
He seemed unbelieving.
During his time in the Forties, all political views were accepted.
"But it's the best place in the world," he said petty.
They'd never do anything like that, not in my town of dreaming spires.
Even my communist friends always showed impeccable manners.
His wet eyes clouded.
Give the whole thing a chance.
I'm sure they're just teasing you.
It'd break my heart if you left.
Exhausted by my frequent travels to London, my emotional resistance weakened.
One of my male friends, also Tory supporters, had given in to the pressure and renounced his beliefs.
During a tutorial the following week in which another history tutor had seriously claimed that I was "an enemy of the people," I decided to do the same.
Even though I secretly disintegrated from shame, I admitted that my parents had subjected me to "brainwashing," calling them "old jerks."
Relief lasted only briefly.
It was my dad who smashed the last nail into the coffin of my Oxford career.
At that time, he wrote two columns a week in the Murdoch press.
My door was locked.
I hid myself in my room, and after five minutes, my hunters gave up.
As they left, I packed a suitcase and took the first train to London.
I've never returned.
You are welcome to call me a whiny floppy dick.
But no 18-year-old should ever suffer from such intimidation and malice in an educational institution.
And even more tragible is that it was Oxford - a university that not only spawned 14 Tory prime ministers, but still hides behind an undeserved reputation of equality and freedom of thought to this day.
"Valentino prefers elegance to celebrity"
On the occasion of the exhibition "Valentino - Master of Couture," which opened in London this week, ABC speaks with Naty Abascal, Fiona Thyssen-Bornemisza and other famous customers of the Italian fashion maker.
Somerset House, the former residence of Isabel I of England, is the only place in the British capital worthy of hosting an exhibition on Valentino Garavani.
This was recognized by the designer at the opening of "Valentino - Master of Couture," a terrific retrospective for which more than 130 dresses of haute couture have been put together created by his "maison" over the past 50 years.
"I love this palace," he says with his unmistakable Italian accent.
This exhibition is the conclusion of a story whose only protagonist is "il signore" Garavani, but which could not have been written without his distinguished clientele.
Valentino has always felt fascination with the stuffy and distant world of nobility.
In the first room of this exhibition, which will be on display until 3 March, there are a variety of private letters and photos with the signature of the most exquisite aristocracy, ranging from Princess Salimah Aga Khan to the Princess Marie-Chantal of Greece and Margarita of England to Lord Snowdon.
Valentino depicts these personal memories as trophies of his social rise from the modest fashion maker in the town of Voghera in northern Italy to the idol of the international jet set.
Being in love with the nobility doesn't have to be detrimental.
"At least they don't step out their butts on your magnificent carpet, as some beautiful celebrities from pop music do," says baroness Fiona Thyssen-Bornemisza.
In the 60s and 70s, the two lived in the Alps and were good friends.
Valentino is a spectacular, generous and elegant host.
"We were all happy when we were invited to his house in Gstaad," explains the ex-wife of "Heini" Thyssen, close friends with forgotten beauties such as Marella Agnelli or Eugenie Niarchos.
Valentino has always embraced the elegance of celebrity.
And yet he's a star.
Valeria Mazza, in a Valentino dress
Even the Argentine model Valeria Mazza doesn't forget the fashion maker's charisma.
Many years ago, after a fashion show at Piazza di Spagna in Rome, we went to his home for dinner.
We were about 20 people, including Sharon Stone and Johnedny Jr.
At every detail of his apartment, the decoration, the food and the music one recognized and felt his influence.
"All invitees felt important and loved," recalls the "top model," which began her career at Haute Couture week in Paris in 1996.
"He's never out of fashion because his designs are works of art," she concludes.
fashion show of origin
Garavani's life is not a story of obsessiveness, rather of mutual lovies.
He likes people with good upbringing and the highest titles, and they love him.
One of the galleries in Somerset House has been transformed into a glorious and 60-meter-long catwalk proposing a role swap: the user occupies the place of the model and walks along the catwalk to admire a dreamlike "audience" wearing masterpieces by Valentino, such as the dress that Jackie Kennedy chose for her wedding with Aristóteles Onassis, the costume
In this audience of fashion dolls, names such as those of Sibilla de Luxemburgo, Gloria von Thurn and Taxis, Mette-Marit of Norway, Rosario of Bulgaria or Sofía von Habsburg shine.
Naty Abascal and the fashion maker, 2006
Many of these customers say the first Valentino is "unforgettable."
I can still remember perfectly.
It was trousers, a shirt, a vest type "Gillette" and a jacket from the autumn/winter collection 1971-1972.
"It was a gift from him," says Naty Abascal, one of the designer's muses.
"I prefer him to everyone else because of his great love for the women and because he highlights our beauty," the ex-Duchess of Feria adds.
I really like the colors he uses; they have a lot of light and underline the face.
Proportions are perfect.
Even the princess and opinion leader at fashion Patricia della Giovampaola d'Arenberg can't forget for the first time when she wore a Valentino.
When I was living in Italy as a teenager, I dreamed of finally having the age and the opportunity to wear one of his evening dresses...
At the end of the 1990s it finally happened.
I bought my first Valentino dress for a feast in my cousin's castle, Prince Edouard de Ligne.
It was a red dress with a flying skirt and a draped top with a décolleté that lives up to its name.
"It was a dream come true," says the widow of Rodrigo d'Arenberg.
"Valentino doesn't depend on fashion, his obsession is timeless," explains this Italian aristocrat, who lives between París, New York and Buenos Aires.
Princess D'Arenberg keeps her evening dresses of the fashion maker "with absolute care", "because a dress is not only a dress, but also the associated memories".
The "king" of fashion
The grand finale of the exhibition in Somerset House is Marie-Chantal Miller's wedding dress for her wedding to Pablo de Grecia in 1995.
Four months of work and 25 "Ragazze" (as the fashion maker called his seamstresses) were necessary to make this ivory silk dress with pearls, twelve different embroideries and a four-and-a-half-meter-long tail.
According to journalist Suzy Menkes, the highest authority of the trade press, this dress was a milestone in haute couture at the end of the 20th century, "the return to the customers of supreme society."
Tarnished for years with the "savoir-être" of the elite, Valentino is now her best specimen.
Cavaliere di Gran Croce (the tallest accommodation of Italy), Cavaliere del Lavoro, Commandeur de L'Ordre des Arts et des Lettres, awarded the Legion of Honour, cumulating Garavani as many honors as any of the husbands of his customers.
"I've always noticed his refinement, his tranquility, his well-groomed and perfect appearance," D'Arenberg admits.
I last seen him a month ago at a gala dinner at the Museum de Orsay.
He was sitting at the table of Countess Jacqueline de Ribes, a very good friend of mine.
“He was immaculate; time leaves no mark on him.”
And that's what a princess says...
The hardest profession in the world: Kawah Ijen's human workhorses
For four euros, the bearers of the Indonesian volcano put their lives and their health at risk if they carry 70 kilos of sulphur over the steep, stony paths.
There are people for whom their work is hell, and others literally work in hell.
This is the case with Anto Wijaya, one of the 400 miners who deserve their lives by getting sulphure from the Kawah Ijen volcano in the east of the Indonesian island of Java.
To do this, he has to climb down to the bottom of his crater every day, where the sulphur-containing gas flowing out of the earth solidifies in contact with the air.
After breaking out large sulphur stones that can weigh up to 70 kilos in total, he transports them into two bamboo baskets on his shoulders over the sloping paths made of stone.
Although it is only 250 meters to the top of the volcano, which rises to a height of 2386 meters, but the exhausted wearers need more than 40 minutes for ascension at tortoise tempo, keeping the balance and having to take their steps carefully so that they don't slip and crash.
They know that every step can cost their lives, as happened to a French tourist who crashed years ago on the steeping slopes of Kawah Ijen.
Kawah Ijen's miners earn 5 euro cents for every kilo of sulphur they mine.
Arrived at the top, they make their way between the tourists, whom they photograph like circus monkeys and walk three kilometers with the tiringly heavy baskets to Libra, which a mining company has set up a little further down at an altitude of 1850 meters
The company is PT Candi Ngrimbi, which since 1960 has exploited the deposits in the volcano and (never better said) its workers, to whom it pays 662 Indonesian rupees (5 euro cents) for every kilo of sulphur.
She then sells it to the petrochemical industry for 10,000 rupees (83 euro cents each), because this mineral is widespread in everyday life and is used for the production of matches, fireworks, cosmetics, dynamite and even for bleaching sugar.
"Since we normally carry 70 kg, we get about 46,000 rupees (3.8 euros) for each route," explains Anto, who usually does three routes a day.
For each route he needs three hours, after which he is completely done, but at the end of the day he then has 138,000 rupies (11.5 euros) together.
Which looks like a miserable reward for such an almost inhuman effort, however, is three times what he would earn with field work.
"The daily wage of the miners here, where 15,000 rupees (1.2 euros) are paid a day during the coffee harvest and the average monthly income is two million rupees (167 euros), is very high," explains the carrier, who previously worked as a bricklayer on the tourist island of Bali.
There he earned 75,000 rupees (6.2 euros) a day, and the work wasn't that hard, but Anto has returned with his family to Banyuwangi, a village nearby, and that's for a reason that's as important in Indonesia as the sulphur: "I married a girl from Bali, where they're Hindus, and I've taken them to Java to convert them to Islam."
Anto has asthma, breathes with difficulty, coughs frequently and his eyes are irritated because of the toxic gases.
27-year-old Anto has been risking his life at Kawah Ijen for three years, whose sulphur has already left the first mark on him, even though he covers his face with a mask and special glasses.
Anto has asthma, breathes with difficulty, coughs frequently and his eyes are irritated because of the poisonous gases from the volcano.
This is the price he has to pay for achieving his dream.
"I'm going to work here for another two years because I want to open a shop or learn Spanish or French," he promises in more than acceptable English.
This sympathetic and intelligent, but life-ridden young man could work as a tourist guide, waiter or at the reception of a hotel, but instead he performs the work of a chask.
He shares a dirty wooden hut with other porters and rises every morning at two o'clock, because the sulphur also pours out at night, where its characteristic yellow color shines bluish in darkness.
Challenging the shadows, he climbs into the crater in the light of a small headlamp on his helmet, which he bought from his own money.
Approximately 400 wearers load the baskets with sulfur on their shoulders on the crater floor.
Despite its large profits, the mining company has not mechanized sulphur mining in order to save costs and does not provide any equipment to the porters, who work on their own account and are paid by weight.
There is also no share of the 30,000 rupees (2.5 euros) surcharge per camera, which the guardians of this nature park, together with the entrance fee in the amount of 15,000 rupees (1.2 euros), demand from the tourists who come to photograph the volcano and the human stretchers.
"This work is for the animals and not for humans," protests Madrusin, a burly, 42-year-old porter who has been working at Kawah Ijen for three decades since leaving school.
He is able to carry up to 110 kg and assures that he continues "as long as possible" because he needs the money to raise his three children, aged between 10 and 18.
I won't retire, I'm going to die here because the volcano has been my whole life.
Even if the sulphur burns the throat and the eyes itch, when the wind suddenly turns and pinches the miners into the thick pillars rising from the crater, the men are so hard that no one complains of serious diseases ... of course except the usual breathing problems, osteoarthritis, pain in the knees and blisters on the shoulders that have deformed from the weight of the basket.
With the basket on his back, Unainik, at the age of 53, is only able to carry 50 kg.
Every day, he and his companions break 15 tons of sulphur from the volcano, which are transported by three trucks via a goat path between the shrubbery to the camp in Tamansari at a distance of 18 kilometers.
"I won't retire, I'm going to die here because the volcano has been my whole life," Unainik explains, opening his mouth wide where some teeth are missing.
Of his five children, the elder also works as a sulphur carrier at the age of 30.
Time passes, but poorness persists generation by generation in one of the toughest professions in the world: that of those who work as a human donkey at Kawah Ijen.
Singapore needs babies to save the economy
Singaporeans blame that they don't have children, the career, the stress and the cost of real estate and child-rearing.
"The population of Singapore needs to grow."
I'm a patriotic husband, you're my patriotic wife: Let's fulfill our civicial duty and create life!
It seems unbelievable that the verses are part of an advertisement for mint candies, but nevertheless - or precisely because of it - they became a viral video on YouTube earlier this year in Singapore.
The sentences are part of a rapper song that makes local sayings such as "pushing a "bao" (loaf) into the oven to make fun of Singapore's low birth rate.
The advertising company BBH, which shot the video, maintains the hope that the commercial will reach out in a funny way to draw attention to the problem.
Its creative director Douglas Hamilton says he wanted to use the power of music to get people to fulfill their "national duty."
This is something just for the Internet, so we had to make it funny and funny.
This is the biggest problem in this country.
When reproducing we are the worst in the world, that’s why we realized it’s a topic we had to take care of.
We knew that the government had tried a lot, such as marketing perfume with pheromones or organising speed dating evenings (fast flirtations).
It is possible that many of these ideas were creative, but they didn’t necessarily lead to success.
That’s why we thought: why don’t we do something as creative as possible to address this problem, such as composing a rap?
1.2 children
Those who don't take this issue lightly are Singapore's government.
Every year it spends US$1,300 on policies to encourage citizens to have more children.
A government package for married couples and parents grants up to 15,000 dollars per child, extends maternity leave and distributes tax benefits.
But everything has had little success so far.
Singapore is a richer, high-technological city-state in south-eastern Asia that is also known for the conservatism of its rulers and its strict social controls.
According to its national population authority, the birth rate is currently 1.2 children per woman.
The last time that rate was over 2, which is also known as a replacement rate - was in 1976.
So why don’t Singaporeans have more children?
Tan Wei Ming, director of marriage and family policy at the national population authority, says this is due to "better education" and "greater career opportunities."
"This has enabled people to have substantially more options in terms of life goals and prioritizes besides getting married and forming a family," she explains.
These changes in social conditions have contributed to the higher number of single people and to the delay of marriage and birth, the result of which is the reduction of the birth rate in Singapore.
Meanwhile, an immigration policy aimed at a drastic increase in immigration in order to counteract the reduction of the population led to resentment among the local population.
In Singapore, there are websites where the barely concealed xenophobia is flourishing against many new immigrants, especially against the Chinese, who are criticized for keeping wages low and failing to integrate.
The surge in immigration is also seen as one of the reasons the ruling party in Singapore scored its worst election result last year since independence.
Since the elections, an attempt has been made to correct the problem with higher contributions and levies for foreign workers.
Unexpected Consequences
While the falling birth rate has the well-known impact on a nation's economic growth, tax revenues, health care costs and immigration policy, Singapore's example also shows unexpected consequences.
The government is trying to ensure that not so many small apartments are built.
So it's already had an impact on the housing market.
The authority for urban development has begun to control the number of small apartments known as "shoe boxes" and may be built in certain areas of the city.
These apartments have a living space of 46 square meters and show a great sales success.
However, there is concern that they can promote the lifestyle of the single, and that they discourage the construction companies from building large housing for families.
But Lim Yew Soon, general manager of the real estate company EL Developers, says that his "shoe boxes" can be sold much faster than larger housing units.
They are so popular, and the units are sold out by days and even weeks faster than the larger housing units.
As a result, they are much better for our cash flow.
He admits, however, that the new standards give the developers clearer orientations, which have previously been placed obstacles if they wanted to build too many small housing units within a project.
Too much stress
Singapore is a city-state.
Even if these new rules could pave the way for an increase in the national birth rate, it can be seen from conversations with the Singaporeans working in the financial district that they will not have much effect.
"People are severely stressed, the apartments and also education are expensive, which is why it is postponed by many," says a young manager.
The kids can have others.
"For me, it's important to dispose of my own money and time," says another, roughly twenty-year-old man.
Men and women cite their careers, the stress and cost of property and education as reasons that they can't have children.
That's whhere Singaporeans have the final say when it comes to having children - that's where the government can still do so much.
The offline private offline is private online
privacy
According to the dictionary, it is the property private or "that area of private life where you are entitled to protection from any type of interference".
What’s private for someone under the age of 16?
How is this definition applied to his everyday life and social networks?
Does he understand the dangers he exposes himself to when he publicizes situations on the Internet that he wouldn’t share in real life?
El Periódico interviewed five teenagers between the ages of 10 and 15 who are often on-the-net.
In four of the cases, they linked the term to "something very own" on a personal level and "to the password and the user" when they applied it to the social networks.
"I wouldn't reveal my deepest secrecies in a post," says 10-year-old Jorge while attempting to explain the importance of "privately" in websites such as Facebook, Twitter, Hotmail and Windows Live Messenger, where he's had accounts for two years.
"These are very confidential secrets, my mother may know them, but not the whole world," he confirms.
On FB I upload beautiful pictures or pictures of games.
I'm running out of time with acquaintances, too.
"I wouldn't share a photo that isn't of me, or from someone who makes the dumbhead," he says.
The boy is aware that it is wrong to publish obscene photos, of naked people, of crimes, or writing offensive or forcible comments.
Jorge assures to know 35 friends he has in FB and nine followers on Twitter.
Most of them are relatives.
Including the mother who has the password to one of the accounts.
I signed up on Twitter to express myself and write interesting tweets.
"I don't know if anyone's answering me, I'm just uploading them," he adds.
"The social networks are entertaining, I can quickly talk to relatives in the distance or with friends," he states.
Asked, he replies that he would never accept an invitation from an unknown.
Even if someone recommended a stranger to him, he would ignore it.
14-year-old José's case is different.
The teenager has user accounts in Hotmail, Facebook, My Space and Ask. In Ask, he admits not to know 20 of the people he has attached to his friends list.
"That doesn't scare me because we've had something in common, for example, music," he states.
According to the boy, no one gave him hints or asked him for his address or phone number.
"If someone pushes me or asks them of me, I'll just remove them from my user account," he admits.
José contacted Ask after reading a recommendation on Twitter.
The boy’s experience, which is now known as cyberbullying, is unknown.
An acquaintance of a friend was blackmailed on a social network.
They threatened him and demanded money.
"He never knew who it was," he assures.
According to José, the victim didn't close the user account.
"He only did it privately."
He then explains a series of steps to configure a user account securely.
Unlike Jorge, this boy would upload photos of acquaintances in uncomfortable or embarrassing situations.
Yes, I'd do that if he's unsympathetic to me or I'm trying to wipe him one.
"But I know that this is cyberbullying," he explains.
Key Questions
Marielos Porras, teacher of English with a university degree in education and learning, thinks that in order to orientate the children and adolescents, it must be understood that it is the purpose of social networks to inform.
"The Internet emerged as a medium for the search for information; however, with the appearance of these pages, the rules of the game have changed," she says.
Porras explains that the academic Marc Prensky, holder of a master's degree in pedagogy at Yale University and author of the work "Digital Natives and Immigrants", formed these terms to explain the phenomenon.
The digital natives are them, the children and teenagers who grew up with this technology.
"We are the digital immigrants they are supposed to teach when we're still learning," he said.
He explains that it is a complex issue "because we demand from them that they have a defined criterion about what is allowed to spread, publish or say at an age where maturity is insufficient for it."
"They're also supposed to be picky when it's most important to them to be popular to have thousands of friends without thinking about the consequences," he adds.
According to the professional, the most effective way to teach the children and young people what privacy is to ask them questions that makes them think.
"Telling them aren't supposed to do it doesn't work," he says.
Porras then mentions some possibilities: There are things you wouldn’t tell a stranger. Then why are you doing it on the Internet?
Or: Would you like it if you published a photo of you, such as the one you published by a friend?
Do you know what the others post about you?
Did you ask the other people for permission in advance if you've labeled photos of celebrations?
And one more question: Does everyone have to know what you’re doing at every moment?
Another point is telling them to understand that they need to act online like they do offline.
They're the same rules.
"Someone outside the Internet acts with respect, morality and other principles, and the same must apply to the social networks," he explains.
Surveillance
Estuardo Guardia, university professor, elementary school teacher and educating counselor, commends that it is essential for parents to read the guidelines of the social networks carefully.
If they understand all sections, they have solid foundations to talk to their children about the importance of opening a user account on the internet.
"For example, the age, or what you are allowed to share or publish," he explains.
According to Guardia, the children must be reminded of the lesson "Thought you shall not talk to strangers."
Completion of the Unasur Summit without publication of the Lima Declaration
The 6th Summit of Presidents of the Union of South American Nations (Unasura) ended today in Peru without the publication of the Lima Declaration, which was previously announced and should theoretically be signed by the seven rulers present.
Efe repeatedly tried to gain access to the signed document at the VI. Ordinary meeting of the heads of state and government of Unasur. Sources at the state and presidential chancellery initially announced that they would hand it over after the examination, but explained afterwards that it would be published on the Peruvian government’s website at some time.
For questions about the text, they stated that the content was presented by the President of Peru, Ollanta Humala, during brief explanations to the press.
Journalists' access to information about the summit was limited at all times.
During the day in the press room, only the image of the presidential meeting was transmitted without sound with the message "private session, limited audio."
The little information circulated among the journalists was obtained from the press spokespersons of some of the Unasur governments present at the meeting, but not from the Peruvian press secretary.
The only document circulated during the day was the list of participating presidents, which provoked unease among the hundreds of journalists from various national and international mediaries calling for more information.
After that, the presidential office of Peru sent the media an e-mail with the "concluding declaration" of the summit meeting, but it was Humala's statement and not the official document at the conclusion of the summit.
Last October, Peru hosted the Third Summit of South America - Arabian States (ASPA), and even on this occasion, the so-called Lamia Declaration, which had previously been announced, was also not published.
On ASPA's official website, you can see that the document was published last Tuesday.
At both international meetings, the Peruvian authorities stressed that the information systems were available for the journalists, but to a large extent they restricted access to information.
The summit also ended with the joint commitment of Chile and Peru to adopt a ruling by the Court of Justice of The Hague, which settles a border conflict between the two countries.
Peru president Ollanta Humala, and Chile, Sebastián Piñera, met during the regional meeting and confirmed that they would accept the ICJ’s decision, which begins in The Hague on Monday, in the trial launched by Lima against Santiago to hear the pleas from both sides.
"We will follow and carry out the verdict on the differences we are currently bringing to this international judgy," Humala, along with his Chilean counterpart, said.
"Chile was, is and will continue to be a country that adheres to international laws, seeks peaceful solutions for disputes, concludes agreements and submits to the international courts," Piñera adds, greeting Humala with a handshake in front of the flags of both countries.
The confirmation by both presidents that they would submit to the ICJ came after Colombia filed an action against Bogotá Agreement following a decision on the maritime borders with Nicaragua, which it considered to be a serious mistake, in which it agreed to submit to the judgments of that international court.
At the summing, the presidents of Brazil, Dilma Rouseff, Venezuela, Hugo Chávez, of Bolivia, Evo Morales, and of Argentina, Cristina Kirchner.
Paraguay, who has been barred from prison since 2011 after the deposition of his President Fernando Lugo, did not attend the meeting.
Hospitable President Ollanta Humala opened the talks in the morning and ended the summit shortly after midday in Lima.
The head of state read out the final document, which announced that 16 decisions were taken and the lines of action for 31 projects have been established between the South American countries over a total investment amount of 17 billion dollars.
Commenting on the decisions taken, he said that the countries of the Unasu will take "significant steps towards the goal of South American citizenship, to which we extend decisions on residence rights."
He said measures would be pushed forward "to improve cooperation in the fight against insecurity in cities and against organised transnational crime, as well as measures to improve access to medicines and the Internet at low prices in all areas of South America, and to act collaboratively and efficiently in the event of natural disasters."
With the crisis in Europe, "economic consolidation (in Latin America) must not take on triumphant traits, but is intended to expand our production performance and show our peoples a better future," Humala added.
"We've decided to give priority to a number of 31 emblematic projects designed to improve spatial connections in South America, and especially in rural and border areas...and thus unite our countries and open up new forms of economic," the Peruvian president said in a read-out statement.
Among these projects would also be five Peruvians lying on the transverse axes of his territory from the coast to Brazil, two of which are supposed to provide a better connection with Ecuador, even though he gave no further details.
The final document also referred to the political situation in Paraguay.
"We hope that the electoral process in this country will serve for its resumption in the union of South American nations, of which it is currently excluded.
The need for Latina to remain an integrated region for prosperity and peace, and with good neighbourly relations, was another aspect that was emphasised at the summit.
In this sense, the President of Colombia, Juan Manuel Santos, said before his participation in the regional meeting that he expected a meeting in Mexico on Saturday with his counterpart of Nicaragua, Daniel Ortega, in order to treat the maritime conflict respectfully according to a verdict questioned by Bogotá of the ICJ.
"Possibly I'll meet with President Daniel Ortega tomorrow (Saturday)," Santos said.
"We will review all of these avenues - they are non-exclusive, and the agreement with Nicaragua is also making talks with Nicaragua necessary," he underlined.
"I hope to be able to tell President Ortega that we will treat this matter as civilised and respectful as possible," Santos added.
Santos and Ortega would meet in Mexico on Saturday, where they intend to be present at the takeover in office of that country's new president, Enrique Peña Nieto.
As part of the summit, the bloc's defence ministers also met in advance for the adoption of the action plan for 2013, which aims to strengthen dialogue and consensus in relation to the region's defence.
Argentina, Bolivia, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, Brazil, Uruguay, Venezuela, Chile, Guyana, Surinam and Paraguay are members at UNASUR, even if the latter country is currently excluded.
Peru presided over time of regional bloc.
"South America needs to learn in the integration of citizens," says Rafael Correa
The president of Ecuador, Rafael Correa, confirmed today that the creation of joint citizenship is a goal whereby "South America can actually learn something from Europe."
Correa, who attended the 6th Summit of Presidents of the Union of South American Nations (Unasaur), held in Lima, explained to state television in Peru that Europeans "killed each other in World War II and in other clashes" and "are practically a fatherland today."
In this sense, he defended the project to create a South American citizenrhip that advances the member states of theirs.
"The free movement of citizens and workers in all South American countries must be achieved, as is already the case in the Andean community; however, there are still reactionary sectors seeking a return to the past," he warned.
The President of Ecuador also advocated the redesignation of the Organization of American States (OEA) undertail to reduce the influence of Anglo-Saxon states and take into account those who signed the San José Agreement on Human Rights.
Those who teach are never committed to anything, and in return, we always sign the South Americans everything.
"It is incomprehensible that the Inter-American Commission on Human Rights is based in Washington and is funded by the United States," he declared as a reference to the political asylum Ecuador granted to WikiLeak's founder Julian Assange.
Correa assured that he did not regret this decision because he had not betrayed his principles, but complied with his "deep democratic values and views on human rights."
He added that at the time there had been "substantiated presumptions that Assange would be extradited to a third country and his procedure would not be upheld."
He also criticized the Swedish judiciary, which had demanded that him be subject to interrogation for an alleged sexual offense on his territory, while "the Swedish jurisprudence allowed this by means of a video conferencing that could have been carried out through Ecuador's embassy in London."
Correa explained that there was a risk that Assange's health and mental state would be compromised.
"Since he has been in our embassy, I haven't spoken to him, but the ambassador informed me that he has a slight problem with the lungs, but not a serious one," Ecuador's head of state said.
There is, however, the risk that he takes physical and psychological damage because he is trapped in a confined space and cannot carry out physical exercises outdoors.
"That would affect everyone's health," he added.
Correa stated that the resolution to the asylum granted to Assange at Ecuador's embassy in London since June has been the granting of a free pass with which he could travel to Ecuador, and that this was in the hands of Britain, Sweden and Europe's legal authorities. He underlined that there had been talks with London to find a solution for the imprisoned founder of WikiLeaks.
We do not act with human rights, we do not apply this word for this case, but there have been ongoing discussions.
"The solution to this problem lies in the hands of Britain, Sweden and the legal authorities of Europe, because Assange's lawyer, Baltazar Garzón, conducts a number of proceedings on various European instances," he explained.
And "when Britain grants this free pass tomorrow, in my opinion, that's done."
And if Swedes interrogated Mr Assange at Ecuador's embassy in London or via Skype, as Swedish jurisprudence allowed and made in other cases, the problem would be settled.
Correa took the opportunity to confirm himself as defenders of press freedom and said that what he does not tolerate is "the mediocrity, the evil intention and the lie that invalidates free speech."
"The greatest enemies of press freedom are not the evil and perverse politicians, but bad journalists who strive and extort profit," he said.
In this sense, he congratulated that Ecuador is no longer dominated by such journalists "and also not by the bankers or hegemonic and bourgeois countries," and anticipated that in a re-election he "will deepen the revolution in order to continue the path in this right direction."
Correa also backed the decision to maintain the veto against Paraguay in Unasur at least until his next elections, arguing that the organism "must remain tough and not be allowed to grant opportunism and non-disguised coup" because "the legitimacy of democracy in Paraguay has been destroyed."
Ecuador's president also considers the desire of his Colombian counterpart, Juan Manuel Santos, now and after the ruling of the International Court of Justice of The Hague, which predicted the maritime sovereignty of Nicaragua to negotiate the maritime borders between the two countries, for "absolutely justified."
At present, this judgment is not implemented.
It is a problem between a Southern and a Central American state.
Conflicts are unavoidable, but they must be overcome in the pursuit of a common path.
They must be treated comprehensively so that you can overcome them and look forward.
Likewise, he relied on a good conclusion of disputes over the maritime borders between Peru and Chile before the same Courtury and said that "it is right for Latin America to invoke international instances if both countries pledge to accept the verdict, even if it turns out to be very harsh."
Regarding the possibility that he will stand as a candidate for the next presidential election in Ecuador in order to achieve his third mandate in a row, he stated that he sees this possibility "with a lot of optimism and joy, even though it is sometimes very difficult."
Correa assured that if he lost the February 2013 elections, he would withdraw from political life.
Power has never interested me personally, but under such unjusticated circumstances as in Ecuador this socio-economic poverty can only be corrected by political power.
"My political movement had the belief that it was I who guaranteed this likely victory, that's whhere I have to comply with that responsibility," he said.
If I were to win, it would be my last period as president, after which I retire from public life.
This also applies if we lose.
"That's decided," he explained.
Correa also referred to the treatment of the health problems of the President of Venezuela, Hugo Chávez, in Cuba.
I've just spoken to the vice-president of Venezuela, Nicolás Maduro. He's told me he'd traveled there for planned, routine treatment, and that he'd be expected to be able to travel back to Cuba after the election campaign.
"In no case does it mean a relapse in President Chávez's state of health," he commented.
Ecuadorian head of state took part today in Lima at the 6th Summit of Heads of State and Government of South American Nations (Unasuria), which ended with the call for higher regional integration to ensure sustainable progress, equality and security.
The reason for dying because of AIDS today is too late detection
Fabrizio was 21 years old when he was confirmed the result of the investigation: HIV-positive.
"It was as if a bomb struck," he says of the moment he received the diagnosis, which the doctor "tried to attenuate" without discernible success.
He hid it from his family.
He decided to take care of his illness alone and began to inform himself about it; and this with such commitment that he had just celebrated his 43rd birthday.
He is undoubtedly one of the oldest patients in the HIV unit of the Hospital Civil de Guadalajara (HCG), where he came after several fights in 1994.
Fabrizio has lived with the human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) for 22 years, which was difficult to imagine in the early 1990s, when there were many doubts, few treatment options and more stigmas.
At that time, the director of an IMSS clinic avoided saying goodbye "because it led to an impasse."
At the time AIDS was synonymous with death.
Today it is possible to survive this syndrome with quality of life.
However, many people still come to the clinic, who know nothing about their disease and in which the virus has already caused damage and has exhausted their immune systems, and who are victims of opportunistic infections.
31 years after the appearance of AIDS in the world, i.e. after the first reported cases appeared, "the great success today means that the survival of a patient who absorbs the treatment in time and the survival of the general population are exactly the same," explained Jaime Andrade Villanueva, the head of the HCG's HIV unit, whereby he said that this information had been backeded in April this year by a respected scientific publication.
Andrade Villanueva, an infectious disease and HIV/AIDS expert, commented that the scientists concluded since 2008 that although AIDS is not a death sentence, the years of survival and quality of life would depend on the degree of impairment of people’s immune systems at the onset of treatment, and that those who aren’t addicted to drugs would have greater odds.
Simply put, the above explanations mean that anyone who receives HIV-positive diagnosis at the age of 25 can, in this sense, "unequivocally turn 75 years as long as he is under control," explained this professional.
In order to correctly classify this progress, it must be remembered that the life expectancy of the Mexicans today is an average of 76 years.
Even if mortality has decreased significantly in recent years (in Mexico the number of people dying from AIDS has decreased from 6,678 cases in 2007 to 4,862 in 2011 (annual report of ONUSIDA)), it is nevertheless true that 60 percent of patients enrolled in the national database have died since the appearance of AIDS.
In Jalisco alone, 255 people died in 2011, and by the month of May this year there are already 187 deaths; however, it is assured that there has been universal access to antiretroviral medicines since 2005.
- So why are people still dying from it?
- In my opinion, the problem is not access to treatment.
This is my opinion, and that's in our hospital.
At least in the past 12 years, we haven’t had any suffercency problems with the drugs. The problem is that the individuals come in far advanced condition because they don’t know their state of infection, i.e., in very late stages of the disease.
“Nine out of ten patients come to us if they already have an accompanying infection. In order to influence global mortality, earlier diagnoses are required, which is why all people in whom this is required must be offered massive detection tests.”
The experts and officials of the Consejo Estatal de Prevención del Sida en Jalisco (Coesida) (state committee on AIDS prevention in Jalisco) agree with this proposal and also the patients such as Fabrizio, who had his test carried out at a private lab, for the only reasure because it had been a friend of his who, despite his young age
Everything's different if you know you've got AIDS.
Some think they're going to die and don't want to hear anything.
"If I already have to die, they'd rather burst three times," one says in my case.
The change has been towards improvement, I eat well, do sports and take the medication.
Today his parents only know that he had cancer.
I'm living a normal life, just like anyone else.
"I work a lot, do a lot, have an active but responsible sex life, I pay attention to my health and that of the other person," enumerates Fabrizio, who agreed to share his intimate details with MILENIO JALISCO in order to animate with his testimony to those who are scared today as part of the worldwide AIDS day.
They should take the test if they belong to the at-risk group. The sooner they know if they are HIV-positive, the better, and if they already know the diagnosis, they are supposed to learn how to live responsibly like all people.
His news is a summary of the fight against AIDS in 2012.
Condoms behind the counter
There are large holes between the health programs and the ordinary citizen, explains Ricardo Salazar, journalist from Guadalajara, who has taken care of the cause of HIV.
And the most important thing is prevention.
In the designated places "the distribution of condoms has actually been increased; in the past they were handed over individually, now they give us parcels with a hundred pieces, which is already good, but there are still people who do not have access to a condom," he said.
Those at risk for new infections include adolescents.
"Why do you need them?" is a common mocking question from social workers, orientation staff in schools, employees in pharmacies and in the health care system who don't want to expose themselves to adolescents, the correspondent assured.
He proposed reorienting this inefficient distribution; the condoms are supposed to be located not only behind the counter, but in dispensing devices in public toilets of facilities frequently visited by adolescents.
Promiscuity should not be promoted.
It's not supposed to pay them the beer and motel, as Gov. Emilio González replied when asked if preservatives would be distributed under his administration.
"Sexuality doesn't look like this; it's best to bring the condoms closer to those who are already engaging in a sexual activity," he put it in a nutshell.
Jalisco's key data
There are 13,435 cumulative cases (12,158 aids and 1,317 HIV).
The state ranks 4th nationally in place of new and cumulative AIDS cases, and 13th in relation to HIV.
92% of contagion cases occur via sexual intercourse, 6% via blood and 2% perinatal.
It is estimated that 50,000 people live with HIV because there are 4 or 5 people for each registered case who do not know it.
After the United States Court of Appeal upheld a verdict that does not recognize the restructuring of Vitro's liabilities reached through a trade bankruptcy in Mexico, an ominous precedent will be set for every national company with branches in the neighbouring country that has payment problems.
It could therefore be said that the procedures for the survival of the companies that allow Mexican laws are not valid in the land of stars and stripes in contradiction to international agreements.
In practice, June 15's recognition of the verdict by Judge Harlin Hale at court in Bancarrotas for the Northern Districture in Texas causes Mexican companies to be helplessly exposed to confiscating their property beyond the Bravo River.
However, the order opens the door to Mexico's largest glass maker to invoke the U.S. Supreme Court because of three inconsistencies.
While the judge of the case indicates that creditors must be subject to bankruptcy law of the United States, Fifth Circuit’s Court of Appeal, based in New Orleans, initially says that the maining proceedings are the trade bankruptcy dealt with in Mexico.
The first point would mean that international co-operation is not well known in procedures for the insolvency of cross-border companies.
In fact, for this purpose, the United Nations model law for the unification of international trade law was created, with the American Law Institute being employed as conciliators.
Secondly, the judgment stipulates that without the votes of the companies after recognition of the liabilities in the bankruptcy mass which the Vitro branches had with their parent company, it would not have been possible to achieve the necessary majority for the restructuring of liabilities.
However, this possibility is recognized by Mexican laws.
In fact, Vitro isn't the first case in which this scheme has been approved.
There are half a dozen examples, including Agremex and Comercial Mexicana, whose liabilities have been confirmed among companies of the group by the Instituto Federal de Concursos Mercantiles.
It is also true that, after deducting the votes of the branches, Vitro's creditors, the creditors who suected Vitro in the courts of the United States, i.e. the vulture funds such as Aurelios Capital, Aurelios Convergencia, Elliot Internacional and Liverpool Limited, would not have achieved a majority.
Voting would be 45 per cent against 37 per cent.
This is not taken into account by the Court of Appeal.
On the other hand, Vitro is to blame for its difficult situation starting in 2008 as part of the great economic crisis in the United States, which gave the land blows.
For this reason, the González Sada family lodges an objection before the same court of appeal so that the vote should take place in plenary, i.e. by all the judges, because only three of them voted.
If this is not granted, the Cerciorare appeal will be lodged calling for the review of the proceedings by a higher authority, in this case by the United States Supreme Court.
The serious thing about this case is that the court set aside a document sent by the Government of Mexico under Figura amicus curiae (Friend of the Court), in which the procedure carried out by Vitro is described under the Act on Commercial Bankruptcies, indicating that it had strictly adhered to the agreements signed by both countries in order to comply with Chapter 15 of the United States bankruptcy law.
It should also be noted that the country has submitted itself to the principles of the United Nations Commission for international trading, i.e. the established rules for cases of cross-border insolvency, which guarantee the equality of debtors and creditors.
Two-band caramboles: a blow for Vitro and for the country.
Valid balance sheet
After the advertisements by the Mexican air transport unions against former owner Gastón Azcárraga Andrade, accused of a fraudulent administration, failed to advance for several months, the Asociación Sindical de Pilotos Aviadores (Trade union association of pilots) has finally made progress.
The application led by Carlos Díaz Chávez Morineau has now filed criminal proceedings against the National Banking and Securities Commission, which is accused of obstruction of the judiciary.
Thereafter, the supervisory authority has systematically refused to inform the head office of the Republic about a transaction carried out by the entrepreneur in order to withdraw 198 million pesos from the trustee account F/589 of Bank IXE in the name Mexicana de Aviación.
These funds were used for the acquisition of shares of Administradora Profesional de Hoteles (the professional hotel administration).
As you know, Azcárraga Andrade is the main shareholder of Posadas hotel chain.
Dragon Mart's encircled
At a weekend meeting at a forum of the University of the Caribbean, a group of national and foreign environmentalists, academists, entrepreneurs and general members of civic society decided to create a broad front to resist the opening of China's Dragon Mart in Cancún.
As you know, we’re talking about a huge sales and distribution center in Mexico, Central America and the Caribbean with products from the land beyond the wall, supplemented by a living area for the employees of 150 companies.
In the run-up, Canacintra (the national chamber of the manufacturing industry) has reached out to unite the governors in the south-east to oppose the monumental facility, which partially destroyed protected areas and is the mother of all threats to the industry.
ACTA's dead
Because the government of a request from the Senate to explain under what conditions and circumstances the Mexican ambassador in Japan signed the anti-counterfeiting trade agreement, which is known under the English abbreviation ACTA, the matter has already been archived, according to the Mexican Institute of Intellectual Property.
As you know, these measures took place, even though the Senate had disregarded the possibility because it regarded it as a violation of freedom of expression on social networks.
Homex long-term
In pursuit of paying long-term liabilities without impairing short-term liabilities, the construction company Homex puts exchange certificates over 500 million pesos on the market.
The issuance is the first of four equal issuances that promise an interest payment every 28 days.
Foundation of Competival
Formed from NYCE, e-Quálity and Kernet, a leading information technology company, was established a group of companies called Competival, whose goal is the service market of software clusters in Central and South America.
The corresponding investments amount to over 1.5 billion dollars.
Héctor "Hetin" Reyes: "Basketball was my life"
The globetrotter of basketball, Héctor "Hetin" Reyes, has been connected to basket sports for more than 60 years and has traveled all over the world.
Few in Puerto Rico possess such a large intellectual collection of the country's history as Héctor "Hetin" Reyes.
Before being tied to the wheelchair by a stroke in 2008, Rexes was part of this sport for 6 years, whether as a lower-class player, player of the first national league, plenipotentiary and leader in the BSN with the Vaqueros de Bayamón or president of the basketball federation.
"All my life I put on a lot of hats in basketball, even several at the same time when I was president of the BSN, chief executive of the national team and president of the federation in the 90s," Reyes recalled during a visit from Primera Hora to his home in Bayamón, where he lives with Isabel, his faithful wife for more than 50 years.
"Basketball was my life."
Reyes doesn't exaggerate with that statement.
The walls of his house are almost all decorated with photos and memories of his long career and testify to them.
Bayamón in the heart
The most emotional memories are those reminiscent of his time at the Vaqueros from the mid-50s as a player until 1982, where he celebrated his 15-year anniversary as co-managing director or managing director of the club.
"These were my best years I enjoyed the most, because I was allowed to be part of eight championships of the Vaqueros since 1967 as managing director, co-managing director or coach.
It has been many fulfilling years, including the five successive championships from 1971 to 1975.
And then I farewelled with a championship in 1981, Jerome Mincy's year debut at BSN.
From then on "Cuco" Ortiz, who was a very good steward," says Reyes.
I know yet that Gene Bartow, who worked here as a coach and was employed at the University of Alabama (Birmingham), said to me: "I have a very vigorous and 2 m tall player.
Do you want him?”
And that was the beginning of Mincy, one of the best players Puerto Rico ever had.
Bartow then recommended the free throw specialist Raymond Gausse, who was naturalized and was one of our best throwers.
I remember how he said that if Mincy had allowed a championship for Bayamon, Gausse would provide for another.
He was granted the championship of Vaqueros with Gausse, but already from a distance, because he was already at the top point of the association in 1988.
At that time, he preferred to enjoy His embarrassment and that of Mincy in the national team.
I remember when we first won against the U.S. at the pre-Olympic tournament in Mexico.
After that came the 1990 World Cup, in which we finished fourth, which should have been bronze if it hadn't been this Canadian referee who let us repeat the last turn for the second time, Reyes said.
Was this 1990 World Cup group the best national team you've ever seen?
She is one of the best, as well as those who won against the Dream Team at the 2004 Olympics.
My favorite, however, is the team of Pan-American Games 1991 in Cuba, where we won gold and offered a fight to the team of the USA, like when they won bronze against us at the World Cup.
Not only Mincy, Gausse, Ramon Rivas, Fico López and "Piculín" (Ortiz) were involved again, but also the young players (Javier) "Toñito" Colón and James Carter, the brothers León (Francisco and Edgar) and Mario "Quijote" Morales, who could not be there in 1990 because of a knee injury.
A team that was perhaps not the best in terms of personalities, but with which we won a gold medal and which gave us great pleasure, was that of the pre-Olympic tournament 1995 in Neuquén, Argentina.
With players like "Canito" Nieves, Pablo Alicea and the young Rolando Hourruitiner as a replacement for the suspended players because of the incidents at the Pan-American Games in Mar del Plata, we won the gold medal contrary to every forecast.
Who was Puerto Rico's best player?
That was undoubtedly Piculín Ortiz.
His figures at the level of the international tournaments are impressive.
No one in Puerto Rico has dominated at this level like Piculín.
And this without taking into account his career in the various leagues in which he played.
Who was the best coach from Puerto Rico?
That's a tricky question.
We've had a number of good coaches like Julio Toro, Flor Meléndez, Carlos Morales, Raymond Dalmau, Armandito Torres.
Of the boys, I like the work of Leo Arill.
What do you think is the best thing you’ve achieved in association?
I was part of the most successful era of the national team from 1988 to 19995, which included the BSN up to 17 teams in one season at the beginning of the 1990s.
What else do you have to do?
There are things I would have introduced, such as regionalization of the lower categories.
For example, the boys in Ponce only play in their territory and only meet teams from other corners of the island in the national playoffs.
Today, the children make unnecessarily many journeys and games.
At least I see the result of the prescribed trainings and courses for coaches, table officials and referees.
That's what I'm proud of.
So what are you doing today?
I like to listen to music, watch videos of music of my time on YouTube, enjoy my grandkids and from time to time I go to a basketball game.
And, of course, I'm enjoying the company of my wife Isabel, who has always been by my side.
Larry Hagman's dead
Larry Hagman was born on September 21, 1931 in Forth Worth (Texas) and became famous worldwide with his role as John Ross Ewing, better known as "J.R." in the television series "Dallas," where he played an unscrupulous, insidious and manipulating businessman.
Larry Hagman, whose role as oil magnate J.R. Ewing in the television series "Dallas" became a symbol of greed of the 80s, has died.
He turned 81 years old.
Hagman, who came back this year as J.R. in a new season of "Dallas," died Friday afternoon due to cancer complications, according to the family's press release on The Associated Press by Warner Bros., producer of "Dallas."
"Larry was back in his beloved birthplace Dallas, where he once again played the iconic role he liked the most," the family said.
Larry's family and his closest friends were with him in Dallas for the celebrations of Thanksgiving.
Linda Gray, who played his wife's role in the original and follow-up series, was with Hagman when he died in the Dallas hospital, her publicist Jeffrey Lane said.
He gave joy to everyone who knew him.
He was creative, generous, funny, witty, loving and talented, and I'll miss him a lot.
"He was an original person and lived life to the fullest," Gray said in a statement.
Hagman was diagnosed with cirrhosi in 1992, and he admitted that he drank a lot for years.
In 1995, a malignant tumor was detected in his liver, and he underlowed transplantation.
In the years before "Dallas," Hagman became known on television as a nice guy from 1965 to 19017 with NBC's sitcom "Bezauberde Jeannie."
There, he played astronaut Tony Nelson, whose life is turned upside down when he meets an attractive "jinn" (played by Barbara Eden), who will live at his home.
He was also actor of two comedies that aired only for a short time, such as The Good Life (NBC, 1971-72) and "Here We Go Again" (ABC, 1973).
His theatrically featured well-reviewed roles in "The Group," "Harry and Tonto" and "Primary Colors."
But it was his masterful interpretation of enchantingly despicable J.R. that made Hagman a superstar.
The dramatic series of the television chain CBS about the Ewing clan and other figures in their environment was broadcast from April 1978 to May 1991.
The advertising slogan "Who shot at J.R.?", the expectation for an episode full of emotion, where the figure of Hagmann was almost killed, led to international speculation, and millions of dollars were wagered in betting houses.
Thus, the series reached an audience record at that time.
When the response became known in a chapter in November 1990, it was seen by an average of 41 million viewers who turned "Dallas" into the second most-seen entertainment program since the end of "MASH" in 1983, which had 50 million viewers.
It was Kristin, the sister-in-law of J.R. (interpreted by Mary Crosby) who had scored.
J.R. had impregnated her and threatened to portray her as a prostitute if she didn't leave the city. But others also had reasons to want him dead.
Hagman interpreted Ewing's role as a corrupt, insatiable man with a charismatic smile: a devious businessman and unfaithful husband who aspired to instruct his alcohol-sick wife Sue Ellen (Linda Gray).
"I know what is supposed to stand on J.R.'s tombstone," Hagman said in 1988.
It should be called "Here rests the honorable citizen J.R. Ewing".
This is the only business he didn't win.
Victoria Principal, co-actor of the original series, recalled Hagman as an "enormous personality on and off the screen" on Friday.
It is unforgettable and irreplaceable for millions of admirers in the world and is preserved in our hearts for us who have been fortunate enough to know and love it.
Ten episodes of the new edition of "Dallas" aired a few months ago with great success on TV channel TNT.
The recording of five chapters for the second season was completed, and a sixth chapter has just been filmed, the broadcaster informed.
There was no immediately explanation from Warner, or TNT about how Hagmann's loss in the series would be treated.
Born in Fort Worth, Texas, he was the son of actress and singer Mary Martin, who played classic works such as "South Pacific" and "Peter Pan."
Martin was still a teenager when she got Larry during her marriage to lawyer Ben Hagman.
He tried his luck on the theatre stages in New York in the early 1950s, after which he served in the air forces in England from 1952 to 1956.
There he met the young designer Maj Axelsson and married her.
The couple had two children, Preston and Heidi, and they lived for a long time in the California city of Malibu, where many celebrities reside.
In 2001, he titled "Hello Darlin: Tall (and Absolutely True) Tales about My Life."
"I didn't write anything that I believed could hurt someone or impair him in any way," the Associated Press said at the time.
After liver transplantation, he campaigned for liver donations and worked as a volunteer at a hospital to help anxious patients.
"I advise them, give them courage, I meet with them when their surgeries are imminent and afterwards," he declared in 1996.
I'd like to give them some consolation, such as "don't worry, it's a bit inconvenient the first time, but you're going to be fine".
He also campaigned against smoking and took part in several campaigns.
Beginning of course studying the "end of the world"
Every week, students explore apocalyptic issues such as nuclear war, zombies, virus and germs and global warming.
Every semphere, when the religious professor decided to hold a course on the end of the world, he knew that he had an irresistible fishing hook: the end of the "long reverse counting" of the Mayan calendar on December 21, which many people believed meant the end of the world.
But Charmé had no idea what to expect in the following two months: the destructive Hurricane Sandy, whom some have referred to as "Deudarmagedon," as well as a growing conflict involving Israel, the place Christian theorists believe the apocalypse begins.
"I didn't realize that this is going to be the most apocalyptic sterm it's ever been," Charmé told students at Rutgers-Camden University (New Jersey) this week.
If someone analyzes what has happened in the world today, as if we were thirty days away, it would be a really good period.
And he recalls that the bad is good for those with apocalyptic mentality.
And he's not the only profession who offers courses about the "end of the world" this semester, which should actually be the last in history.
In Temple, fellow Professor Barry Vacker gives the course "Media, Culture and the End of the World."
Every week, students explore apocalyptic issues such as nuclear war, zombies, virus and germs and global warming.
"We're analysing why these ideas spread over time," he said, as well as the way hypothetical scenarios are offered that lead to certain human behavior.
If nuclear material gets into the hands of terrorists, for example, a war could break out.
This month, the students analyzed films with apocalyptic content and looked at how they could be compared with real life.
"I've tried to inform the students about what's possible, likely, believable and impossible," Vacker said.
On the main campus of the University of Pennsylvania, the professor of Latin American history Matthew Restall and his colleague Amara Solari, an associate professor of art history and anthropology, have formed a team to offer a course with the simple designation "The end of the world."
"We're not picking up in 2012 so we always keep the option of being able to re-deter the course in case the world doesn't go down," Restall said.
Despite the "imminent demise", the students must study, create projects and participate in final exams.
In Penn State, the final exam is conducted on the eve of the apocalypse, which is why students have no chance but "continue working until the night where the world is reportedly coming to an end," Restall said.
Classes were very popular.
"In two hours there were no more places," Restall said of his high-avert-average course course, which filled with 35 students.
For weeks prior to the start of the semester, we received e-mails from students who asked if there were still places available.
For their part, the students assure that the course is one of the most interesting.
"It's intriguing to see what people do to comfort themselves," said Bridgid Robinson, a 23-year-old student in Rutgers-Camden for religion and sociology from Haddonfield, New Jersey.
The apocalyptic sect or religious mentality is actually only a matter of comfort or its absence.
25-year-old student Will Wekesa for psychology and nursing explains to have seen all the apocalyptic films.
"Never have I heard anything from a class where this is taught," he said.
I'll enjoy it.
But none of the students surveyed - and less yet a professor - declared to believe in the last date of December 21.
"Our first project dealt with the prophecy of the Mayans, and in certain ways we discredite it," Julie Zeglen, a student last year in Temple, 21 years old and from West Chester.
The Mayans never predicted the end of the world; it's just a key point in the calendar, Restall said.
He notes, however, that there is an apocalyptic fear in Western society that dates back several centuries when people reacted to changes in their environment by predicting the end of the world.
The internet has increased these speculations.
"Elsewhere people don't think so," he says.
This is mainly the case in the English-speaking world.
Joseph Dougherty, a religious professor at the University of La Salle who teaches in the Philippines this year, quickly responds to the question of whether he would know a course on the "end of the world" there.
"The Philippines do not participate in the end of the world," he wrote as a hint of an exemption from a higher authority.
We've got a pardon from the pope.
Restall pointed out that many days have been spoken by the last court over the years, and said that if nothing happens on December 21, "people immediately think of a new date" or philosophized that December 21 was the beginning of a seven-year period after which the end of the world came.
Students and professors take this date easily.
Several said they would like to go to celebrations about "the end of the world."
"Maybe I'll call a few friends so we can laugh together," said Samira Ford, a 20-year-old student for communications.
